-- SAT ScoreBoost -- bulk import of the practice question bank into public.questions
-- Generated by supabase/generate_import.js -- do not hand-edit, regenerate instead.
-- Run in the Supabase SQL editor AFTER schema.sql (requires write access, i.e. run as yourself in the dashboard).
-- Part 4 of 10 (842 questions). Run every part.

-- Allow difficulty 4 (Extra Hard); harmless to repeat in every part.
alter table public.questions drop constraint if exists questions_difficulty_check;
alter table public.questions add constraint questions_difficulty_check check (difficulty between 1 and 4);

insert into public.questions
  (id, module, domain, skill, difficulty, passage, prompt, choices, answer, explanation, choice_explanations)
values
  ($q$cs-h269$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many art historians regard the Renaissance as a sharp break from the Middle Ages, when artists rediscovered the realism and proportions of ancient Greek and Roman art after centuries of neglect.<br><br>Text 2<br>Art historian Caterina Bellini argues that the break was less sharp than often claimed. Medieval artists, she notes, studied and copied ancient works throughout the period, and some fourteenth-century painters were already experimenting with depth and naturalistic detail. The Renaissance, Bellini contends, intensified trends that had been developing for generations.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Bellini (Text 2) most likely respond to the art historians' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Bellini would argue that medieval artists never studied or copied ancient Greek and Roman art.","Bellini would agree that the Renaissance marked a complete break with the art of the Middle Ages.","Bellini would argue that fourteenth-century painters rejected naturalistic detail in their work.","Bellini would argue that the historians exaggerate how abruptly the Renaissance departed from earlier art."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Bellini says the break was "less sharp than often claimed" and that the Renaissance "intensified trends" already underway.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h270$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many researchers credit the decline in smoking in the United States since the 1960s mainly to public health campaigns that informed people about the dangers of tobacco.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Rafael Duarte argues that rising cigarette taxes played at least as large a role. His analysis of state data found that smoking rates fell fastest in states that raised taxes most sharply, even after accounting for differences in public health campaigns. Young people, who have less money to spend, were especially responsive to higher prices.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of the two texts explain the decline in smoking?$q$, $q$["Text 1 focuses on the decline of smoking among young people, while Text 2 focuses on older adults.","Text 1 argues that smoking rates have increased since the 1960s, while Text 2 argues that they have declined.","Text 1 emphasizes the role of information about health risks, while Text 2 emphasizes the role of price.","Text 1 argues that taxes reduced smoking, while Text 2 argues that public health campaigns had no effect."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Text 1 credits public health campaigns (information), while Duarte emphasizes cigarette taxes (price).$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h271$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some scientists argue that the extinction of many large animals in the Americas around 13,000 years ago—mammoths, giant ground sloths, and saber-toothed cats among them—was caused mainly by human hunters, who arrived in the region around the same time.<br><br>Text 2<br>Paleontologist Grace Hollis notes that the extinctions also coincided with rapid climate change at the end of the last ice age, which transformed the grasslands many large animals depended on. Hollis argues that hunting and climate change likely worked together: animals already stressed by shrinking habitats may have been unable to withstand additional pressure from human hunters.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Hollis (Text 2) most likely respond to the scientists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Hollis would argue that human hunters played no role in the extinction of large animals in the Americas.","Hollis would argue that the scientists' explanation is incomplete because it does not account for the role of climate change.","Hollis would argue that climate change alone caused the extinctions, since the large animals died out before hunters could have had any significant effect.","Hollis would argue that the extinctions occurred long before humans arrived in the Americas."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Hollis argues hunting and climate change "likely worked together," adding a factor that the hunting-focused explanation omits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h272$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many teachers believe that students should be allowed to use calculators freely in math classes. Calculators, they argue, free students from tedious arithmetic and allow them to focus on understanding concepts and solving complex problems.<br><br>Text 2<br>Math educator Rosa Delgado agrees that calculators can be valuable tools. But she cautions that students who rely on them too early may never develop number sense—the intuitive understanding of how numbers relate—that helps them notice when an answer is unreasonable. Delgado recommends that students master basic arithmetic before using calculators routinely.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Delgado (Text 2) most likely respond to the teachers' position in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Delgado would accept the benefits the teachers describe but argue that the timing of calculator use matters.","Delgado would argue that calculators should never be used in any math class.","Delgado would agree that students should use calculators freely from the very beginning of their schooling.","Delgado would argue that number sense is unimportant for solving complex problems."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Delgado "agrees that calculators can be valuable tools" but recommends mastering arithmetic first—so she objects to early, unrestricted use.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h273$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Literary critic Alan Hughes argues that a novel's meaning is determined by its author's intentions. To understand a work correctly, he contends, readers should seek out what the author meant to convey, using letters, interviews, and other biographical evidence.<br><br>Text 2<br>Critic Yuna Park argues that once a novel is published, its meaning is no longer controlled by its author. Readers from different times and places, she notes, find meanings in classic works that their authors could not have foreseen, and these readings can be just as valid as the author's own.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how Hughes (Text 1) and Park (Text 2) view the meaning of a novel?$q$, $q$["Hughes argues that biographical evidence is unreliable, while Park argues that readers should rely on it.","Hughes values letters and interviews as evidence of meaning, while Park argues that biographical evidence is useful only for understanding contemporary novels.","Hughes argues that authors rarely have clear intentions, while Park argues that they always do.","Hughes locates a work's meaning in the author's intentions, while Park believes readers can validly find meanings the author did not intend."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Hughes ties meaning to authorial intention; Park argues that readers' unforeseen interpretations can be equally valid.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h274$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Supporters of daylight saving time argue that shifting clocks forward in spring saves energy. With more daylight in the evening, they contend, people use less electricity for lighting.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Laura Bennett studied electricity use in a U.S. state that adopted daylight saving time statewide in 2006. She found that household electricity use actually rose slightly, because savings on lighting were outweighed by increased use of air conditioning in the evening and heating in the morning.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Bennett (Text 2) most likely respond to the supporters' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Bennett's findings support the claim in Text 1 by showing that daylight saving time reduces electricity use.","Bennett's findings show that daylight saving time has no effect on how people use lighting in the evening.","Bennett's findings challenge the claim in Text 1 by showing that other energy uses offset the savings on lighting.","Bennett's findings show that people use more lighting in the evening during daylight saving time."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Bennett found that lighting savings were outweighed by higher heating and cooling use, so electricity use rose—contradicting the energy-saving claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h275$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Anthropologist Peter Lang argues that early farming communities were healthier than the hunter-gatherer groups that preceded them. Farming, he contends, provided a more reliable food supply, reducing the risk of starvation.<br><br>Text 2<br>Anthropologist Amina Yusuf points to skeletons from early farming villages, which often show signs of poorer health than those of earlier hunter-gatherers: shorter stature, more tooth decay, and evidence of nutritional deficiencies. Diets based on a few crops, she argues, lacked the variety of foraged foods, and crowded villages helped diseases spread.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Yusuf (Text 2) most likely respond to Lang's argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Yusuf would agree that early farmers were healthier than hunter-gatherers because of their reliable food supply.","Yusuf would argue that skeletal evidence contradicts Lang's claim about the health of early farmers.","Yusuf would argue that hunter-gatherers were more likely to experience tooth decay than early farmers.","Yusuf would argue that early farming villages were less crowded than hunter-gatherer camps."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Yusuf cites skeletons showing poorer health among early farmers, contradicting Lang's claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h276$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many critics consider the composer's late string quartets her finest work, praising their emotional depth and structural complexity. Written after she lost most of her hearing, they argue, the quartets reveal an inner world freed from outside influence.<br><br>Text 2<br>Musicologist Anton Becker agrees that the late quartets are extraordinary. But he notes that the composer's letters from the period show her intensely engaged with the music of younger composers, which friends played for her on the piano so she could feel the vibrations. The quartets, Becker argues, reflect dialogue with her contemporaries, not isolation from them.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Becker (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Becker would share the critics' high opinion of the quartets but dispute their claim about the composer's isolation from outside influence.","Becker would argue that the late quartets reflect the influence of the composer's hearing loss more than the critics recognize, making them less original.","Becker would agree that the quartets were written in complete isolation from other music.","Becker would argue that the composer did not lose her hearing late in her life."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Becker "agrees that the late quartets are extraordinary" but argues they reflect dialogue with contemporaries, not freedom from outside influence.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h277$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some ocean scientists propose fertilizing parts of the ocean with iron to fight climate change. Iron, they note, stimulates the growth of plankton, which absorb carbon dioxide; when the plankton die, some sink to the deep ocean, carrying carbon with them.<br><br>Text 2<br>Oceanographer Helen Ruiz notes that field experiments have produced disappointing results. Although iron triggered plankton blooms, most of the carbon they absorbed was released back into the water when the plankton were eaten or decomposed near the surface, and only a small fraction sank. The blooms also risked depleting oxygen and disrupting food webs.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ruiz (Text 2) most likely respond to the proposal in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Ruiz would argue that iron fertilization fails because iron does not stimulate plankton growth in the open ocean, though it may do so in coastal waters.","Ruiz would agree that iron fertilization has been proven to remove large amounts of carbon.","Ruiz would argue that plankton never absorb carbon dioxide from the water.","Ruiz would argue that field experiments suggest the proposal is less effective than its advocates expect and carries risks."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Ruiz reports that little carbon sank and that blooms carried ecological risks, undermining the proposal's promise.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h278$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some historians portray the Vikings primarily as raiders who terrorized the coasts of Europe, plundering monasteries and towns from the late eighth century onward.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Ingrid Larsen emphasizes that raiding was only one part of Viking activity. Vikings also established extensive trade networks stretching from the Arctic to Baghdad, founded towns such as Dublin, and settled as farmers in places including Iceland and Greenland. Focusing on raids, she argues, obscures the economic and cultural roles that Vikings played.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Larsen (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' portrayal in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Larsen would argue that the Vikings never raided monasteries or towns in Europe.","Larsen would agree that the Vikings were primarily raiders but argue that their raids were less destructive than the historians suggest.","Larsen would argue that the historians' portrayal is too narrow because it overlooks the Vikings' other activities.","Larsen would argue that Viking trade networks did not extend beyond Scandinavia."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Larsen says raiding was "only one part" of Viking activity and that focusing on it obscures trade, town-founding, and settlement.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h279$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Ruth Feldman argues that happiness is largely determined by genetics. Studies of identical twins raised apart, she notes, show that their levels of happiness are strikingly similar, suggesting that each person has a relatively fixed "set point."<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Diego Morales agrees that genes influence happiness. But his long-term studies show that major life events—such as long-term unemployment or disability—can shift people's happiness levels for years, and that some people never return to their former baseline. The set point, Morales argues, is less fixed than once believed.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Feldman (Text 1) and Morales (Text 2) would most likely agree with which statement?$q$, $q$["Both would agree that happiness is completely fixed and cannot change over a lifetime.","Both would agree that genetics has some influence on how happy people are.","Both would agree that major life events have no lasting effect on happiness.","Both would agree that identical twins raised apart have very different levels of happiness."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Feldman emphasizes genetic influence, and Morales "agrees that genes influence happiness," though he thinks the set point is more changeable.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h280$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many conservationists support creating large marine protected areas where fishing is banned. Such areas, they argue, allow fish populations to recover, and fish that grow large and abundant inside protected zones eventually spill over into surrounding waters, benefiting fishers.<br><br>Text 2<br>Fisheries scientist Kwame Boateng notes that the benefits depend heavily on enforcement. In several protected areas he studied, illegal fishing was so common that fish populations inside the boundaries were no larger than those outside. Boateng supports protected areas but argues that they require patrols and monitoring to deliver the benefits their supporters expect.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Boateng (Text 2) most likely respond to the conservationists' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Boateng would support the conservationists' goals but argue that protected areas succeed only when rules are enforced.","Boateng would argue that marine protected areas never allow fish populations to recover.","Boateng would argue that protected areas harm fishers because the fish that recover inside them rarely spill over into the surrounding waters.","Boateng would argue that fish inside protected areas never spill over into surrounding waters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Boateng "supports protected areas" but found that without enforcement they didn't help, so benefits depend on patrols and monitoring.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h281$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some historians attribute the fall of the Western Roman Empire in 476 CE mainly to invasions by Germanic peoples, whose armies overran Roman territory and eventually deposed the last emperor in the West.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Julia Marchetti argues that the invasions succeeded because the empire had already been weakened from within. Decades of civil wars, a declining tax base, and reliance on hired soldiers whose loyalty was uncertain, she contends, left the Western Empire unable to resist pressures it had withstood for centuries.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Marchetti (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["Marchetti would argue that Germanic peoples never invaded Roman territory.","Marchetti would agree that the invasions were decisive but argue that they were carried out mainly by hired soldiers who had turned against Rome.","Marchetti would argue that the Western Roman Empire was stronger in 476 CE than ever before.","Marchetti would argue that the historians' explanation overlooks internal problems that made the invasions successful."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Marchetti argues the invasions succeeded because of internal weaknesses, which the invasion-focused explanation omits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h282$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the senator had publicly opposed the bill for months, her eventual vote in its favor was less a reversal than a ______ shift: she had been quietly negotiating amendments all along, and the final version incorporated nearly all of them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["precipitous","calculated","grudging","capricious"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Her vote resulted from long negotiation, so the shift was deliberate and planned: "calculated." "Capricious" means impulsive, and "precipitous" means sudden, both of which contradict the long negotiation. "Grudging" implies reluctance, but the text suggests she got what she wanted.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h283$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Critics have called the novelist's prose ______, but her admirers argue that its spareness is deliberate: by withholding description, she forces readers to imagine the characters' surroundings for themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ornate","florid","austere","lyrical"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The admirers defend the prose's "spareness," so the critics must have described it with a word meaning severely plain: "austere." "Florid" and "ornate" mean elaborately decorated, and "lyrical" means expressive and musical. None of these describes spareness.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h284$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The museum's new director has been praised for making the collection more accessible, but some longtime staff members view her changes as ______: in their view, she has replaced careful scholarship with crowd-pleasing spectacle.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["an imposition","a restoration","a dilution","an innovation"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The staff believe scholarship has been replaced with spectacle, a weakening of quality: "a dilution." "An innovation" is neutral or positive, "a restoration" implies returning to an earlier state, and "an imposition" stresses force rather than lowered quality.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h285$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Historian Ana Lucía Ortega argues that the treaty's significance has been ______ by later scholars, who read into it a commitment to self-governance that its eighteenth-century authors likely never intended.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dismissed","inflated","obscured","documented"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Later scholars read more into the treaty than its authors intended, overstating its importance: "inflated." "Obscured" and "dismissed" suggest the significance was hidden or downplayed, the opposite. "Documented" is neutral.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h286$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 4, $q$The chef's approach to Peruvian cuisine is ______: she preserves the techniques her grandmother taught her while freely incorporating ingredients from Japan, Italy, and West Africa.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["insular","syncretic","derivative","orthodox"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Blending inherited techniques with ingredients from many cultures is "syncretic." "Orthodox" and "insular" imply strict tradition or isolation, and "derivative" implies unoriginal copying, an unfavorable judgment the text doesn't make.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h287$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Early reviews of the film were ______; some critics called it a masterpiece, while others dismissed it as self-indulgent, and few took a middle position.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["polarized","lukewarm","unanimous","tentative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reviews split sharply into opposite extremes with few in between, so they were "polarized." "Lukewarm" implies moderate reactions, "unanimous" implies agreement, and "tentative" implies hesitation, none of which fits strong opposing judgments.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h288$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In her memoir, the physicist recalls that her early interest in science was not encouraged but merely ______ by her teachers, who neither discouraged her questions nor went out of their way to answer them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["tolerated","applauded","fostered","stifled"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The teachers neither discouraged nor actively supported her, so they "tolerated" her interest. "Fostered" and "applauded" imply encouragement, which the text denies, and "stifled" implies discouragement, which it also denies.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h289$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The new evidence does not overturn the prevailing theory about the bridge's collapse, but it does ______ it: investigators now believe the corrosion that was blamed for the failure was worsened by a design flaw in the supporting cables.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["complicate","refute","corroborate","supersede"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The evidence adds a contributing factor without overturning the theory, making it more complex: "complicate." "Refute" and "supersede" mean overturning or replacing, which the text denies, and "corroborate" means simply confirming, which ignores the added factor.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h290$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Kwame Osei found that residents of the neighborhood were ______ about the proposed redevelopment: they welcomed the promised jobs and new stores but feared that rising rents would eventually force them out.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sanguine","ambivalent","indifferent","adamant"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Residents held both positive and negative feelings at once, so they were "ambivalent." "Indifferent" means not caring, "adamant" means firmly decided, and "sanguine" means optimistic, none of which captures the mix.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h291$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The composer's late works are notably ______; after decades of writing sprawling symphonies, she turned to short pieces for solo piano, some lasting less than a minute.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["verbose","elaborate","concise","fragmentary"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Short pieces after sprawling ones are "concise." "Verbose" and "elaborate" suggest length or complexity. "Fragmentary" suggests incompleteness, not brevity by design, so it misdescribes finished short pieces.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h292$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some economists warn that the city's reliance on a single industry makes its economy ______: a downturn in tourism, they note, would leave thousands of residents without work and few other employers to turn to.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["precarious","stagnant","robust","diversified"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Dependence on one industry leaves the economy insecure and at risk: "precarious." "Robust" and "diversified" contradict the warning, and "stagnant" means not growing, which isn't the concern described.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h293$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ the long-standing rivalry between the two research groups, the conference organizers deliberately scheduled their presentations back to back, hoping a direct exchange of ideas would prove productive.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["acknowledge","exacerbate","sidestep","resolve"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The organizers chose confrontation over avoidance, so the rejected option is to "sidestep" the rivalry. "Exacerbate" would mean worsening it, which is closer to what they risked doing. "Acknowledge" and "resolve" don't contrast with putting the groups together.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h294$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the archaeologists' initial excavation turned up only a few pottery shards, the team remained ______, convinced that the site's location on an ancient trade route made more significant finds likely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["undeterred","complacent","indifferent","disillusioned"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite meager results, the team kept its confidence and continued: "undeterred." "Disillusioned" means disappointed, "indifferent" means uninterested, and "complacent" implies smug inaction rather than determined persistence.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h295$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The poet's language is deceptively ______: her poems use the vocabulary of everyday speech, yet a careful reading reveals layers of allusion to classical mythology and scripture.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscure","convoluted","plain","archaic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Deceptively" signals that the surface hides complexity, so the language seems simple: "plain." "Obscure," "archaic," and "convoluted" describe language that appears difficult, which contradicts "vocabulary of everyday speech."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h296$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Researchers had expected the drug to produce a modest improvement, so they were surprised when its effects proved ______: patients' symptoms eased within hours rather than weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["gradual","negligible","immediate","sporadic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Symptoms eased within hours, far faster than expected: "immediate." "Gradual" contradicts "hours rather than weeks," "negligible" contradicts the improvement, and "sporadic" implies irregularity.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h297$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The director's adaptation is ______ to the novel in its plot, reproducing nearly every scene, yet it transforms the book's tone, turning a bleak tragedy into something closer to dark comedy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["indifferent","faithful","antithetical","peripheral"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Reproducing nearly every scene shows adherence to the source: "faithful." "Antithetical" means opposed, "indifferent" means unconcerned, and "peripheral" means marginal, none of which fits close adherence.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h298$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Hye-jin Park's study of wild bonobos challenges the assumption that their peaceful reputation is ______; she observed frequent but brief conflicts that were quickly resolved through grooming and other reconciliation behaviors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unqualified","unfounded","exaggerated","recent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Park found that bonobos do fight but reconcile quickly, so their peacefulness is real but not absolute. She challenges the assumption that it is "unqualified." Challenging the idea that the reputation is "exaggerated" or "unfounded" would mean defending the reputation in full, which her observation of frequent conflicts doesn't do. "Recent" is unrelated to her findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h299$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 4, $q$Many of the town's residents regard the annual festival as ______, an event so long established that few can imagine the summer without it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["novel","sacrosanct","ephemeral","superfluous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$An event so established that its absence is unimaginable is treated as untouchable: "sacrosanct." "Ephemeral" means short-lived, "superfluous" means unnecessary, and "novel" means new.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h300$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The committee's report was ______ in its criticism of the agency, cataloging dozens of specific failures and naming the officials responsible for each.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["perfunctory","conciliatory","oblique","unsparing"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Listing many specific failures and naming officials shows harsh thoroughness: "unsparing." "Perfunctory" means superficial, "oblique" means indirect, and "conciliatory" means seeking to make peace.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h301$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Linguist Tomás Ferreira notes that the dialect's grammar, often dismissed as sloppy, is in fact remarkably ______, following rules for verb agreement as consistent as those of the standard language.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["systematic","idiosyncratic","fluid","rudimentary"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Following consistent rules means the grammar is "systematic," which rebuts the charge of sloppiness. "Idiosyncratic" means peculiar to an individual, "rudimentary" means basic, and "fluid" implies flexibility rather than consistency.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h302$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Despite its ______ appearance, the deep-sea anglerfish is only a few centimeters long; photographs taken at close range make it look far larger and more threatening than it is.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["diminutive","drab","innocuous","fearsome"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Photographs make the fish look "larger and more threatening," so its appearance is "fearsome." "Diminutive" and "innocuous" describe the reality, not the appearance, and "drab" concerns color.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h303$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The two scientists' accounts of the discovery ______ on almost every detail, from the date of the key experiment to who first noticed the anomaly, a discrepancy that historians have never fully explained.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["elaborate","hinge","converge","diverge"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A "discrepancy" means the accounts differ, so they "diverge." "Converge" means agree, "elaborate" means add detail, and "hinge" means depend.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h304$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the playwright's dialogue often seems ______, each apparently aimless exchange later proves essential to the plot, planting details that become significant in the final act.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["terse","digressive","didactic","incisive"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Apparently aimless" exchanges seem to wander off topic: "digressive." "Incisive" and "terse" mean sharp or brief, and "didactic" means instructive, none of which fits "aimless."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h305$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The governor's proposal was ______ enough to win support from both parties: it raised the funding that one side wanted while leaving untouched the tax rates the other side was determined to protect.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["pragmatic","quixotic","doctrinaire","partisan"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A practical compromise designed to satisfy both sides is "pragmatic." "Partisan" and "doctrinaire" imply rigid loyalty to one side or ideology, and "quixotic" implies unrealistic idealism.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h306$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The botanist's field notes, written over four decades, are ______: they record not only the plants she collected but also weather, local names, the songs of nearby birds, and conversations with farmers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["encyclopedic","fragmentary","specialized","cursory"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Notes covering plants, weather, names, birds, and conversations are comprehensive: "encyclopedic." "Cursory" means hasty, "specialized" means narrowly focused, and "fragmentary" means incomplete.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h307$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some historians consider the general's retreat a ______ blunder, but others argue that it preserved his army to fight again and was therefore a shrewd decision.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["catastrophic","tactical","minor","calculated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contrast with "shrewd decision" requires a word suggesting a disastrous mistake: "catastrophic." "Calculated" implies deliberate planning, which supports the other view. "Tactical" is neutral, and "minor" understates a debate over its significance.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h308$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$In contrast to her outspoken predecessor, the new ambassador is known for her ______; she rarely gives interviews and chooses her public statements with great care.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["bravado","volubility","reticence","candor"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rarely speaking and choosing words carefully reflect "reticence." "Candor," "volubility," and "bravado" describe openness, talkativeness, or boldness, all closer to the outspoken predecessor.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h309$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The study's conclusions are ______ at best: its sample included only twenty-three participants, all of whom were recruited from a single university's psychology department.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unassailable","spurious","provisional","definitive"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A tiny, unrepresentative sample makes the conclusions tentative and open to revision: "provisional." "Definitive" and "unassailable" contradict "at best," and "spurious" (false) goes further than the text, which questions the conclusions' strength rather than calling them false.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h310$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than simply cataloging the ruins, archaeologist Leila Haddad sought to ______ the daily routines of the people who lived there, using food residues, tool wear, and the layout of rooms to infer how households spent their time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["preserve","embellish","commemorate","reconstruct"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Inferring daily routines from evidence means building a picture of the past: "reconstruct." "Commemorate" means to honor, "embellish" means to add fictional detail, and "preserve" means to protect the physical ruins, which is closer to cataloging.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h311$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The memo's language was carefully ______, allowing the company to suggest that layoffs were possible without committing to any specific plan.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["explicit","inflammatory","equivocal","emphatic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Language that suggests without committing is deliberately ambiguous: "equivocal." "Emphatic" and "explicit" mean forceful or clear, and "inflammatory" means provocative.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h312$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Mathematician Ines Calloway's proof, at just two pages, was praised for its ______; earlier attempts to prove the same result had run to more than fifty pages and relied on extensive computer calculations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["obscurity","rigor","elegance","ambition"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A short, clear proof replacing long, computation-heavy ones is praised for "elegance." "Rigor" refers to logical strictness, which the long proofs presumably also had, and "ambition" and "obscurity" don't match the contrast with length.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h313$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the hurricane caused extensive flooding, the city's newly built seawall ______ the worst of the damage, protecting the historic district that had been devastated by a similar storm decades earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["foreshadowed","exacerbated","mitigated","precipitated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The seawall reduced the severity of the damage: "mitigated." "Precipitated" and "exacerbated" mean caused or worsened, and "foreshadowed" means hinted at in advance.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h314$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Hamid's essays are ______ in the best sense: they move easily from economics to poetry to personal memory, drawing connections that a specialist in any single field might miss.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["haphazard","eclectic","pedantic","esoteric"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ranging widely across fields "in the best sense" is "eclectic." "Haphazard" implies disorder, the bad sense of breadth. "Esoteric" means understood by few, and "pedantic" means fussily focused on minor details.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h315$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The new regulations are intended to ______ the practice of overfishing rather than to eliminate it entirely; officials acknowledge that some boats will continue to exceed their quotas.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["curb","eradicate","sanction","document"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reducing a practice without eliminating it is to "curb" it. "Eradicate" means to eliminate entirely, which the text rules out. "Sanction" is ambiguous (it can mean approve), and "document" means record.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h316$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Far from being ______ observers of their environment, honeybees actively modify it: workers fan their wings to cool the hive, and they collect plant resins to seal cracks against invading microbes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["meticulous","casual","vigilant","passive"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Far from" signals the opposite of "actively modify," so the word must be "passive." "Vigilant" and "meticulous" describe attentive observation, which doesn't contrast with action, and "casual" doesn't oppose "actively."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h317$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The architect's design ______ the building's historical facade while replacing its crumbling interior entirely, so that from the street the structure looks almost exactly as it did in 1890.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["renovates","retains","replicates","obscures"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The facade is kept while the interior is replaced: "retains." "Renovates" implies changing the facade, "obscures" means hiding it, and "replicates" means making a copy, which would replace the original.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h318$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The candidate's speech was strikingly ______, avoiding the bold promises of her rivals and instead emphasizing the limits of what any single official could accomplish.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["measured","grandiose","evasive","strident"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Avoiding bold promises and emphasizing limits reflects restraint: "measured." "Grandiose" and "strident" describe the opposite. "Evasive" implies avoiding questions, but she directly addresses limits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h319$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$While most of the expedition's members wrote ______ accounts of their journey, describing heroic struggles against the elements, the ship's cook kept a diary that dwells mainly on boredom, bad food, and petty arguments.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["candid","romanticized","meticulous","mundane"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Accounts of "heroic struggles" that contrast with the cook's unglamorous diary are "romanticized." "Mundane" and "candid" describe the cook's diary, and "meticulous" doesn't capture the contrast in tone.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h320$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some scholars argue that the proverb's meaning has been ______ over centuries of use: what originally warned against greed is now commonly understood as advice about patience.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["codified","preserved","clarified","distorted"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A meaning that shifted from a warning about greed to advice about patience has been altered or twisted: "distorted." "Clarified" and "preserved" imply the meaning was improved or kept, and "codified" means formally recorded.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h321$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Rosa Maldonado describes the relationship between the fig tree and its pollinating wasp as ______: neither species can reproduce without the other.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["parasitic","opportunistic","incidental","obligate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$When neither species can reproduce without the other, the relationship is required, or "obligate." "Opportunistic" and "incidental" imply the relationship is optional, and "parasitic" implies one species harms the other.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h322$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The trade negotiations, which had seemed close to collapse in March, were ______ in April when both sides agreed to drop their most contentious demands.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["derailed","revived","stalled","concluded"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Talks near collapse that recovered after concessions were "revived." "Stalled" and "derailed" mean stopped, and "concluded" would require an agreement, not merely renewed progress.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h323$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The author's portrait of her father is notably ______: she describes his generosity and humor alongside his temper and his long absences, refusing to either idealize or condemn him.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sentimental","balanced","scathing","laudatory"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Presenting both strengths and flaws without idealizing or condemning is "balanced." "Laudatory" and "sentimental" lean toward idealizing, and "scathing" toward condemning.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h324$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The fossil record for early birds remains ______: crucial transitional forms are known from only a handful of specimens, and entire lineages are represented by single bones.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["contested","definitive","abundant","sparse"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Few specimens and single bones mean the record is thin: "sparse." "Abundant" and "definitive" contradict this, and "contested" refers to disagreement rather than scarcity.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h325$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Initially ______ about the value of video games in classrooms, the teacher changed her mind after observing how a simulation game helped struggling students grasp the basics of supply and demand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["indifferent","ignorant","skeptical","enthusiastic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$She "changed her mind" after seeing the game's benefits, so she must have doubted its value: "skeptical." "Enthusiastic" doesn't fit a change toward approval, "ignorant" implies she knew nothing, and "indifferent" implies she had no opinion to change.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h326$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The documentary is ______ in its use of archival footage, splicing clips from different decades together without labels, so that viewers may mistake a 1970s protest for one from the 1990s.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["cavalier","innovative","sparing","scrupulous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Splicing unlabeled clips so that viewers are misled shows careless disregard for accuracy: "cavalier." "Scrupulous" means the opposite, "sparing" means using little, and "innovative" is positive.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h327$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Historians once viewed the trading post as ______ to the region's economy, a minor outpost of little importance, but new analysis of shipping records shows that it handled nearly a third of the colony's fur exports.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["detrimental","marginal","indispensable","integral"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"A minor outpost of little importance" defines "marginal." "Integral" and "indispensable" describe the newer view, and "detrimental" means harmful.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h328$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The novelist ______ the conventions of the detective story: the crime is never solved, the detective is barely mentioned after the first chapter, and the culprit's identity is irrelevant to the book's concerns.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["subverts","embraces","exemplifies","codifies"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Leaving the crime unsolved and sidelining the detective undermines genre expectations: "subverts." "Embraces," "exemplifies," and "codifies" all mean following or reinforcing the conventions.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h329$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Engineers found that the old bridge's iron, far from being ______, had retained most of its original strength after 150 years, a durability they attributed to the high purity of the ore.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["malleable","degraded","pristine","reinforced"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Far from being" signals the opposite of retained strength, so the word means weakened: "degraded." "Pristine" and "reinforced" align with strength, and "malleable" refers to flexibility rather than weakening.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h330$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although she is often described as a ______ figure in the history of photography, Lena Vukovic in fact worked within a thriving circle of women photographers whose influence on her was considerable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["solitary","seminal","derivative","marginal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contrast is with working in a thriving circle that influenced her, so the common description must portray her as isolated: "solitary." "Seminal" means influential, which doesn't contrast with the circle. "Marginal" and "derivative" concern importance and originality, not isolation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h331$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the new vaccine requires only a single dose and no refrigeration, public health officials expect it to ______ immunization campaigns in remote regions, where maintaining cold storage has been a persistent obstacle.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["complicate","supplant","facilitate","postpone"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Removing the refrigeration requirement makes campaigns easier: "facilitate." "Complicate" and "postpone" mean the opposite, and "supplant" means replace.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h332$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The council's decision to delay the vote was widely seen as ______; members hoped that by the time it was held, public anger over the proposal would have faded.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unprecedented","principled","strategic","impulsive"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Delaying so that anger fades is a calculated move: "strategic." "Impulsive" means unplanned, "principled" implies a moral motive, and "unprecedented" concerns novelty, not motive.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h333$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The translator's notes ______ the many ambiguities of the original text, explaining in each case why she chose one meaning over another rather than concealing the difficulty.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["resolve","acknowledge","ignore","exaggerate"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$She explains her choices "rather than concealing the difficulty," so she openly admits the ambiguities: "acknowledge." "Resolve" would mean eliminating the ambiguities rather than discussing them, "ignore" is the opposite, and "exaggerate" adds an unsupported judgment.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h334$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Measurements from the probe were ______ with earlier predictions: the planet's magnetic field was almost exactly as strong as models had forecast.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["incompatible","tangential","contemporaneous","consistent"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Results that match predictions are "consistent with" them. "Incompatible" means the opposite, "tangential" means only loosely related, and "contemporaneous" means occurring at the same time.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h335$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The researchers were careful not to ______ their results: although the treatment reduced symptoms in mice, they stressed that it had not yet been tested in humans and might not work the same way.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["withhold","replicate","overstate","contextualize"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Stressing limitations shows care not to exaggerate: "overstate." "Replicate" means repeat, "withhold" means conceal, and "contextualize" describes what they actually did, not what they avoided.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h336$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Unlike her earlier novels, which unfold over decades, the author's latest book is ______ in scope, confined to a single afternoon in a single kitchen.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["expansive","ambiguous","episodic","circumscribed"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A book confined to one afternoon in one room has a narrowly limited scope: "circumscribed." "Expansive" describes the earlier novels, "episodic" means made up of loosely connected episodes, and "ambiguous" concerns meaning, not scope.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h337$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though the council members disagreed sharply about the budget, their debate remained ______, with each side acknowledging the other's concerns and avoiding personal attacks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["civil","inconclusive","acrimonious","perfunctory"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Acknowledging concerns and avoiding personal attacks despite sharp disagreement makes the debate "civil." "Acrimonious" contradicts this, "perfunctory" means done without effort, and "inconclusive" concerns the outcome rather than the tone.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h338$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The species' range has ______ dramatically over the past century; once found across much of the continent, the frog now survives in only a few isolated mountain streams.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["expanded","fluctuated","contracted","stabilized"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Going from much of a continent to a few streams means the range shrank: "contracted." "Fluctuated" implies going up and down, and "expanded" and "stabilized" contradict the decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h339$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Political theorist Jonah Adebayo argues that civic trust is ______: built slowly over generations, it can be destroyed by a single scandal and may take decades to recover.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fragile","intangible","resilient","reciprocal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Something easily destroyed and slow to recover is "fragile." "Resilient" means the opposite. "Intangible" and "reciprocal" are true of trust in general but don't match the emphasis on easy destruction.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h340$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The film's special effects, groundbreaking when it was released in 1977, now look ______ to modern audiences accustomed to computer-generated imagery.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["innovative","seamless","dated","ominous"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Effects that were once groundbreaking but now compare poorly with modern imagery look "dated." "Innovative" and "seamless" describe how they once seemed, and "ominous" concerns mood.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h341$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The diplomat's memoir is less a record of events than an act of ______: she consistently portrays her own decisions as wise and blames setbacks on colleagues.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["self-discovery","self-justification","self-effacement","self-criticism"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Portraying one's decisions as wise and blaming others is defending oneself: "self-justification." "Self-criticism" and "self-effacement" are the opposite, and "self-discovery" implies personal insight that the description doesn't suggest.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h342$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The chemist's discovery was ______: she had been searching for a better adhesive when she noticed that a failed batch formed an unusually strong, flexible film.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["serendipitous","derivative","methodical","inevitable"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Finding something valuable by accident while looking for something else is "serendipitous." "Methodical" and "inevitable" imply a planned or certain result, and "derivative" means unoriginal.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h343$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Because the manuscript's author is unknown, scholars have tried to ______ its origins from indirect evidence: the type of parchment, the style of the handwriting, and the dialect in which it is written.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disregard","verify","fabricate","infer"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Drawing conclusions from indirect clues is to "infer." "Verify" means to confirm something already proposed, "fabricate" means to invent, and "disregard" means to ignore.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h344$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Critics have described the singer's voice as ______, noting that it can shift in a single phrase from a fragile whisper to a full-throated roar.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["monotonous","versatile","restrained","strident"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A voice moving from whisper to roar has a wide range: "versatile." "Monotonous" means unvarying, "strident" means harsh, and "restrained" means held back.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h345$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Some economists consider the region's rapid growth ______, warning that it depends on high commodity prices that could fall at any time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unsustainable","equitable","unprecedented","incremental"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Growth dependent on volatile prices that could collapse is "unsustainable." "Unprecedented" concerns novelty, "equitable" concerns fairness, and "incremental" contradicts "rapid."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h346$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 4, $q$The play's humor is ______; its jokes depend on references to obscure eighteenth-century politics, and few modern audience members understand them without footnotes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["slapstick","topical","broad","recondite"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Humor that requires obscure knowledge is "recondite." "Broad" and "slapstick" describe accessible, physical humor. "Topical" means relevant to current events, which describes the jokes in their own time but not their present obscurity.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h347$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's hypothesis was initially met with ______, but within a decade, accumulating evidence had persuaded most of her colleagues.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["acclaim","indifference","hostility","incredulity"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The contrast with later being "persuaded" indicates initial disbelief: "incredulity." "Acclaim" doesn't need persuasion, "indifference" suggests no reaction, and "hostility" implies anger rather than doubt about the evidence.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h348$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The settlement's layout suggests that its founders ______ defense over convenience: houses were clustered tightly on a hilltop far from the river that supplied their water.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["neglected","sacrificed","reconciled","prioritized"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Choosing a defensible hilltop despite the distance from water means valuing defense more: "prioritized." "Sacrificed" and "neglected" reverse the relationship, and "reconciled" implies balancing the two.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h349$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The museum's exhibition ______ the conventional narrative of the city's founding, giving equal space to the Indigenous communities whose land the settlers occupied.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["refutes","simplifies","reiterates","broadens"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Adding Indigenous perspectives to the founding story widens it: "broadens." "Reiterates" means repeats, "simplifies" means reduces, and "refutes" means disproves, which overstates adding perspectives.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h350$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The coach's strategy was ______: rather than relying on a few star players, she rotated the entire roster so that no one would be exhausted by the final minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["hierarchical","egalitarian","improvised","conservative"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Giving all players roughly equal time rather than relying on stars is "egalitarian." "Hierarchical" is the opposite. "Improvised" means unplanned, and "conservative" means cautious.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h351$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Although the two languages share many words, their grammars are ______; one marks tense with verb endings, the other with separate particles placed at the end of the sentence.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["interchangeable","divergent","rudimentary","analogous"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Grammars that mark tense in completely different ways are "divergent." "Analogous" and "interchangeable" imply similarity, and "rudimentary" means basic.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h352$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Ornithologists had long considered the bird ______ to the island, but genetic analysis revealed that its closest relatives live on the mainland, two hundred kilometers away, and that the two populations still occasionally interbreed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dormant","invasive","migratory","endemic"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The surprise is that the bird has close, interbreeding mainland relatives, which contradicts the belief that it was unique to the island: "endemic." "Invasive" implies harmful introduction, "dormant" means inactive, and "migratory" wouldn't be contradicted by mainland relatives.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h353$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The editor praised the young journalist's reporting as ______, noting that every claim in her articles was supported by documents or on-the-record sources.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["speculative","sensational","partisan","scrupulous"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Supporting every claim with evidence shows careful accuracy: "scrupulous." "Sensational" and "speculative" imply exaggeration or guesswork, and "partisan" implies bias.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h354$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The city's housing shortage is ______, the result of decades of restrictive zoning rather than a sudden surge in population.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["transient","unforeseen","exaggerated","entrenched"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A problem built up over decades is deeply established: "entrenched." "Transient" means short-lived, "unforeseen" means unexpected, and "exaggerated" questions whether the shortage is real.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h355$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The memoirist's tone toward her younger self is gently ______: she recounts her teenage certainties with affection but makes clear how little she then understood.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["detached","reverent","bitter","ironic"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Presenting her past certainties with affection while showing how naive they were creates gentle irony. "Reverent" implies admiration, "bitter" implies resentment, and "detached" implies a lack of the affection described.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h356$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ the sculptor's reputation, the forgery scandal, in an odd twist, increased interest in her genuine works and drove up their prices.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["diminishing","establishing","defending","enhancing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Rather than" sets up a contrast with the increased interest, so the expected effect would have been to damage her reputation: "diminishing." "Enhancing" describes what actually happened, and "establishing" and "defending" don't form the contrast.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h357$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The policy's effects have been ______: wealthy suburbs have benefited from lower taxes, while poorer neighborhoods have seen their schools lose funding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["uneven","uniform","temporary","negligible"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Benefits for some areas and losses for others mean the effects are "uneven." "Uniform" is the opposite, "negligible" means minor, and "temporary" concerns duration.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h358$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Physicist Ayo Bello argues that the experiment's surprising result is likely an ______, caused by a faulty sensor, rather than evidence of new physics.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["axiom","artifact","innovation","anomaly"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In science, an "artifact" is a false result introduced by equipment or method, which matches "caused by a faulty sensor." An "anomaly" is any unexpected result, which could still be evidence of new physics, so it doesn't capture Bello's point. "Axiom" and "innovation" don't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h359$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The festival's organizers took a ______ approach to programming, booking only performers who had appeared at previous festivals and avoiding any experimental acts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inclusive","cautious","daring","haphazard"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Booking only familiar performers and avoiding experiments is "cautious." "Daring" is the opposite, "haphazard" implies a lack of planning, and "inclusive" contradicts the exclusion of new acts.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h360$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though often described as ______, the desert supports a surprising diversity of life, including more than two hundred species of birds and dozens of flowering plants adapted to bloom after rare rains.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fertile","temperate","barren","pristine"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The contrast with "surprising diversity of life" requires a word meaning lifeless: "barren." "Fertile" would not contrast, "temperate" concerns climate, and "pristine" means unspoiled.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h361$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$The biographer's account is ______ by her close friendship with her subject; she omits the composer's well-documented feuds and portrays him as universally beloved.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["enriched","validated","compromised","complicated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Omitting unflattering facts because of friendship damages the account's reliability: "compromised." "Enriched" and "validated" are positive, and "complicated" doesn't convey the loss of reliability.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h362$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For decades, ecologists assumed that the decline of an Arctic seabird, the ivory gull, was driven mainly by shrinking sea ice. <u>Yet when biologist Freya Nilsen analyzed eggs collected over forty years, she found mercury concentrations rising steadily, reaching levels known to impair reproduction in other bird species.</u> Nilsen does not dismiss the role of sea ice, but she argues that pollution may be an equally important and long-overlooked factor.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that leads Nilsen to propose an additional explanation for the gull's decline.","It presents evidence that leads Nilsen to reject the view that sea ice loss has harmed the gull.","It describes a method that ecologists had long used to measure the effects of sea ice loss.","It provides an example of a species whose reproduction is known to be impaired by mercury."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mercury evidence leads Nilsen to argue that pollution is another important factor. The choice beginning "It presents evidence that leads Nilsen to reject…" is tempting, but the text says she does "not dismiss the role of sea ice," so she adds an explanation rather than rejecting one.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h363$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1913 novel. The narrator describes a character returning to her childhood home.<br><br>The house was smaller than she remembered, as houses always are. The porch sagged a little toward the east, and the lilac her mother had planted had grown wild, its branches pressing against the parlor window. <u>Yet when she stepped inside, the smell of the place—wax, woodsmoke, and something faintly sweet she could never name—undid all her composure at once.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the character is disappointed by how much the house has deteriorated since her childhood.","It shows that a sensory detail affects the character more powerfully than the changes she has observed.","It explains why the character remembers the house as being larger than it actually is.","It reveals that the character has never been able to identify the source of the house's distinctive smell."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After a calm inventory of visible changes, "Yet" introduces the smell, which overwhelms her ("undid all her composure"). The choice beginning "It reveals that the…" picks out a minor detail rather than the sentence's function, and the choice beginning "It suggests that the…" misreads her emotional response as disappointment.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h364$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that the first maps of the world were produced to help travelers find their way. Historian Paulo Mendes points out, however, that some of the oldest surviving maps, such as a Babylonian clay tablet from around 600 BCE, show places that no traveler could have reached, including mythical islands beyond a surrounding ocean. Such maps, Mendes argues, were less practical guides than statements about how their makers understood the cosmos and their own place within it.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It presents a historian's argument about ancient maps, raises an objection to that argument, and then explains why the objection ultimately fails.","It describes a historical artifact, explains how it was made, and then compares it with later maps.","It presents a common assumption, offers evidence that complicates it, and then describes an alternative interpretation.","It traces the development of mapmaking from ancient Babylon to the present day."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text opens with the assumption that maps guided travelers, gives the Babylonian tablet as counterevidence, and ends with Mendes's alternative view. It raises no objection to Mendes, and it doesn't explain how the tablet was made or trace history to the present.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h365$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying urban birds have found that great tits in cities sing at higher pitches than those in forests. <u>Low-frequency traffic noise, the researchers note, tends to mask lower-pitched songs.</u> Birds that sing higher may therefore be better able to communicate with potential mates and rivals in noisy environments.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that urban birds have difficulty attracting mates.","It offers an alternative explanation for the observation that city birds sing at higher pitches than forest birds.","It describes a problem that the researchers expected but did not observe in their study of urban birds.","It provides information that helps explain why city birds might benefit from singing at higher pitches."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The fact about traffic noise sets up the explanation in the final sentence: higher songs avoid being masked. It doesn't offer an alternative explanation or describe an unobserved problem, and the text doesn't claim urban birds have difficulty attracting mates.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h366$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1854, physician John Snow mapped cholera deaths in a London neighborhood and noticed that they clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street. Most experts at the time believed cholera spread through foul air. <u>Snow persuaded local officials to remove the pump's handle, and new cases in the area soon declined.</u> His work is now considered a founding moment in epidemiology, though some historians note that the outbreak was already subsiding when the handle was removed.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why most experts of the time believed that cholera spread through foul air.","It describes an action and outcome that seemed to support Snow's view, which the final sentence then qualifies.","It describes the method Snow used to map the cholera deaths in the London neighborhood.","It provides conclusive proof that cholera was spread through contaminated water rather than foul air, settling the debate described earlier."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The underlined sentence reports the intervention and the decline in cases, which appear to confirm Snow's theory. The final sentence then notes the outbreak was already subsiding. The choice beginning "It provides conclusive proof…" is tempting but ignores that qualification, so "conclusive" overstates the case.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h367$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is adapted from a 1908 essay.<br><br>The city dweller tells himself he is too busy to look at the sky. He crosses the square each morning with his eyes on the pavement, calculating the day's affairs. But the sky is there all the same, doing extraordinary things above his hat: clouds assembling and dissolving, light shifting from pearl to brass. He need only lift his head.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To encourage readers to notice the beauty that is available to them even in the midst of busy lives","To compare the skies above cities with those above the countryside","To argue that city life prevents people from appreciating the natural world","To describe the daily routine of a typical city dweller in the early twentieth century, from his morning walk to his evening return"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay contrasts the busy man's downward gaze with the remarkable sky and ends with "He need only lift his head," urging attention. The choice beginning "To argue that city…" overstates: the text says the beauty is available, not that city life prevents seeing it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h368$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economist Sara Lindqvist studied towns that received new public libraries in the early twentieth century, funded by philanthropist Andrew Carnegie. Comparing them with similar towns whose library applications were rejected, she found that the library towns later produced more patents per resident. <u>Lindqvist acknowledges that towns eager enough to apply may have differed in other ways, but she notes that rejected towns had also applied, so eagerness alone cannot explain the gap.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It introduces a second finding of Lindqvist's study about the towns that applied for Carnegie libraries.","It explains why Carnegie chose to fund libraries in some towns but not in others.","It concedes that Lindqvist's findings may be explained by differences among towns that she did not measure.","It anticipates a possible objection to Lindqvist's conclusion and explains why her study design addresses it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence raises the objection that eager towns might differ, then explains that comparing with rejected applicants rules this out. The choice beginning "It concedes that Lindqvist's…" captures only the concession and misses the rebuttal.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h369$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Octavia Mendes's novel <i>The Salt Road</i> follows three generations of a family of salt harvesters on the coast of Portugal. <u>Mendes devotes long passages to the technical details of the work—raking the crystals, timing the tides, reading the wind—sometimes for several pages at a time.</u> Some reviewers found these passages tedious, but others argued that they convey, as no summary could, how completely the work shaped the characters' lives.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why Mendes chose to write about salt harvesters.","It summarizes the plot of the novel's first section.","It provides evidence that the novel's account of salt harvesting is historically inaccurate.","It describes a feature of the novel about which reviewers disagreed."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence identifies the long technical passages, which the final sentence shows reviewers judged differently. It doesn't summarize plot, address accuracy, or explain her motives.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h370$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most volcanoes form along the boundaries of tectonic plates, where plates collide or pull apart. The Hawaiian Islands, however, sit in the middle of the Pacific Plate, thousands of kilometers from any boundary. Geologists explain them by a "hot spot": a plume of hot material rising from deep in Earth's mantle. As the Pacific Plate moves slowly northwest over this stationary plume, new volcanoes form, and older ones are carried away and become extinct.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To compare the Hawaiian Islands with volcanoes that form at plate boundaries","To explain how a group of volcanoes formed in a location where volcanoes would not typically be expected","To describe the process by which volcanoes on the Hawaiian Islands become extinct as they are carried away from the hot spot","To argue that most volcanoes form along the boundaries of tectonic plates"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text sets up Hawaii as an exception and explains its formation by the hot spot. The general rule is background, not an argument, and extinction is only one part of the explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h371$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1920 short story. Mrs. Halloran has just received a letter.<br><br>She read it twice, then folded it into precise thirds and set it on the mantel beside the clock. She wound the clock, though it did not need winding. She straightened the photograph of her son in his uniform, wiped a speck of dust from the glass, and then, very carefully, sat down.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the text's focus on Mrs. Halloran's actions?$q$, $q$["It conveys her emotional response to the letter indirectly through deliberate, controlled behavior.","It suggests that she is preparing her house for her son's return.","It emphasizes her habitual concern with cleanliness and order.","It shows that she is indifferent to the news contained in the letter and is occupied instead with routine household chores."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her unnecessary, meticulous actions, such as winding a clock that doesn't need it, hint at a strong emotion she is holding in check. The choice beginning "It emphasizes her habitual…" reads the actions literally and misses their emotional significance.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h372$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Psychologists once thought that infants were born with little ability to perceive the world, experiencing what William James called a "blooming, buzzing confusion." Experiments since the 1960s have overturned this view. <u>By measuring how long infants look at different images, researchers have shown that newborns prefer faces to other patterns and can distinguish their mother's voice from a stranger's within days of birth.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a limitation of the experiments conducted since the 1960s.","It explains why William James believed that newborn infants experience the world as a \"blooming, buzzing confusion.\"","It suggests that newborns prefer their mother's voice to her face.","It offers examples of findings that challenged an earlier view of infant perception."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence gives specific findings that overturned the "blooming, buzzing confusion" view. It doesn't explain James's reasoning, identify a limitation, or compare voice with face.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h373$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Botanist Lina Okoro has been studying the Wollemi pine, a tree known from fossils and thought extinct until a small grove was discovered in an Australian canyon in 1994. Fewer than one hundred wild trees survive. <u>Because nearly all of them are genetically identical, a single disease could wipe out the entire wild population.</u> To guard against this, Okoro and her colleagues have propagated thousands of seedlings and distributed them to botanical gardens around the world.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why the Wollemi pine was thought to be extinct until 1994.","It suggests that the seedlings distributed to botanical gardens around the world are more genetically diverse than wild trees.","It identifies a risk that motivates the conservation strategy described in the final sentence.","It describes a disease that has already damaged the wild population of Wollemi pines."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The genetic uniformity creates a vulnerability that the seedling program addresses. The sentence doesn't explain the belief in extinction or claim diversity among seedlings, and it describes a hypothetical, not actual, disease.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h374$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Literary critics often describe poet Langley Ross as a nature poet, citing her many poems about rivers, birds, and changing seasons. Scholar Imani Brooks argues that this label is misleading. In Ross's poems, Brooks observes, natural images almost always serve as metaphors for human relationships: a frozen river stands for a strained marriage, a migrating flock for children leaving home. For Ross, Brooks concludes, nature was less a subject than a language.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To explain why literary critics have described Ross as a nature poet","To describe several of Ross's poems about rivers, birds, and changing seasons","To present a scholar's argument that a common characterization of a poet overlooks how she used natural imagery","To argue that Ross's poems about human relationships are more successful than her poems about rivers, birds, and changing seasons"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text presents Brooks's view that the "nature poet" label misses Ross's metaphorical use of nature. It makes no judgment about which poems are more successful, and the critics' reasoning and the poem examples serve Brooks's argument.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h375$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 2015, a team of engineers set out to build a bridge in rural Rwanda using only local materials and labor. <u>The nearest cement supplier was a two-day drive away, and the community had no access to heavy machinery.</u> The engineers turned to a design based on traditional suspension bridges, using steel cables salvaged from old mining equipment and timber cut from nearby forests. The bridge, completed in four months, now carries hundreds of people across the river each day.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes constraints that help explain the engineers' choice of design and materials.","It provides evidence that traditional suspension bridges are stronger than modern bridges.","It suggests that the bridge took longer to complete than the engineers expected.","It explains why the engineers decided to build a bridge in rural Rwanda."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lack of cement and machinery explains the turn to a traditional design with local materials. The sentence doesn't explain why they built a bridge at all, and the text gives no information about expected timelines or comparative strength.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h376$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many studies have found that people who own dogs live longer, on average, than people who do not. Some researchers have concluded that dog ownership improves health, perhaps by encouraging exercise. Epidemiologist Rafael Duarte urges caution. People who are already healthy and active, he notes, may be more likely to get dogs in the first place, which would produce the same pattern even if dogs had no effect on health at all.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It presents a researcher's claim and then offers several pieces of evidence that support it.","It presents a hypothesis about dogs and health, describes an experiment designed to test that hypothesis, and then reports the results.","It presents a finding and a conclusion drawn from it, then describes a researcher's concern about that conclusion.","It describes two competing studies and explains why one is more reliable than the other."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text gives a correlation, a causal interpretation, and Duarte's concern about reverse causation. No experiment is described, and there are no competing studies or supporting evidence for a claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h377$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1913 novel set on the American prairie.<br><br>The shabby little town looked as if it had been set down on the prairie by accident. <u>None of the houses seemed to belong there; they huddled together as if each had been blown in by the same wind and was waiting for another to carry it away.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how the town's founders chose its location on the prairie.","It suggests that the town was recently damaged by a powerful storm.","It extends the opening sentence's portrayal of the town as impermanent and out of place.","It introduces a contrast between the town's appearance and the prosperity of its residents."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The image of houses blown in and waiting to be carried away develops the opening sentence's idea that the town was "set down ... by accident." The wind is figurative, not a literal storm, and nothing in the text concerns the residents' prosperity or the founders' choices.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h378$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Plastic recycling rates in many countries remain below 10 percent. Chemist Ji-woo Han argues that the problem is partly chemical: <u>most plastics are made of long polymer chains that degrade each time they are melted and reshaped, so recycled plastic is usually weaker than new plastic.</u> Han's lab is developing polymers that can be broken down into their original building blocks and reassembled without any loss of quality.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the results of an experiment conducted in Han's lab.","It suggests that recycled plastic is unsuitable for any practical use.","It offers a reason that recycling rates are expected to rise in the coming years.","It explains a limitation of current plastics that Han's research aims to overcome."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The degradation of polymer chains is the chemical problem Han's new polymers address. The text doesn't predict rising rates or describe experimental results, and "unsuitable for any practical use" overstates "usually weaker."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h379$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the Mexican painter Remedios Varo arrived in Mexico City in 1941 as a refugee from war in Europe, she supported herself by designing advertisements and decorating furniture. <u>Only in the 1950s, after more than a decade of such work, was she able to devote herself fully to painting.</u> The intricate, dreamlike canvases she produced in the final decade of her life are now considered among the most important works of Surrealism.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It establishes that the works for which Varo is now celebrated were produced relatively late in her life.","It suggests that the years Varo spent designing advertisements and decorating furniture shaped the style of her later paintings.","It indicates that Varo's paintings were unpopular during her lifetime.","It explains why Varo left Europe for Mexico City in 1941."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$By noting that full-time painting came only in the 1950s, the sentence prepares for the point that her major works came in her final decade. The text doesn't connect her commercial work to her style or describe her paintings' popularity during her life.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h380$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Biologists have long been puzzled by the yawn. It appears in nearly all vertebrates, from fish to humans, yet its function remains unclear. One hypothesis holds that yawning increases oxygen intake, but experiments in which people breathed air with extra oxygen found no change in how often they yawned. <u>A more recent hypothesis proposes that yawning cools the brain: studies have found that people yawn more when the air is cooler than their body temperature and less when it is hotter.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why yawning appears in nearly all vertebrates.","It provides additional evidence against the hypothesis that yawning increases oxygen intake.","It offers a conclusive answer to the puzzle introduced in the first sentence.","It presents an alternative explanation along with evidence that is consistent with it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$After rejecting the oxygen hypothesis, the text offers the brain-cooling hypothesis with supporting temperature data. The sentence doesn't address oxygen, and "conclusive" overstates a hypothesis the text calls "more recent," not proven.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h381$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In her history of the American lunch counter, Dana Whitfield traces how these inexpensive restaurants spread through department stores and drugstores in the early twentieth century, serving shoppers and workers who wanted a quick meal. <u>Whitfield then turns to the 1960 sit-ins in Greensboro, North Carolina, where Black students sat at a whites-only Woolworth's counter and refused to leave until they were served.</u> The ordinary lunch counter, she argues, became a powerful symbol precisely because it was so familiar.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why lunch counters became less popular after the early twentieth century.","It describes Whitfield's personal involvement in the civil rights movement.","It provides an example of how lunch counters spread through department stores and drugstores.","It introduces an event that shows how an everyday institution took on broader significance."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sit-ins illustrate how a familiar, ordinary place became symbolically powerful, as the final sentence explains. The sentence isn't an example of the counters' spread, and nothing indicates their decline or Whitfield's personal involvement.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h382$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For years, the standard advice for preventing peanut allergies in children was to avoid giving them peanuts until age three. <u>A 2015 clinical trial, however, found that infants who regularly ate peanut-containing foods starting in their first year were about 80 percent less likely to develop an allergy than infants who avoided peanuts.</u> Health agencies soon revised their guidelines to recommend early introduction for most infants.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a finding that health agencies initially rejected because of the trial's small size.","It presents a finding that contradicted earlier advice and prompted a change in official recommendations.","It explains why some infants are more likely than others to develop peanut allergies.","It describes a finding that confirmed the earlier advice to delay feeding peanuts to children until they reach the age of three."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The trial's result ran counter to the avoidance advice and led agencies to change their guidelines. It didn't confirm the old advice, it doesn't explain individual susceptibility, and the text says nothing about agencies rejecting it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h383$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1921 novel. The narrator is a young clerk.<br><br>Mr. Pell never raised his voice. He did not need to. When he was displeased, he would simply set down his pen, remove his spectacles, and polish them slowly with a white handkerchief, and the whole office would fall silent, every clerk bent over his ledger as though his life depended on a column of figures.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe the daily tasks performed by clerks in an office in the early twentieth century","To explain why Mr. Pell has become displeased with the work of the narrator and his colleagues","To portray a character whose authority is expressed through quiet gestures rather than open anger","To suggest that the clerks in the office secretly mock Mr. Pell's habits and mannerisms"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text shows that Mr. Pell commands the office through small, deliberate actions instead of shouting. It describes his effect, not the clerks' tasks, and nothing suggests mockery or explains a particular cause of his displeasure.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h384$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Urban planner Mei Lin studied two neighborhoods with similar populations and incomes. One had wide streets designed for cars; the other had narrow streets lined with shops and small parks. <u>Residents of the second neighborhood walked an average of 40 minutes more per week and reported knowing more of their neighbors by name.</u> Lin argues that street design can shape not only how people travel but also how connected they feel to their communities.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how Lin selected the two neighborhoods to compare in her study.","It presents findings that Lin uses as the basis for a broader claim about street design.","It suggests that residents of the neighborhood with wide streets were less healthy overall than residents of the other neighborhood.","It offers a limitation that weakens Lin's conclusion about street design."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The walking and neighbor-knowledge findings support Lin's claim in the final sentence. The selection criteria come in the first part of the text, nothing in the sentence weakens her conclusion, and health isn't discussed.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h385$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Historians have long debated why the Indus Valley civilization, which flourished from about 2600 to 1900 BCE, declined. Earlier theories blamed invasion or a sudden catastrophe. More recent research points to a gradual weakening of the monsoon, which would have reduced the river flooding that farmers depended on. <u>Sediment cores from lakes in the region show a drying trend beginning around 2200 BCE, consistent with this explanation.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why earlier historians believed that invasion or a sudden catastrophe had caused the civilization's decline around 2200 BCE.","It offers physical evidence that supports the more recent explanation for the civilization's decline.","It presents evidence that a sudden catastrophe destroyed the civilization around 2200 BCE.","It indicates that the civilization was most prosperous around 2200 BCE, before declining."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sediment cores show gradual drying, which supports the monsoon hypothesis. A "trend" is gradual, not a sudden catastrophe, and the sentence says nothing about invasion theories or peak prosperity.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h386$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many readers know Mary Seacole only as a nurse who cared for British soldiers during the Crimean War. Her 1857 autobiography reveals a more varied life. Born in Jamaica, Seacole ran a hotel in Panama, traveled widely in the Caribbean, and learned herbal medicine from her mother. In Crimea, she operated not a hospital but a combined store and restaurant, from which she provided care to soldiers on the side.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To compare Seacole's work during the Crimean War with that of other nurses","To show that Seacole's life was more complex than the familiar image of her suggests","To describe the herbal remedies that Seacole learned from her mother in Jamaica","To argue that Seacole's contributions as a nurse have been greatly exaggerated by historians"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text contrasts the narrow popular image with the broader life described in her autobiography. It doesn't claim her nursing was exaggerated, herbal medicine is only one detail, and no other nurses are discussed.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h387$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Some deep-sea fish produce red light, an unusual ability because most deep-sea creatures cannot see red wavelengths. <u>The stoplight loosejaw, for example, emits red light from organs beneath its eyes, allowing it to illuminate prey that cannot see the light and therefore does not know it is being watched.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes a fish that is an exception to the pattern of red light production.","It explains why most deep-sea creatures are unable to see red wavelengths of light.","It gives an example of how a fish benefits from the unusual ability described in the first sentence.","It suggests that the stoplight loosejaw is the only deep-sea fish that can produce red light, which makes it unusual among predators."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The loosejaw illustrates red light production and shows its advantage: hidden illumination of prey. The sentence doesn't explain why others can't see red, the fish isn't an exception, and "for example" implies other species exist.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h388$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Composer Hiroshi Tanabe's early works were dense and dissonant, admired by critics but rarely performed. In his forties, he began incorporating folk melodies from his native Hokkaido into his music. <u>Audiences responded enthusiastically, and his later symphonies became staples of orchestral programs in Japan.</u> Some critics, however, complained that Tanabe had sacrificed his originality for popularity.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the public reception of Tanabe's change in style, which the final sentence contrasts with a critical response.","It suggests that Tanabe's early works were more original than his later symphonies.","It provides evidence that critics admired Tanabe's later works more than his early ones.","It explains why Tanabe began incorporating folk melodies from his native Hokkaido into his music after years of writing dissonant works."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence reports audiences' enthusiasm for the new style, which contrasts with some critics' complaint. It doesn't explain his motive, and it concerns audiences, not critics. The final judgment about originality belongs to the critics, not this sentence.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h389$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Astronomer Nadia Osei searches for planets orbiting other stars by watching for tiny dips in a star's brightness, which can occur when a planet passes in front of it. <u>The method has a built-in bias: it detects only planets whose orbits happen to be aligned edge-on with Earth, so most planetary systems are invisible to it.</u> Osei and her colleagues correct for this bias statistically when estimating how common planets are.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how planets passing in front of their stars cause the tiny dips in brightness that Osei and her colleagues watch for.","It suggests that Osei's estimates of how common planets are cannot be trusted.","It identifies a shortcoming of the method that the final sentence explains how researchers address.","It describes an alternative method that Osei uses to detect planets."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence names the alignment bias, and the final sentence says how Osei corrects for it. The choice beginning "It suggests that Osei's…" is tempting but ignores that correction, and the sentence describes no alternative method.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h390$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1905 novel. Lily is attending a party.<br><br>Lily laughed at the right moments and said the expected things, but her mind was elsewhere. She had learned long ago that a smile could be worn like a garment, put on in the morning and taken off at night, and that no one at such gatherings ever looked closely enough to see the seams.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the comparison of a smile to a garment?$q$, $q$["It indicates that Lily enjoys the party more than she expected she would.","It emphasizes that Lily is dressed more elegantly than the other guests at the party.","It reveals that the other guests at the party are also concealing their true feelings behind smiles worn like garments.","It suggests that Lily's cheerfulness is a deliberate performance that others fail to recognize."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A smile "worn like a garment" with hidden "seams" depicts her cheerfulness as a costume no one sees through. The comparison isn't literal, and it doesn't say anything about other guests concealing their feelings.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h391$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Andrés Villa interviewed 120 people who had moved from rural villages to Mexico City. Many described their first months as lonely and disorienting. <u>Yet nearly all reported that within two years they had built close networks of friends, often through churches, sports leagues, or people from their home regions.</u> Villa argues that cities, often portrayed as isolating, can foster strong communities for newcomers who find the right institutions.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why migrants from rural villages often experience loneliness during their first months in the city.","It presents a finding that complicates the initial impression of migrants' experience and supports Villa's argument.","It suggests that most migrants to Mexico City eventually return to their home regions.","It provides evidence that cities are generally more isolating than rural villages."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Yet" turns from early loneliness to later community, which supports Villa's claim that cities can foster community. The sentence doesn't explain loneliness or mention returning home, and the choice beginning "It provides evidence that…" reverses Villa's argument.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h392$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The modern bicycle seems simple, but its design took decades to develop. Early versions in the 1860s, nicknamed "boneshakers," had iron-rimmed wooden wheels and pedals attached directly to the front wheel. To go faster, makers enlarged the front wheel, producing the towering "high-wheeler" of the 1870s, which was fast but prone to dangerous falls. Only in the 1880s did the "safety bicycle," with equal-sized wheels and a chain drive, combine speed with stability.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It describes a problem with the modern bicycle and then proposes a solution to that problem.","It makes a general claim about the bicycle and then traces a series of designs that illustrate it.","It compares two bicycle designs and argues that the earlier one was better than the later one.","It explains how bicycles are manufactured today and then describes how they are maintained."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text claims the design took decades and then traces the boneshaker, high-wheeler, and safety bicycle. It proposes no solution, covers three designs without preferring the earlier ones, and doesn't discuss manufacturing.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h393$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Biologists studying sea turtles have found that the temperature of the sand in which eggs incubate determines the hatchlings' sex: warmer sand produces more females. <u>On some beaches in northern Australia, more than 99 percent of young green turtles are now female.</u> Researchers worry that if temperatures continue to rise, some populations may eventually lack enough males to reproduce.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the population of green turtles in northern Australia is declining rapidly.","It explains why sea turtles in northern Australia prefer to lay their eggs on warmer beaches.","It provides a striking example of the effect described in the first sentence, which sets up the concern in the final sentence.","It presents evidence that sand temperature has little effect on whether sea turtle hatchlings are male or female, contrary to earlier studies."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The extreme female ratio illustrates temperature-dependent sex determination and leads to worries about too few males. It supports, not undercuts, the effect, and the text discusses neither nesting preferences nor current population decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h394$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Linguist Clara Weiss studies whistled languages, in which speakers convey words by whistling rather than speaking. These languages arise in places with rugged terrain, such as mountain valleys, where a whistle carries much farther than a shout. <u>Silbo Gomero, used on the Canary Island of La Gomera, can be understood at distances of up to five kilometers.</u> Weiss argues that whistled languages show how flexibly human language adapts to its environment.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It offers a specific example that illustrates the advantage of whistled languages described in the previous sentence.","It describes a whistled language that differs from the others discussed in the text in being used mainly on flat, open terrain.","It presents evidence that whistled languages are more expressive than spoken languages.","It explains how speakers of whistled languages learn to produce words by whistling."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Silbo Gomero's five-kilometer range illustrates how whistles carry farther than shouts. The sentence doesn't address learning or expressiveness, and La Gomera is mountainous, consistent with the rugged-terrain pattern.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h395$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the painter Alma Thomas retired from teaching in 1960, at age sixty-eight, she had exhibited only occasionally. Over the next decade, she developed a distinctive style of bright, mosaic-like brushstrokes. In 1972, she became the first Black woman to have a solo exhibition at the Whitney Museum of American Art. Her late flowering, critics have noted, challenges the common assumption that artists do their most important work when young.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To argue that artists who begin painting seriously late in life generally produce better and more original work than younger artists","To present an artist's career as a counterexample to a widely held assumption about artistic achievement","To describe the technique that Alma Thomas used to create her mosaic-like brushstrokes","To explain why Alma Thomas exhibited her paintings only occasionally while she was teaching"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text traces Thomas's late success and ends by noting that it challenges the idea that artists peak young. It doesn't explain her technique or her early infrequent exhibiting, and it doesn't claim late starters are generally better.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h396$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most leaves are green because they contain chlorophyll, which absorbs red and blue light and reflects green. <u>In autumn, deciduous trees stop producing chlorophyll, and as it breaks down, yellow and orange pigments that were present all along become visible.</u> Red colors, by contrast, come from pigments that many trees produce only in autumn.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light but reflects green light.","It describes a process that causes leaves to produce yellow and orange pigments in autumn.","It explains the source of some autumn colors, setting up a contrast with the source of red.","It suggests that red pigments are present in leaves throughout the year."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence explains that yellow and orange were hidden all along, and "by contrast" introduces red pigments made only in autumn. The choice beginning "It describes a process…" misreads it: the yellow pigments are revealed, not produced, in autumn. The choice beginning "It suggests that red…" reverses the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h397$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have found that children who are read to frequently develop larger vocabularies than those who are not. <u>Psychologist Leah Grant points out, however, that the books parents choose may matter as much as how often they read: picture books contain, on average, more rare words than everyday conversation does.</u> Grant suggests that parents who simply talk more with their children may not provide the same benefits as reading together.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that the frequency with which parents read to their children has no effect on the size of children's vocabularies.","It describes the method Grant used to measure the size of children's vocabularies.","It introduces a qualification about what makes reading beneficial, which leads to Grant's suggestion in the final sentence.","It explains why parents tend to choose picture books rather than other kinds of books."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Grant adds that book content matters, specifically rare words in picture books, which explains her final suggestion about talk versus reading. She says book choice matters "as much as" frequency, not that frequency has no effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h398$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1937 novel.<br><br>Grandmother kept the farm's accounts in a black notebook that no one else was permitted to touch. Every evening she sat at the kitchen table and wrote in it by lamplight, her lips moving silently. When she died, my father opened the notebook at last. It contained no numbers at all, only the names of birds she had seen that day, each followed by a single word: <i>early</i>, <i>late</i>, <i>alone</i>, <i>many</i>.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why Grandmother refused to let anyone else touch the notebook.","It reveals that the notebook served a purpose different from the one the family had assumed.","It suggests that Grandmother had become confused during the final years of her life and had stopped keeping the farm's accounts.","It confirms that Grandmother kept careful records of the farm's finances."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The family believed the notebook held accounts, but it held bird observations, a surprise revealed only at the end. It doesn't confirm finances, the reason for secrecy is left implicit, and nothing suggests confusion; the entries are orderly.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h399$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people believe that the Great Wall of China is visible from the Moon with the naked eye. <u>In fact, the wall, though thousands of kilometers long, is generally less than ten meters wide, far too narrow to be seen from that distance.</u> Even from low Earth orbit, astronauts report that it is difficult to spot without magnification.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the wall would be visible from the Moon if it were somewhat longer.","It provides evidence that the Great Wall is the longest structure ever built by humans.","It corrects a popular belief by explaining why the wall cannot be seen from the Moon.","It describes the experiences of astronauts who have tried to see the wall from orbit."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence refutes the belief by pointing to the wall's narrow width. Its length is mentioned only to concede a point, the astronauts appear in the next sentence, and narrowness, not length, is the issue.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h400$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economist Ruth Park examined why some farmers in Malawi adopted a new drought-resistant maize variety while their neighbors did not. Price and availability, she found, explained little of the difference. <u>Instead, adoption spread through social networks: farmers were far more likely to plant the new variety if a friend or relative had already grown it successfully.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides an example of a farmer who adopted the new maize variety because of its low price.","It explains how the drought-resistant maize variety was developed and distributed to farmers.","It suggests that farmers who adopted the new variety were wealthier than those who did not.","It presents the factor that Park found to explain adoption after ruling out other explanations."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$After dismissing price and availability, Park identifies social networks as the explanation. The sentence gives a pattern, not an individual example. It doesn't discuss development or wealth, and price was ruled out.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h401$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1610, Galileo turned his telescope toward Jupiter and observed four small points of light near the planet that changed position from night to night. He concluded that they were moons orbiting Jupiter. <u>This was a significant finding, because many scholars of the time held that everything in the heavens orbited Earth.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that Galileo's contemporaries, including the scholars who believed everything orbited Earth, quickly accepted his conclusion.","It describes the method Galileo used to determine that the points of light were moons.","It explains why Galileo's observation was important in the context of beliefs held at the time.","It presents evidence that the points of light Galileo observed were not actually moons."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence explains the finding's significance: objects orbiting Jupiter contradicted the view that everything orbited Earth. It doesn't describe method or reception, and it doesn't challenge the observation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h402$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Cognitive scientist Omar Haddad studies how people estimate quantities. In one experiment, participants were shown jars of marbles and asked to guess how many each contained. Individual guesses varied wildly, some off by more than 50 percent. <u>But the average of all the guesses was typically within 5 percent of the true number.</u> Haddad argues that this "wisdom of crowds" effect depends on people guessing independently, without hearing one another's answers.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a result that contrasts with the inaccuracy of individual guesses and illustrates the effect Haddad discusses.","It explains why individual participants' guesses about the number of marbles varied so widely.","It provides evidence that participants heard one another's answers before making their guesses, which explains why the average was accurate.","It describes a limitation of Haddad's experiment that may have affected his results."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"But" contrasts the accurate average with the wild individual guesses, illustrating the wisdom-of-crowds effect. The sentence doesn't explain the variation, and the text implies independence rather than shared answers.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h403$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many historians credit the printing press with sparking the Protestant Reformation. Before printing, they note, Martin Luther's arguments would have reached only a small audience; with it, his pamphlets spread across Europe within weeks, turning a local dispute into a continental movement.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Ilse Berger cautions against treating technology as the cause of the Reformation. Printing presses, she observes, also operated in Spain and Italy, where Luther's ideas gained little ground. What mattered, Berger argues, was a combination of political conditions—especially German princes eager to assert independence from Rome—that made audiences receptive.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Berger (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' view in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would agree that printing caused the Reformation but argue that its effects were limited to Germany, where the pamphlets first circulated widely.","She would reject the idea that printing played any role in spreading Luther's ideas.","She would argue that printing alone cannot explain the Reformation's success, since it did not produce the same result everywhere.","She would argue that Luther's pamphlets actually spread more slowly than historians have claimed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Berger points to printing in Spain and Italy without a comparable result, so she would argue that printing wasn't sufficient. She doesn't deny that printing played any role, which makes the choice beginning "She would reject the…" too strong, and she doesn't dispute the speed of the spread.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h404$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Ecologist Jonas Reiter argues that reintroducing wolves to Yellowstone National Park in 1995 triggered a "trophic cascade": by hunting elk and changing where elk grazed, wolves allowed willows and aspens to recover along streams, which in turn stabilized riverbanks and brought back beavers and songbirds.<br><br>Text 2<br>Studies since 2010 have found that willow recovery in Yellowstone has been patchy and modest. Ecologist Meera Shah notes that elk numbers also fell because of human hunting outside the park and severe winters, and that decades of elk browsing had lowered water tables, making it hard for willows to regrow even where elk were scarce.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Shah (Text 2) most likely respond to Reiter's claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would argue that wolves had no measurable effect on the number or behavior of elk in the park.","She would argue that the reintroduction of beavers, rather than wolves, caused the willows to recover.","She would suggest that Reiter overstates the wolves' influence, since other factors affected elk and willows.","She would agree with Reiter and offer additional evidence that willows have fully recovered along streams."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Shah cites human hunting, winters, and lowered water tables, plus patchy recovery, so she would say Reiter overstates the wolves' role. She doesn't claim wolves had no effect, she describes recovery as modest, not full, and she doesn't credit beavers.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h405$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some educators argue that students learn best when they discover principles on their own through open-ended exploration. When students construct knowledge themselves, the argument goes, they understand it more deeply and remember it longer than when they are simply told.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Daniel Ferro reviewed decades of classroom studies and found that novice learners generally performed better with explicit instruction, in which teachers demonstrate procedures and explain concepts directly. Exploration, Ferro found, benefited mainly students who already had substantial background knowledge in a subject.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ferro (Text 2) most likely respond to the educators' argument in Text 1?$q$, $q$["He would argue that students remember information longer when they discover it themselves.","He would agree that open-ended exploration is the most effective approach for all students.","He would argue that it applies at best to experienced learners and not to most beginners.","He would claim that background knowledge has little effect on how students learn."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ferro found exploration helped mainly students with background knowledge, while novices did better with explicit instruction. He would limit the educators' claim to experienced learners. The other choices contradict his findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h406$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Art historian Paula Greer argues that the Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer used a camera obscura—a darkened box that projects an image through a lens—to compose his paintings. The soft, out-of-focus highlights in works like <i>The Milkmaid</i>, she notes, resemble the optical effects such a device produces.<br><br>Text 2<br>Scholar Henrik Vos acknowledges that Vermeer's paintings contain optical effects consistent with a camera obscura. But Vos points out that no inventory of Vermeer's possessions lists such a device, and that painters of his era could have observed similar effects simply by studying light closely. The visual evidence, Vos argues, is suggestive but not decisive.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Vos (Text 2) would most likely respond to Greer's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that the inventory of Vermeer's possessions proves that he never used a camera obscura.","He would grant that her evidence is relevant but argue that it does not rule out other explanations.","He would fully agree with Greer and offer the inventory as further support for her view.","He would argue that Vermeer's paintings contain no optical effects of the kind Greer describes."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Vos accepts that the effects exist ("consistent with a camera obscura") but calls the evidence "not decisive," since close observation could explain them. The inventory doesn't "prove" anything; the absence of evidence is only one point, and he doesn't fully agree.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h407$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Because octopuses can solve puzzles, use tools, and recognize individual humans, many researchers describe them as highly intelligent. Their intelligence is especially remarkable because it evolved independently of that of mammals and birds, in animals that live only a year or two.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Ruth Akande warns against measuring octopuses by human standards. Calling an octopus "intelligent" because it solves puzzles designed by people, she argues, tells us more about our own expectations than about how octopuses actually experience and navigate their world, which may involve abilities we have not thought to test.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Akande (Text 2) most likely view the evidence for octopus intelligence described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["As irrelevant, because octopuses live too briefly to develop real intelligence","As reliable only because it was gathered in the octopuses' natural habitats","As convincing proof that octopuses are more intelligent than mammals and birds, since their intelligence evolved in such short-lived animals","As potentially incomplete, because it reflects the kinds of abilities humans value and may miss others"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Akande argues that human-designed tests reflect our expectations and may miss abilities we haven't tested. She doesn't rank intelligence, she doesn't dismiss octopus cognition on the grounds of lifespan, and nothing says the tests were done in the wild.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h408$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Urban economist Felix Aranda argues that building more housing in expensive cities will lower rents over time. When supply rises, he contends, landlords must compete for tenants, and even new luxury apartments free up older units as wealthier residents move into them.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Grace Liu studied neighborhoods where large numbers of new apartments were built. She found that average rents in surrounding areas did rise more slowly than citywide, but that the effect took about five years to appear and was smallest for the lowest-cost units.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Liu (Text 2) most likely respond to Aranda's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["Her findings would contradict it, since rents near new construction rose faster than in the rest of the city.","Her findings would largely support it, while suggesting that the benefits are gradual and uneven.","Her findings would neither support nor contradict it, since she did not study rents.","Her findings would support it and show that the lowest-cost units benefit most from new construction."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slower rent growth near new construction supports Aranda, but Liu's findings add that the effect is delayed and weakest for cheap units. The choice beginning "Her findings would contradict…" reverses her finding, and the choice beginning "Her findings would support…" reverses the detail about low-cost units.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h409$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>The novelist Jean Rhys's <i>Wide Sargasso Sea</i> (1966) retells the story of the "madwoman in the attic" from Charlotte Brontë's <i>Jane Eyre</i>, giving her a history and a voice. Critic Anna Moll praises the novel as a corrective: it exposes how Brontë's book reduced a Caribbean woman to a frightening symbol.<br><br>Text 2<br>Critic Tobias Wren admires Rhys's novel but resists calling it a "corrective." The label, he argues, implies that <i>Wide Sargasso Sea</i> matters chiefly as a response to Brontë. Rhys's novel, in Wren's view, stands on its own as a work of art whose power does not depend on readers knowing the earlier book.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Moll (Text 1) and Wren (Text 2) would most likely agree that <i>Wide Sargasso Sea</i>$q$, $q$["is a novel worthy of admiration.","fails to give the character from <i>Jane Eyre</i> a convincing voice.","should be read only by those who are familiar with <i>Jane Eyre</i>.","is valuable mainly because of its relationship to <i>Jane Eyre</i>."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Moll praises the novel and Wren admires it, so both value it. They disagree about whether its importance lies in its response to Brontë. Wren explicitly rejects the choices beginning "is valuable mainly because…" and "should be read only…", and neither critic suggests the fourth.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h410$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Physicist Lena Holm argues that nuclear fusion will become a practical energy source within twenty years. Recent experiments, she notes, have for the first time produced more energy from fusion reactions than the lasers delivered to the fuel, a milestone long considered the key scientific hurdle.<br><br>Text 2<br>Engineer Victor Osei agrees that the recent results are a genuine scientific achievement. But he points out that the lasers themselves consumed far more electricity than the reaction produced, and that a working power plant would also need to repeat the reaction many times per second for years. These engineering challenges, Osei argues, are at least as daunting as the scientific ones.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Osei (Text 2) most likely respond to Holm's prediction (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would consider it overly optimistic because the achievement she cites leaves major practical obstacles unresolved.","He would argue that fusion will never be used as a practical energy source under any circumstances.","He would argue that the recent experiments did not actually produce more energy from fusion than the lasers delivered to the fuel.","He would agree with it, since the recent experiments have solved the main challenges to fusion power."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Osei accepts the scientific milestone but stresses the overall energy balance and the need for repeated reactions, so he would find Holm's timeline too optimistic. He doesn't dispute the milestone, and "never" goes beyond anything he says.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h411$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Linguists once assumed that the earliest human languages were simple, with few words and little grammar, and that complexity developed gradually. Some still argue that the languages of small, isolated societies preserve this early simplicity.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Ama Mensah has documented languages spoken by small communities in West Africa and the Amazon. Many, she reports, have grammars more intricate than those of widely spoken languages like English, with elaborate systems for marking how a speaker knows a piece of information—whether by seeing it, hearing it, or inferring it.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mensah (Text 2) most likely respond to the view that small societies' languages preserve early simplicity (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would agree, noting that these languages have fewer words than widely spoken languages.","She would argue that these languages are simple in grammar but complex in vocabulary.","She would argue that English preserves more features of early human language than other languages.","She would point to evidence that such languages can be highly complex, undermining the view."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Mensah's evidence of intricate grammars in small communities contradicts the claim that their languages are simple. She says nothing about vocabulary size or English preserving early features.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h412$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many nutrition researchers recommend that adults drink eight glasses of water a day. Staying well hydrated, they argue, improves concentration, supports kidney function, and helps regulate body temperature.<br><br>Text 2<br>Physiologist Henrik Larsen notes that there is little scientific basis for the specific figure of eight glasses. Much of the water people need, he points out, comes from food and from other beverages, and healthy adults can generally rely on thirst to tell them when to drink. Larsen does not dispute that adequate hydration is important.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Larsen (Text 2) most likely respond to the recommendation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["He would agree that adults should drink exactly eight glasses of water a day in addition to other beverages.","He would argue that drinking water does not affect concentration or kidney function.","He would argue that most adults should drink considerably more than eight glasses of water a day.","He would accept that hydration matters but question the need for a specific number of glasses of water."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Larsen affirms that hydration is important but says the eight-glass figure lacks support. He doesn't deny hydration's benefits, and he never suggests drinking more.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h413$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Teresa Ruiz argues that the Maya city of Tikal declined in the ninth century primarily because of prolonged drought. Lake sediments from the region record several decades of unusually low rainfall at the time the city's population fell.<br><br>Text 2<br>Archaeologist Kofi Danso agrees that drought struck the region but notes that some Maya cities only a few hundred kilometers from Tikal continued to thrive through the same period. He argues that warfare among rival cities and the collapse of trade networks made Tikal especially vulnerable when drought arrived.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Danso (Text 2) most likely respond to Ruiz's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that drought alone cannot explain Tikal's decline, since other factors made the city vulnerable.","He would agree that drought was the only significant cause of the decline of Maya cities.","He would argue that Tikal's population actually grew during the period of drought.","He would dispute the evidence from lake sediments that a prolonged drought occurred in the region during the ninth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Danso accepts the drought but argues that warfare and trade collapse explain why Tikal fell while nearby cities survived. He doesn't dispute the drought, doesn't call it the only cause, and doesn't claim Tikal grew.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h414$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Composer Mariana Duarte argues that classical orchestras should commission more new works. Programs dominated by eighteenth- and nineteenth-century music, she contends, turn orchestras into museums and discourage younger audiences who want to hear music that reflects their own time.<br><br>Text 2<br>Orchestra director Paul Stein notes that when his orchestra programmed a season heavy with new works, ticket sales fell by a third. Many longtime subscribers, he observes, attend specifically to hear familiar masterpieces. Stein now pairs each new piece with a well-known symphony, an approach that has kept attendance steady while still introducing contemporary music.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Stein (Text 2) most likely respond to Duarte's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that younger audiences prefer eighteenth- and nineteenth-century music to new works.","He would agree that programming more new works reliably increases ticket sales, especially among the younger audiences Duarte hopes to attract.","He would argue that orchestras should never perform new works, since audiences prefer older music.","He would support introducing new works but caution that doing so too heavily may alienate existing audiences."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Stein still introduces new works but found that a heavy dose cut sales, so he pairs new and familiar pieces. He doesn't reject new music, and his experience contradicts the claim that new works raise sales. He says nothing about younger audiences' preferences.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h415$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Researchers who study remote work have found that employees working from home report higher job satisfaction and complete more tasks per hour than their office-based colleagues. These findings, the researchers argue, suggest that companies should allow most employees to work remotely.<br><br>Text 2<br>Economist Laila Haddad notes that most studies of remote work measure individual productivity over a few months. Her research on software teams, tracked over three years, found that remote workers were promoted less often and that teams working entirely remotely produced fewer innovative products, possibly because chance conversations among colleagues became rarer.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Haddad (Text 2) most likely respond to the researchers' recommendation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would agree with it and offer her findings as further evidence that remote work increases innovation.","She would suggest that it overlooks longer-term and team-level costs not captured by the studies it relies on.","She would argue that individual productivity is higher in offices than at home.","She would argue that remote workers are actually less satisfied with their jobs than office-based workers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Haddad's long-term, team-level research reveals costs (fewer promotions, less innovation) that short-term individual studies miss. She doesn't dispute the satisfaction or productivity findings, and her findings go against innovation benefits.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h416$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some biologists argue that the bright colors of poison dart frogs evolved as a warning to predators: the vivid patterns advertise the frogs' toxicity, and predators that have had an unpleasant experience learn to avoid them.<br><br>Text 2<br>Biologist Sofia Marín studied a species of poison dart frog whose populations vary in color, from bright red to dull brown. She found that the brightest populations were not the most toxic, and that in some regions, color seemed to play a larger role in attracting mates than in deterring predators.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Marín (Text 2) most likely respond to the biologists' argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that poison dart frogs are not actually toxic to predators.","She would suggest that the warning explanation may not account fully for the frogs' coloration.","She would agree that the brightest frogs are always the most toxic, as the argument predicts.","She would argue that predators are unable to see the colors of poison dart frogs."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Marín's findings, that brightness doesn't track toxicity and color attracts mates, suggest the warning explanation is incomplete. She doesn't deny toxicity, her findings contradict the choice beginning "She would agree that…", and she says nothing about predators' vision.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h417$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Critics have praised the documentary <i>River Voices</i> for letting residents of a flood-damaged town tell their own stories, without narration. The absence of an outside narrator, they argue, gives the film an unusual intimacy and authenticity.<br><br>Text 2<br>Film scholar Naomi Brandt points out that even without narration, the director shaped the story through editing: she selected which of more than two hundred hours of interviews to include and arranged them in a particular order. The film's apparent authenticity, Brandt argues, is itself a carefully constructed effect.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Brandt (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' claim in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would agree that the film presents the residents' stories without any influence from the director.","She would argue that the residents' stories in the film were scripted by the director.","She would argue that the film would have been more authentic with an outside narrator.","She would argue that the critics overlook how the director's choices shape what seems authentic."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Brandt says editing shapes the film, so its authenticity is constructed, which challenges the critics' view that the absence of a narrator makes it authentic. She doesn't claim the stories were scripted, and she doesn't recommend narration.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h418$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many scientists have argued that the dinosaurs went extinct about 66 million years ago because of an asteroid impact near what is now Mexico's Yucatán Peninsula. The impact would have thrown enough debris into the atmosphere to block sunlight and collapse food chains worldwide.<br><br>Text 2<br>Geologist Aisha Kamara notes that massive volcanic eruptions in India, known as the Deccan Traps, were releasing enormous amounts of gas in the same period. She argues that the eruptions had already stressed ecosystems before the asteroid struck, and that the extinction resulted from both events together.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kamara (Text 2) most likely respond to the explanation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would agree that the asteroid impact was the sole cause of the dinosaurs' extinction.","She would argue that the asteroid impact did not occur at the time of the dinosaurs' extinction.","She would argue that it is incomplete because it leaves out another major contributing factor.","She would argue that the volcanic eruptions in India occurred long after the dinosaurs went extinct."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Kamara accepts the asteroid but adds the Deccan Traps as a contributing cause, so she would call the explanation incomplete. She doesn't deny the impact or call it the sole cause, and she places the eruptions in the same period.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h419$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Educational researcher Marcus Lee argues that schools should ban smartphones during the school day. Phones, he contends, distract students from lessons and reduce face-to-face interaction among peers.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Priya Nair studied schools that adopted phone bans. Test scores rose modestly at schools where phone use had previously been widespread, but changed little at schools where most students already kept their phones put away. Nair suggests that the benefits of a ban depend on how much phones were disrupting a school in the first place.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Nair (Text 2) most likely respond to Lee's recommendation (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would suggest that a ban may help some schools considerably more than others.","She would argue that phones increase face-to-face interaction among students.","She would argue that phone bans lower test scores at most schools that adopt them.","She would agree that every school should adopt a ban, since bans raise scores everywhere."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Nair found benefits only where phone use had been widespread, so a ban's value varies by school. Her findings show no score declines, contradict "raise scores everywhere," and don't address face-to-face interaction.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h420$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Anthropologist Elena Kovacs argues that the spread of agriculture about 10,000 years ago improved human well-being. Farming, she notes, produced food surpluses that supported larger populations, permanent settlements, and eventually cities, writing, and specialized crafts.<br><br>Text 2<br>Skeletal remains from early farming communities tell a more mixed story. Compared with earlier hunter-gatherers, early farmers were often shorter, had more tooth decay, and showed more signs of nutritional stress and infectious disease, likely because of diets dominated by a few crops and crowded living conditions.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Kovacs's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["By suggesting that the benefits she describes for societies did not necessarily translate into better health for individuals","By arguing that hunter-gatherers developed writing and cities before the spread of agriculture","By arguing that the spread of agriculture did not actually lead to larger populations, permanent settlements, or the eventual rise of cities","By agreeing that early farmers were healthier than the hunter-gatherers who preceded them"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 2's skeletal evidence shows worse individual health among early farmers, complicating Kovacs's claim that well-being improved, without denying the societal developments she lists. The choice beginning "By arguing that the…" disputes what Text 2 doesn't address, and the choice beginning "By agreeing that early…" reverses it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h421$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many writing teachers advise students to avoid the passive voice, as in "The experiment was conducted," arguing that active constructions, such as "We conducted the experiment," are clearer and more direct.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Robert Halle notes that the passive voice has legitimate uses. It allows writers to focus on the recipient of an action, to omit an unknown or unimportant actor, and to keep the topic of a paragraph in a consistent position. In scientific writing, he argues, the passive often reflects a sensible emphasis on procedures rather than on the researchers.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Halle (Text 2) most likely respond to the teachers' advice in Text 1?$q$, $q$["He would consider it too sweeping, since the passive voice is sometimes the better choice.","He would argue that active constructions are generally less clear than passive ones.","He would agree that the passive voice should always be avoided in scientific writing, where clarity matters most.","He would argue that the passive voice should be used in all forms of writing."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Halle says the passive has legitimate uses, so a blanket rule to avoid it goes too far. He doesn't call active voice generally less clear or recommend the passive everywhere, and he defends it in scientific writing.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h422$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Marine biologist Hana Sato argues that coral reefs can adapt to warming oceans. Some corals, she notes, survived recent heat waves that killed their neighbors, and these survivors may pass heat tolerance to their offspring.<br><br>Text 2<br>Ecologist Diego Ruiz agrees that some corals are more heat-tolerant than others. But he points out that corals reproduce slowly and that heat waves are now occurring every few years. Adaptation, he argues, is unlikely to keep pace unless warming slows.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Sato (Text 1) and Ruiz (Text 2) would most likely agree that$q$, $q$["coral reefs will adapt quickly enough to survive current rates of warming.","corals reproduce quickly enough to adapt to rising temperatures.","some corals are better able to withstand heat than others.","heat waves have become less frequent in recent years."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Sato cites survivors of heat waves, and Ruiz agrees that some corals are more heat-tolerant. They disagree about whether adaptation will keep pace. Ruiz contradicts the choices beginning "coral reefs will adapt…" and "corals reproduce quickly enough…", and the choice beginning "heat waves have become…" contradicts his point about frequent heat waves.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h423$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Tech analyst Mira Jensen argues that artificial intelligence tools will eliminate most jobs in translation within a decade. Machine translation, she notes, has improved so rapidly that it now produces acceptable results for most everyday texts at almost no cost.<br><br>Text 2<br>Translator Kemal Arslan notes that demand for human translators has actually grown in recent years. Clients increasingly use machine translation for routine material, he explains, but then hire people to revise the output and to handle legal, literary, and marketing texts where nuance and accountability matter.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Arslan (Text 2) most likely respond to Jensen's prediction (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that machine translation has not improved in quality in recent years, despite claims about its rapid progress.","He would agree that demand for human translators has fallen sharply in recent years.","He would argue that clients no longer use machine translation for routine material.","He would argue that machine translation is changing translators' work rather than simply eliminating it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Arslan describes translators revising machine output and handling specialized texts, a shift in the work rather than its disappearance. He doesn't deny machine translation's improvement, and he says demand has grown and clients do use it.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h424$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>For much of the twentieth century, scholars dismissed the Kingdom of Kush, located in what is now Sudan, as a mere imitator of ancient Egypt, pointing to its pyramids and its adoption of Egyptian gods.<br><br>Text 2<br>Archaeologist Nadia Osman argues that Kush was an innovator in its own right. Its capital at Meroë was a major center of iron production, its writing system, Meroitic, was developed independently, and Kushite rulers even conquered Egypt in the eighth century BCE, ruling it for nearly a century.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Osman (Text 2) most likely respond to the view described in Text 1?$q$, $q$["She would argue that it overlooks Kush's distinctive achievements and its power relative to Egypt.","She would argue that Kush did not build pyramids or worship any Egyptian gods.","She would argue that Egypt imitated Kush in building pyramids and worshipping its gods, reversing the direction of influence.","She would agree that Kush borrowed nearly all of its culture from Egypt."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Osman's evidence of independent writing, iron production, and conquest of Egypt counters the "mere imitator" view. She doesn't deny the pyramids or Egyptian gods, and she doesn't claim Egypt imitated Kush.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h425$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Advocates of four-day school weeks argue that the schedule saves districts money on transportation and utilities and that students, with a long weekend to rest, return to class more focused.<br><br>Text 2<br>A study of rural districts that adopted four-day weeks found that cost savings averaged only about 1 to 2 percent of budgets. Students' math scores grew slightly more slowly than in comparable five-day districts, though attendance improved modestly.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the advocates' claims in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By suggesting that the benefits they claim are smaller or less clear-cut than they imply","By arguing that four-day weeks reduce student attendance significantly","By confirming that students in four-day districts learn more quickly in math","By arguing that four-day weeks actually cost districts more money than five-day weeks and lower attendance"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 2 finds small savings and slower math growth, though attendance improved, so the claimed benefits are modest and mixed. Text 2 shows savings, not higher costs, and it reports slower math growth and better attendance.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h426$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Psychologist Anders Lund argues that boredom is purely harmful. Bored people, his surveys show, are more likely to make impulsive purchases, overeat, and take unnecessary risks.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Bea Moreno found that participants who first completed a dull task, copying numbers from a phone book, later generated more creative uses for everyday objects than participants who had not been bored. Moreno suggests that boredom may prompt the mind to seek novelty.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Moreno (Text 2) most likely respond to Lund's view (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that dull tasks reduce people's ability to think creatively.","She would agree that boredom has no positive effects on thinking.","She would argue that it overlooks a possible benefit of boredom.","She would argue that bored people are actually less likely to take unnecessary risks."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Moreno's finding that boredom boosted creativity points to a benefit that Lund's "purely harmful" view ignores. She doesn't dispute his risk findings, and the choices beginning "She would agree that…" and "She would argue that dull…" contradict her results.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h427$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Grace Obi argues that the 1863 Emancipation Proclamation was largely symbolic. It applied only to Confederate states, where the Union had no power to enforce it, and exempted slaveholding states loyal to the Union.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Samuel Price acknowledges the proclamation's legal limits but emphasizes its practical effects. It authorized the enlistment of Black soldiers, nearly 180,000 of whom served in the Union army, and it turned the advancing Union army into an army of liberation, freeing enslaved people in each territory it captured.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how Obi (Text 1) and Price (Text 2) view the Emancipation Proclamation?$q$, $q$["Obi focuses on its effect on Black soldiers, while Price focuses on its effect on loyal states.","Obi argues that it freed most enslaved people immediately, while Price argues that it freed none.","Obi emphasizes its limited legal reach, while Price emphasizes its concrete consequences.","Obi believes it was unconstitutional, while Price believes it was fully enforceable."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Obi stresses that the proclamation couldn't be enforced where it applied, while Price stresses enlistment and liberation as the army advanced. Neither makes the claims in the choices beginning "Obi argues that it…" and "Obi believes it was…", and the choice beginning "Obi focuses on its…" reverses their focuses.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h428$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some nutrition experts recommend eating breakfast every day, citing studies showing that people who skip breakfast tend to weigh more than those who eat it.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Tomasz Wolski conducted a trial in which participants were randomly assigned either to eat breakfast or to skip it for sixteen weeks. Weight change did not differ significantly between the groups. Wolski notes that observational studies cannot rule out the possibility that people who eat breakfast differ in other ways, such as exercise or sleep habits.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Wolski (Text 2) most likely respond to the experts' recommendation (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would agree that eating breakfast leads to lower body weight.","He would argue that skipping breakfast causes people to lose weight.","He would argue that the studies they cite do not show that eating breakfast itself affects weight.","He would argue that people who eat breakfast exercise less than those who skip it, which explains why they weigh more."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Wolski's randomized trial found no difference, and he notes that observational studies can't separate breakfast from other habits. He doesn't claim skipping breakfast causes weight loss, and he only speculates that other habits may differ, without specifying how.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h429$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Architect Nadia Rahim argues that cities should preserve historic buildings whenever possible. Older structures, she says, give neighborhoods character and a sense of continuity that new construction cannot replicate.<br><br>Text 2<br>Urban planner Leo Grant agrees that historic buildings have value, but he notes that strict preservation rules in some cities have blocked new housing in neighborhoods where it is badly needed, driving up rents. Grant proposes protecting the most significant buildings while allowing more flexibility elsewhere.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Grant (Text 2) most likely respond to Rahim's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that her approach, applied broadly, can have costs that justify a more selective policy.","He would argue that historic buildings contribute nothing to a neighborhood's character.","He would agree that every historic building should be preserved regardless of housing needs.","He would argue that new construction gives neighborhoods more character than old buildings do, so preservation rules should be eliminated."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Grant values historic buildings but warns that broad preservation can block housing, and he proposes selective protection. He doesn't dismiss their value, and he doesn't make the comparative claim in the choice beginning "He would argue that new…".$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h430$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Biologist Yuki Tanaka argues that the songs of humpback whales serve mainly to attract females. Only males sing, she notes, and singing peaks during the breeding season.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Omar Bakr observed that female humpbacks rarely approach singing males. Instead, singers are most often joined by other males, sometimes leading to brief contests. Bakr suggests that songs may function primarily in interactions among males.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Bakr (Text 2) most likely respond to Tanaka's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that humpback songs have no connection to the breeding season.","He would argue that observed behavior around singers does not fit well with her proposed function.","He would argue that female humpbacks also sing during the breeding season.","He would agree that songs attract females, since females often approach singing males and remain with them through the breeding season."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Bakr observed that females rarely approach singers while males often do, which undercuts the mate-attraction idea. He doesn't claim females sing or deny the link to breeding season, and the choice beginning "He would agree that…" contradicts his observation.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h431$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Poet and critic Lena Ward argues that poetry should be read aloud. Its music, she says—rhythm, rhyme, and the sounds of words—is essential to its meaning and is lost when poems are read silently.<br><br>Text 2<br>Critic Haruto Mori agrees that sound is important in much poetry, but he notes that many modern poets arrange words visually on the page, using line breaks, spacing, and even shapes that cannot be conveyed by speaking. For such poems, Mori argues, silent reading may reveal meanings that a performance would obscure.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mori (Text 2) most likely respond to Ward's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that it does not apply equally well to all poems.","He would argue that rhyme is more important than rhythm in modern poetry.","He would agree that all poetry should be read aloud to be understood.","He would argue that sound plays no role in the meaning of poetry."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Mori accepts that sound matters in much poetry but says visually arranged poems may be better read silently, so Ward's claim doesn't hold for all poems. He doesn't deny sound's role or rank rhyme and rhythm.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h432$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>A 2019 study found that people who spent at least two hours per week in nature reported better health and well-being than those who spent less time outdoors. The authors recommend that doctors encourage patients to spend regular time in green spaces.<br><br>Text 2<br>Public health researcher Ama Boateng praises the study but notes that it relied on participants' own reports of their health and time outdoors. People who already feel healthy, she points out, may be more inclined to spend time in nature, so the direction of the relationship is uncertain.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Boateng (Text 2) most likely respond to the authors' recommendation (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that the study shows conclusively that time in nature improves health, since it surveyed so many participants.","She would argue that participants spent much more time outdoors than they reported.","She would argue that spending time in nature harms people's health.","She would caution that the study's findings may not show that time in nature causes better health."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Boateng raises the possibility of reverse causation and notes the reliance on self-reports, which questions whether nature causes the health benefit. She doesn't call the evidence conclusive, claim harm, or claim underreporting.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h433$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Economists often assume that people value a dollar saved the same as a dollar earned. On this view, a shopper should be equally willing to drive across town to save $10 on a $20 item or on a $1,000 item.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky found that people were much more willing to make a trip to save $5 on a $15 purchase than to save $5 on a $125 purchase. People, they concluded, evaluate savings relative to the price of an item rather than in absolute terms.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kahneman and Tversky (Text 2) most likely respond to the assumption in Text 1?$q$, $q$["They would argue that people are more willing to travel to save money on expensive items.","They would argue that people value a dollar saved more than a dollar earned in all situations.","They would argue that their findings show people do not actually behave as the assumption predicts.","They would agree that people are equally willing to travel to save the same amount on any item."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Their finding shows people treat the same $5 savings differently depending on the price, contradicting the assumption. They don't make the second claim, and the choice beginning "They would argue that people are…" reverses their result.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h434$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Geologist Ruth Fischer argues that the giant stone circles at Nabta Playa in Egypt, built around 7,000 years ago, served as a calendar. Some stones, she notes, align with the position of the rising sun at the summer solstice, which coincided with the arrival of seasonal rains.<br><br>Text 2<br>Archaeologist Kwasi Adu notes that only a few of the many stones at the site align with astronomical events, and that with so many stones, some alignments would occur by chance. Adu does not rule out an astronomical purpose but argues that the evidence is weaker than often claimed.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Adu (Text 2) most likely respond to Fischer's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would consider it possible but insufficiently supported by the alignments she cites.","He would argue that the site was built long after the seasonal rains ended.","He would agree that the alignments prove the stones were used as a calendar.","He would argue that none of the stones at Nabta Playa align with astronomical events."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adu doesn't rule out Fischer's idea but argues that a few alignments among many stones could be chance. He accepts that some stones align, says nothing about timing, and doesn't think the alignments prove her claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h435$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some sports scientists recommend that young athletes specialize in a single sport early, arguing that thousands of hours of focused practice are needed to reach elite levels.<br><br>Text 2<br>A study of more than 1,500 elite athletes found that most played multiple sports in childhood and specialized only in their mid-teens. Early specialists were more likely to suffer overuse injuries and to quit their sport before adulthood.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the recommendation in Text 1?$q$, $q$["By agreeing that early specialization is the most reliable path to elite performance","By presenting evidence that early specialization may be unnecessary for elite success and may carry risks","By arguing that practice has no effect on athletic performance","By arguing that athletes who play multiple sports as children are more likely to suffer overuse injuries than early specialists are"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Text 2 shows that most elite athletes specialized late and that early specialists faced more injuries and dropouts. It doesn't claim practice is irrelevant, and the choice beginning "By arguing that athletes…" reverses its finding.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h436$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Literary scholar Inés Calderón reads Emily Dickinson's frequent dashes as signs of hesitation and uncertainty, moments where the speaker pauses, unable to finish a thought.<br><br>Text 2<br>Scholar Paul Okonkwo argues that Dickinson's dashes are not hesitations but connections. By refusing to separate her phrases with periods or commas, Okonkwo contends, Dickinson links ideas that conventional punctuation would divide, inviting readers to see relationships among them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a difference in how Calderón (Text 1) and Okonkwo (Text 2) interpret Dickinson's dashes?$q$, $q$["Calderón sees them as errors, while Okonkwo sees them as deliberate artistic choices.","Calderón sees them as interruptions in thought, while Okonkwo sees them as ways of joining ideas.","Calderón sees them as unimportant to the poems' meaning, while Okonkwo sees them as the single key to understanding Dickinson's work.","Calderón sees them as connections, while Okonkwo sees them as signs of uncertainty."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Calderón reads the dashes as pauses or hesitations, while Okonkwo reads them as links between ideas. Neither calls them errors or unimportant, and the choice beginning "Calderón sees them as connections…" reverses the two views.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h437$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some engineers argue that self-driving cars will dramatically reduce traffic deaths, since more than 90 percent of crashes involve human error.<br><br>Text 2<br>Safety researcher Mara Ellis notes that the statistic about human error includes crashes in which a human was only partly at fault, and that automated systems introduce new kinds of errors, such as failing to recognize unusual objects. Ellis expects safety gains from automation but argues that their size is still highly uncertain.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Ellis (Text 2) most likely respond to the engineers' argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would suggest that it overstates the likely benefit by relying on a statistic that is easily misread.","She would argue that self-driving cars will make roads more dangerous than they are now, because of the new kinds of errors they introduce.","She would argue that human error plays almost no role in traffic crashes.","She would agree that self-driving cars will eliminate more than 90 percent of crashes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ellis says the 90 percent figure is misleading and that automation brings new errors. She expects gains but of uncertain size, so she would see the engineers' claim as overstated. She doesn't predict more danger or deny human error.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h438$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Historian Lucía Vega argues that the Silk Road's greatest impact was economic: it moved silk, spices, and precious metals across Eurasia, enriching merchants and the cities along its routes.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Ibrahim Saleh contends that the routes' most lasting legacy was the movement of ideas. Buddhism traveled from India to China along them, as did technologies such as papermaking, which moved westward from China to the Islamic world and eventually to Europe.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, Vega (Text 1) and Saleh (Text 2) would most likely agree that the Silk Road$q$, $q$["had little effect on the economies of the cities along its routes.","was used mainly for transporting religious texts.","had significant effects on the societies connected by its routes.","moved technologies only from west to east."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both historians describe major effects, economic for Vega and intellectual for Saleh, though they differ on which was greatest. The other choices contradict one or both texts.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h439$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Ecologist Freya Holm argues that planting trees on a massive scale is one of the most effective ways to fight climate change, since trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow.<br><br>Text 2<br>Scientist Rajesh Menon notes that planting trees in grasslands and savannas, which naturally have few trees, can destroy ecosystems that store large amounts of carbon in their soils. He also points out that young trees absorb relatively little carbon for decades. Menon supports protecting existing forests as a higher priority.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Menon (Text 2) most likely respond to Holm's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would caution that large-scale planting can have drawbacks and may not be the best priority.","He would argue that existing forests release more carbon than they absorb, so planting new trees is the only effective strategy.","He would argue that trees do not absorb carbon dioxide as they grow.","He would agree that trees should be planted in grasslands and savannas."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Menon points to harm to grasslands and slow carbon uptake by young trees, and he prioritizes protecting forests. He doesn't deny absorption, opposes planting in grasslands, and says nothing about forests releasing carbon.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h440$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many historians describe the Middle Ages in Europe as a period of scientific stagnation, when learning was confined to copying ancient texts.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Seb Falk points to medieval innovations such as mechanical clocks, eyeglasses, and the astrolabe-based instruments that monks used to track time and the stars. Medieval scholars, Falk argues, did not merely preserve ancient knowledge but extended it.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Falk (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' characterization in Text 1?$q$, $q$["He would argue that ancient scholars, rather than medieval ones, invented mechanical clocks, eyeglasses, and astrolabe-based instruments.","He would argue that medieval monks had no interest in astronomy.","He would argue that it ignores evidence of genuine medieval invention and scholarship.","He would agree that medieval scholars only copied ancient texts."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Falk cites medieval inventions to show scholars extended knowledge, which contradicts the stagnation view. The other choices contradict his examples or are unsupported.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h441$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Many teachers believe that students have distinct "learning styles"—visual, auditory, or kinesthetic—and that instruction matched to a student's style improves learning.<br><br>Text 2<br>Psychologist Harold Pashler and colleagues reviewed the research on learning styles. While many people express preferences for one mode of instruction, they found almost no well-designed studies showing that matching instruction to those preferences improved learning outcomes.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Pashler and colleagues (Text 2) most likely respond to the teachers' belief (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["They would say that while students may have preferences, matching instruction to them is not shown to help.","They would agree that matching instruction to students' learning styles reliably improves outcomes, as many teachers believe.","They would argue that students have no preferences about how they receive instruction.","They would argue that visual instruction is superior for all students."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The review acknowledges preferences but finds no good evidence that matching helps. It doesn't deny preferences, rejects the choice beginning "They would agree that…", and doesn't favor visual instruction.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h442$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Marine scientist Elise Moreau argues that deep-sea mining for metals such as cobalt and nickel should be banned. The seafloor ecosystems where these metals occur, she notes, are poorly understood, and damage from mining could take thousands of years to recover.<br><br>Text 2<br>Engineer Tomas Lindqvist acknowledges the ecological risks but notes that metals for electric-vehicle batteries must come from somewhere, and that land-based mines have also caused serious environmental and human harm. He argues for strict regulation of deep-sea mining rather than an outright ban.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Lindqvist (Text 2) most likely respond to Moreau's proposal (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would accept that deep-sea mining carries risks but argue that a ban ignores the costs of the alternatives.","He would argue that land-based mines cause no environmental harm.","He would agree that deep-sea mining should be banned completely.","He would argue that deep-sea mining poses no real risk to seafloor ecosystems, since land-based mines cause far more serious harm."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Lindqvist grants the risks but notes that land mining also does harm, so he favors regulation over a ban. The other choices contradict his acknowledgment of risks or his comments about land mines.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h443$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>In her study of letters written by nineteenth-century immigrants to the United States, historian Anja Novak emphasizes their optimism. Writers frequently urged relatives to join them, describing high wages and plentiful land.<br><br>Text 2<br>Historian Carlos Mena cautions that surviving immigrant letters may not represent immigrants' experiences as a whole. Letters urging relatives to emigrate were often saved and even published by shipping companies as advertising, while letters describing hardship were less likely to be preserved.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Mena (Text 2) most likely respond to Novak's emphasis on optimism (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would argue that nineteenth-century immigrants never described high wages in their letters.","He would suggest that the optimism may partly reflect which letters happened to survive.","He would argue that shipping companies wrote most of the letters themselves.","He would agree that the surviving letters accurately represent all immigrants' experiences."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Mena notes that hopeful letters were preferentially preserved, so the optimism in the record may be partly a product of selection. He doesn't deny that the letters describe wages, and he doesn't claim companies wrote the letters.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h444$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Neuroscientist Karin Holt argues that adults cannot grow new neurons in the hippocampus, a brain region involved in memory. Her team examined brain tissue from adults and found almost no young neurons.<br><br>Text 2<br>Neuroscientist Maura Boldrini's team, using different methods to preserve brain tissue, found thousands of young neurons in the hippocampi of adults, even in people in their seventies. Boldrini suggests that differences in how tissue is handled after death may explain the conflicting results.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Boldrini (Text 2) most likely respond to Holt's conclusion (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would agree that adults cannot grow new neurons in the hippocampus.","She would argue that new neurons are found only in people under the age of seventy, which explains why Holt found almost none.","She would argue that the hippocampus plays no role in memory.","She would suggest that Holt's findings may reflect methodological factors rather than an absence of new neurons."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Boldrini found many young neurons and suggests that tissue handling explains the conflict, implying Holt's result may be an artifact. She found neurons even in people in their seventies, and she doesn't dispute the hippocampus's role.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h445$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Critics of video games often argue that they encourage social isolation, as players spend hours alone in front of screens instead of interacting with others.<br><br>Text 2<br>Researcher Nicole Banks surveyed more than 2,000 teenagers and found that most of their gaming time was spent playing online with friends, often while talking over headsets. Many described games as a main way of staying in touch with friends who had moved away.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Banks (Text 2) most likely respond to the critics' argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that it doesn't reflect how many teenagers actually play video games.","She would argue that teenagers spend very little time playing video games.","She would argue that face-to-face interaction is less valuable than online interaction.","She would agree that video games lead most teenagers to become socially isolated."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Banks found gaming is largely social, which contradicts the isolation claim. She doesn't measure total time or rank types of interaction.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h446$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Linguist Peter Lowe argues that texting and social media are degrading young people's writing skills, pointing to the abbreviations and missing punctuation common in online messages.<br><br>Text 2<br>Linguist Gretchen McCulloch argues that internet writing is a distinct, informal register, much like casual speech. People who use abbreviations online, she notes, can still write formally when a situation calls for it, just as people who speak casually with friends can speak formally at a job interview.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would McCulloch (Text 2) most likely respond to Lowe's concern (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["She would argue that formal writing should include abbreviations used online.","She would agree that online writing has made many young people unable to write formally, though she considers the change harmless.","She would argue that informal online writing does not necessarily indicate a loss of formal writing ability.","She would argue that young people rarely use abbreviations in online messages."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$McCulloch treats online writing as a separate register that coexists with formal skills, which counters Lowe's view of degradation. She doesn't agree with him, doesn't recommend abbreviations in formal writing, and doesn't deny their use.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h447$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Some agricultural scientists argue that organic farming is better for the environment than conventional farming, since it avoids synthetic pesticides and fertilizers that can pollute soil and water.<br><br>Text 2<br>Agronomist Lars Peters notes that organic farms typically produce 20 to 25 percent less food per acre than conventional farms. Feeding the same population organically, he argues, could require clearing more land, which would destroy habitat and release stored carbon—possibly outweighing organic farming's local benefits.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Peters (Text 2) most likely respond to the scientists' argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would agree that organic farming is clearly better for the environment overall.","He would argue that it may overlook an environmental cost that arises from organic farming's lower yields.","He would argue that organic farms actually produce more food per acre than conventional farms, so no additional land would need to be cleared.","He would argue that synthetic pesticides cause no pollution of soil or water."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Peters points to lower yields leading to more land clearing, a cost the scientists' argument overlooks. He doesn't deny pesticide pollution, doesn't fully agree, and states the opposite of the choice beginning "He would argue that organic…".$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h448$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1<br>Art critic Renée Dubois argues that the value of a painting lies entirely in what viewers can see. Knowing who made it, when, or why, she contends, should not affect our judgment of its beauty.<br><br>Text 2<br>Philosopher Arthur Kemp points out that when a celebrated painting was revealed to be a forgery, the image itself did not change, yet critics' assessment of it collapsed. Kemp argues that our appreciation of art inevitably depends on knowing how it was made, including the skill and originality it represents.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Kemp (Text 2) most likely respond to Dubois's argument (Text 1)?$q$, $q$["He would agree that viewers should judge paintings only on what they can see, since the forged image itself did not change.","He would argue that the forged painting was more beautiful than the original.","He would argue that critics are generally unable to recognize forgeries.","He would argue that the forgery case shows that knowledge of a work's origins does shape how it is judged."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Kemp uses the collapsed assessment of an unchanged image to show that origins matter to appreciation, which challenges Dubois. He doesn't rank the forgery's beauty or generalize about critics' ability to detect forgeries.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h449$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1950s, many doctors prescribed bed rest for patients recovering from heart attacks, sometimes for six weeks or more. <u>Cardiologist Samuel Levine challenged this practice, seating patients in armchairs within days and finding that they recovered as well as or better than those kept in bed.</u> Today, early movement is a standard part of cardiac care.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that patients kept in bed for six weeks recovered faster than others.","It describes a challenge to an established practice that foreshadows a later change in standard care.","It describes a practice that is still recommended for patients recovering from heart attacks.","It explains why doctors in the 1950s believed that long periods of bed rest helped heart patients heal fully."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Levine's armchair approach challenged bed rest, and the final sentence shows that early movement became standard. The sentence doesn't explain doctors' reasoning, and the choices beginning "It presents evidence that…" and "It describes a practice…" reverse its findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h450$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1911 novel. Mrs. Abbot is speaking to her son.<br><br>"Of course you must do as you think best," she said, folding her napkin into a smaller and smaller square. "I wouldn't dream of standing in your way. It's only that your father always said the city ruins young men. But you're grown now, and I'm sure you know better than your father did."$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of Mrs. Abbot's statement in the text?$q$, $q$["It expresses disapproval of her son's plans while appearing to accept them.","It shows that she has no strong opinion about where her son chooses to live.","It indicates that she agrees with her son that his father's views were mistaken.","It reveals that she plans to move to the city with her son."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$She claims she won't stand in his way but invokes his father's warning and adds a pointed remark, which signals disapproval behind apparent acceptance. Her fidgeting with the napkin reinforces the tension. The other choices take her words at face value or invent a plan.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h451$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have long debated whether Neanderthals created art. Paintings in three Spanish caves, including a ladder-like red design and hand stencils, have been dated using uranium in the mineral crusts that formed over them. <u>The dates indicate that the paintings are at least 64,000 years old, about 20,000 years before modern humans arrived in Europe.</u> If the dating is correct, Neanderthals must have made them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the subject matter of the paintings found in three Spanish caves.","It presents evidence that modern humans arrived in Europe much earlier than previously believed, about 64,000 years ago.","It explains the method that researchers used to date the paintings in the Spanish caves.","It presents a result that, if accurate, supports a particular answer to the question raised in the first sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The dates precede modern humans' arrival, which leads to the conclusion that Neanderthals made the art. The dating method is described in the previous sentence, and the sentence implies nothing about earlier arrival.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h452$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most ant species forage individually, with each worker searching on its own and recruiting others only after finding food. Army ants, by contrast, hunt in massive coordinated raids of up to several hundred thousand workers that sweep across the forest floor. <u>So effective are these raids that many other animals, including antbirds and certain flies, follow the swarms to catch the insects fleeing from them.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that army ants depend on antbirds to locate their prey.","It illustrates the impact of army ant raids by describing how other species exploit them.","It explains how army ant workers recruit hundreds of thousands of other workers to join a raid after one of them finds food.","It provides an example of an ant species that forages individually."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Other animals following the raids shows how powerful the raids are. The sentence doesn't describe recruitment or individual foraging, and the dependency runs the other way: birds rely on ants.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h453$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, many New Yorkers doubted that it was safe. A week after the opening, a rumor that the bridge was collapsing caused a stampede that killed twelve people. <u>The following year, showman P. T. Barnum led twenty-one elephants across the bridge, a spectacle widely reported as proof of its strength.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that the bridge was later damaged by the weight of the elephants.","It explains why a rumor that the bridge was collapsing caused a deadly stampede a week after the bridge opened to the public.","It provides evidence that engineers had underestimated the bridge's strength.","It describes an event that addressed the public doubts mentioned earlier in the text."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Barnum's elephant parade was presented as proof of strength, answering the doubts and fears described earlier. It doesn't explain the stampede, imply damage, or concern engineers' estimates.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h454$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that photographs taken from airplanes or satellites are recent tools for archaeology. In fact, archaeologists began using aerial photography in the 1910s, when pilots flying over England noticed patterns in crop fields: <u>plants growing over buried walls were shorter and ripened earlier, revealing the outlines of ancient buildings invisible from the ground.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how early aerial observers were able to detect structures beneath the ground.","It describes the kinds of crops most commonly grown in England in the 1910s.","It suggests that buried walls cause crops to grow taller than surrounding plants.","It presents evidence that aerial photography is a recent development in archaeology, first used only after satellites became available."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The portion explains the crop patterns that revealed buried walls. It doesn't support the "recent" assumption the text rejects, isn't about crop types, and the choice beginning "It suggests that buried…" reverses "shorter."$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h455$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Kiri Ngata studies the kea, a parrot native to New Zealand's mountains. Keas are known for their curiosity, sometimes dismantling car parts in parking lots. <u>In experiments, Ngata found that keas could use probabilities to make choices, picking from a jar with more of their preferred tokens even when the jar was larger overall.</u> This ability was previously documented only in humans and great apes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides an example of the kea's habit of dismantling car parts in parking lots.","It suggests that keas are more intelligent than humans and great apes.","It explains why keas prefer some kinds of tokens over others in experiments.","It presents a finding that the final sentence reveals to be unusual among animals."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The probabilistic reasoning finding is put in context by the final sentence: it was previously seen only in humans and apes. The sentence doesn't concern car parts or explain preferences, and the text makes no claim that keas are smarter than humans.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h456$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$For decades, the Great Pacific Garbage Patch was described as an island of plastic visible from space. In reality, much of the debris consists of tiny fragments spread through the water. <u>A 2018 survey using aircraft and trawling nets estimated that the patch contains about 80,000 metric tons of plastic, much of it in larger objects such as fishing nets, which accounted for nearly half of the total mass.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It adds quantitative detail that further complicates the popular image of the patch.","It suggests that the amount of plastic in the patch has decreased since 2018.","It explains why fishing nets break down more slowly than other kinds of plastic.","It confirms that the patch is a solid island of plastic visible from space."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The survey's estimate, including the large share made up of fishing nets, adds nuance beyond both the island image and the "tiny fragments" description. It doesn't confirm the island myth, explain breakdown rates, or describe a trend.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h457$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Painter Kerry James Marshall has said that when he visited museums as a young man, he saw almost no images of Black people in the paintings on display. <u>Since the 1980s, his large canvases have depicted Black figures in scenes of everyday life—barbecues, beauty salons, couples in parks—rendered in the grand scale traditionally reserved for history paintings.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why museums in the past collected few paintings depicting Black people and how that has changed since the 1980s.","It describes Marshall's work in a way that suggests a response to the absence he noticed in museums.","It suggests that Marshall's paintings have been rejected by major museums.","It provides examples of history paintings that Marshall saw as a young man."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Marshall's monumental paintings of Black life answer the absence he observed. The sentence doesn't explain museums' collecting, describe the paintings he saw, or discuss rejection.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h458$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Most of the water on Earth's surface is salt water. Desalination plants can convert seawater into drinking water, but the process uses large amounts of energy. <u>Engineers in Chile are now testing plants powered entirely by solar panels in the Atacama Desert, one of the sunniest places on Earth.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that most of Earth's surface water is salt water.","It explains how desalination plants convert seawater into drinking water and why the process requires so much energy.","It suggests that desalination requires less energy in sunny locations.","It describes an effort that may address a drawback mentioned in the previous sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Solar-powered plants could address the energy cost of desalination. The sentence doesn't explain the process or support the saltwater fact, and it doesn't say desalination itself uses less energy in the sun.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h459$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Psychologist Elena Rossi asked participants to estimate how long it would take them to complete various tasks, from writing a report to assembling furniture. Most underestimated, often by half. <u>Strikingly, participants underestimated even when they had completed similar tasks before and could recall how long those had taken.</u> Rossi suggests that people focus on the specific plan for a task and neglect their own past experience.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the kinds of tasks that participants were asked to estimate in the study.","It explains why participants overestimated the time needed for some tasks.","It presents a detail that makes the participants' errors more surprising and leads to Rossi's explanation.","It suggests that participants who had completed similar tasks before made more accurate estimates than participants who had not."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Underestimating despite relevant experience is surprising, and it supports Rossi's claim that people neglect past experience. The choice beginning "It suggests that participants…" reverses the finding, the tasks are described earlier, and the participants underestimated rather than overestimated.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h460$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In the 1960s, Jane Goodall reported that chimpanzees at Gombe, Tanzania, stripped leaves from twigs and used them to fish termites from mounds. <u>The finding startled scientists, many of whom defined humans as the only toolmakers.</u> Goodall's mentor, Louis Leakey, famously replied that scientists would now have to redefine "tool," redefine "man," or accept chimpanzees as humans.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that other scientists had already observed chimpanzees making tools before Goodall reported her findings at Gombe.","It describes the method Goodall used to observe chimpanzees at Gombe.","It explains why Goodall's observation challenged a prevailing view of what distinguishes humans.","It presents evidence that chimpanzees use tools more skillfully than humans do."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence shows that the finding was startling because it contradicted the definition of humans as the only toolmakers, which sets up Leakey's remark. It doesn't describe methods, prior observations, or relative skill.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h461$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Hydrologist Priya Raman studies "atmospheric rivers," long, narrow bands of water vapor that carry moisture from the tropics. A single atmospheric river can transport more water than the Amazon River. <u>These storms supply up to half of California's annual precipitation, refilling reservoirs after droughts.</u> But the strongest ones can also cause catastrophic floods, a danger Raman expects to grow as the climate warms.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that atmospheric rivers have become less common in California as the climate has warmed over recent decades.","It compares the size of atmospheric rivers with that of the Amazon River.","It establishes a benefit of atmospheric rivers before the text turns to the danger they pose.","It explains how atmospheric rivers form over tropical oceans."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence describes how atmospheric rivers help California, and "But" then introduces flooding risk. Formation and the Amazon comparison appear elsewhere, and nothing indicates declining frequency.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h462$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1929 novel.<br><br>The village had one street, and on it stood the post office, the church, the general store, and Mrs. Gale's house. It was generally agreed that of these four, Mrs. Gale's was the most reliable source of news.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that Mrs. Gale worked at the general store and spoke with all its customers.","It introduces a note of humor by suggesting that Mrs. Gale is the village's chief gossip.","It explains why the post office was the most important building in the village.","It indicates that the village church was rarely attended by the villagers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ranking Mrs. Gale's house above the post office and other institutions as a source of news humorously implies that she gossips. Nothing suggests the post office was most important, that she worked at the store, or that the church was unattended.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h463$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Hamid Rezaei studied why some neighborhoods recovered faster than others after a severe 1995 heat wave in Chicago. Neighborhoods with similar poverty rates had very different death rates. <u>Rezaei found that neighborhoods with busy sidewalks, local shops, and active community groups had far fewer deaths, since neighbors were more likely to check on elderly residents.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It describes the weather conditions that caused the heat wave in Chicago.","It identifies a factor that helps explain the difference described in the previous sentence.","It provides evidence that poverty was the main cause of deaths during the heat wave.","It suggests that elderly residents were less affected by the heat wave than younger ones."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Social connection explains why neighborhoods with similar poverty had different death rates. The previous sentence rules out poverty as the explanation, and the sentence says nothing about weather or age differences in risk.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h464$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Classical music recordings once fit on a single side of a vinyl record, limited to about twenty-two minutes. When the compact disc was developed in the late 1970s, engineers debated how much music it should hold. <u>According to a widely repeated story, an executive insisted that a disc hold all of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, which runs about seventy minutes.</u> The final standard of seventy-four minutes accommodated even slow performances of the work.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a popular account of how a particular requirement influenced a technical decision.","It explains how engineers developed the technology used in the compact disc.","It provides evidence that vinyl records could hold all of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony.","It suggests that the compact disc was designed mainly for recordings of popular music rather than for long classical works."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The story links the Ninth Symphony's length to the CD's capacity, which the final sentence confirms. Vinyl held only about twenty-two minutes per side, and the sentence doesn't explain the technology or claim the CD was designed for popular music.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h465$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have found that bilingual children often outperform monolingual children on tasks that require switching attention between rules. Some have concluded that speaking two languages strengthens general mental control. <u>However, large studies that carefully matched children by family income and education have often found the advantage shrinking or disappearing.</u> The question remains actively debated.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains why bilingual children outperform monolingual children on tasks that require switching attention between rules.","It confirms that bilingualism strengthens general mental control in all children.","It describes the tasks that researchers used to measure children's attention.","It presents evidence that casts doubt on the conclusion described in the previous sentence."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Matched studies weakening the advantage cast doubt on the conclusion that bilingualism strengthens control. The sentence doesn't confirm or explain the advantage, and the tasks are described earlier.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h466$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Many people picture the Sahara as an endless sea of sand dunes. Yet dunes cover only about a quarter of the desert. <u>Much of the rest consists of gravel plains, rocky plateaus, dry valleys, and mountains, some of which rise more than 3,000 meters and occasionally receive snow.</u>$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides evidence that the Sahara's mountains receive more snow than rain each year, unlike the rest of the desert.","It supports the correction of a common image by describing the desert's other landscapes.","It suggests that the Sahara's mountains are covered by sand dunes.","It explains why sand dunes cover only about a quarter of the Sahara."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Describing gravel plains, plateaus, and mountains supports the point that the Sahara isn't mostly dunes. The sentence doesn't explain why, "occasionally" doesn't support the snow claim, and the mountains are presented as separate from dunes.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h467$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$Economist Ines Moreau studies "food deserts," areas without nearby supermarkets. Policymakers have often assumed that opening a supermarket in such an area would improve residents' diets. <u>Moreau tracked purchases before and after new supermarkets opened in dozens of such neighborhoods and found that residents' diets changed very little.</u> She suggests that preferences and prices, not just distance, shape what people buy.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It confirms that opening supermarkets in food deserts substantially improves residents' diets.","It presents a finding that challenges an assumption and prompts Moreau's alternative explanation.","It describes how Moreau defined the term \"food desert\" in her research.","It explains why supermarkets have avoided opening stores in some neighborhoods."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The finding of little dietary change challenges policymakers' assumption and leads to Moreau's suggestion about preferences and prices. It doesn't confirm the assumption, explain store locations, or define the term.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-h468$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$In 1932, the physicist James Chadwick discovered the neutron, a particle with no electric charge. <u>Because it carries no charge, a neutron is not repelled by the positively charged nucleus of an atom and can penetrate it easily.</u> This property made the neutron an ideal tool for splitting atoms, a process that within a decade would lead to nuclear reactors.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It suggests that neutrons are attracted to the negatively charged electrons of an atom.","It describes the experiment that Chadwick used to discover the neutron in 1932.","It explains a property of the neutron that accounts for the later use described in the final sentence.","It presents evidence that the first nuclear reactors were developed before the neutron was discovered, using other particles to split atoms."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The neutron's lack of charge lets it penetrate nuclei, which explains why it was useful for splitting atoms. The sentence doesn't describe Chadwick's experiment, the choice beginning "It suggests that neutrons…" is unsupported, and the choice beginning "It presents evidence that…" reverses the chronology.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was closed for repairs for nearly a year. ______ commuters were forced to take a lengthy detour through downtown.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The detour was a consequence of the bridge closure, so a cause-and-effect transition like 'As a result' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The committee reviewed dozens of proposals. ______ only two met every funding requirement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ultimately,","Additionally,","Likewise,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence states the final outcome of the review process, which 'Ultimately' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Many assumed the small bookstore would close within a year of the chain store's arrival. ______ it has since expanded to two more locations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bookstore expanding contradicts the expectation that it would close, so a contrast word like 'Instead' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The lecture covered the causes of the recession in great detail. ______ it left many questions about the recovery unanswered.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Therefore,","For example,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second clause presents a limitation of the lecture, contrasting with the thoroughness described first, so 'However' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The new policy reduced paperwork for small businesses. ______ it introduced a separate reporting requirement that many owners found equally burdensome.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["At the same time,","As a result,","For instance,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence describes a simultaneous downside alongside the benefit mentioned first, which 'At the same time' captures.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Researchers first hypothesized that the fish avoided the reef due to pollution. ______ water samples showed pollution levels well within normal range.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","Specifically,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The water sample results undercut the original hypothesis, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The festival relies entirely on volunteer labor. ______ it manages to attract over 10,000 attendees each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Attracting a large crowd despite relying only on volunteers is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The novel's first half follows a single narrator in painstaking detail. ______ the second half jumps between five different perspectives across two decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Similarly,","As a result,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A single narrator versus five shifting perspectives describes two structurally opposite halves, so 'By contrast' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city increased funding for public transit by 20%. ______ ridership grew by only 3% over the following year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A large funding increase producing only a small ridership gain is an unexpected, contrasting result, so 'Nonetheless' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The two researchers disagreed on nearly every methodological point. ______ they arrived at strikingly similar conclusions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Instead,","In contrast,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence states two separate true facts in tension—disagreement on method, agreement on conclusions—calling for a plain contrast word. 'Instead' would wrongly suggest the agreement replaced the disagreement, and 'In contrast' is reserved for comparing two distinct subjects side by side, not one pair's unexpected outcome, so 'Yet' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The company's earnings fell short of projections for the third straight quarter. ______ its stock price rose after the announcement, surprising analysts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Predictably,","Similarly,","As expected,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage itself notes the outcome 'surprised analysts,' so the transition should flag the unexpected result rather than call it predictable.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The trail is only two miles long. ______ its steep elevation gain makes it one of the park's most difficult hikes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A short trail that is still very difficult is a contrast in expectations, which 'Nevertheless' signals correctly.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Wright brothers made their first powered flight in 1903 near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.
• The flight lasted 12 seconds and covered 120 feet.
• Their success followed years of glider experiments.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the brevity of the historic flight relative to its later significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though it lasted only 12 seconds, the Wright brothers' 1903 flight near Kitty Hawk marked the dawn of powered aviation.","The Wright brothers experimented with gliders for several years before 1903.","Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, is known for its consistent winds.","The Wright brothers' flight covered a distance of 120 feet."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer combines the flight's short duration (12 seconds) with its outsized historical significance, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Amazon River discharges more water than the next seven largest rivers combined.
• It has no bridges crossing its main stem.
• Much of its basin is inaccessible by road.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the river's exceptional water volume. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Amazon River discharges more water than the next seven largest rivers combined.","The Amazon River has no bridges crossing its main stem.","Much of the Amazon basin cannot be reached by road.","The Amazon flows through several South American countries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly conveys the river's water volume, which is the specific goal stated in the prompt.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Marie Curie won Nobel Prizes in both Physics (1903) and Chemistry (1911).
• She is the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.
• She discovered the elements polonium and radium.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the uniqueness of Curie's achievement among Nobel laureates. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Marie Curie remains the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different scientific fields.","Marie Curie discovered the elements polonium and radium.","Marie Curie won her first Nobel Prize in 1903.","Nobel Prizes are awarded in several different categories."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer states the specific fact about Curie's singular distinction among laureates, matching the stated goal exactly.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Barrier Reef spans over 2,300 kilometers.
• It is visible from space.
• It comprises nearly 3,000 individual reefs.$q$, $q$The student wants to convey the reef's enormous scale using a single striking detail. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Great Barrier Reef is large enough to be visible from space.","The Great Barrier Reef is home to a wide variety of marine life.","Coral reefs face threats from rising ocean temperatures.","The reef is made up of nearly 3,000 separate smaller reefs."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Being visible from space is the single most striking detail conveying scale, better fulfilling the goal than the more technical detail about nearly 3,000 smaller reefs.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Esperanto was created in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof.
• It was designed to be an easy-to-learn universal second language.
• Today it has an estimated 2 million speakers worldwide.$q$, $q$The student wants to introduce Esperanto by explaining the purpose behind its creation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Esperanto was created in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof as an easy-to-learn universal second language.","Esperanto currently has an estimated 2 million speakers.","L. L. Zamenhof lived during the 19th century.","Many constructed languages have been invented over the centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer links the creator, date, and stated purpose (an easy-to-learn universal language), directly explaining why Esperanto was created.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses have three hearts.
• Two pump blood to the gills; one pumps blood to the rest of the body.
• The main heart stops beating when the octopus swims.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a surprising consequence of the octopus's unusual circulatory system. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because its main heart is the one that pumps blood to the rest of its body, that circulation briefly stops every time the octopus swims.","An octopus's main heart pumps blood to the rest of its body, while its other two hearts serve only the gills.","An octopus has three separate hearts to circulate its blood.","Two of an octopus's three hearts pump blood to its gills."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the main heart's specific role with the fact that it stops during swimming to state the surprising consequence; the others restate isolated notes or describe the hearts' division of labor without naming that consequence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.
• Its exact destruction date and cause remain debated by historians.
• It is believed to have held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize scholarly uncertainty about the library's demise. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Historians still debate exactly when and how the Library of Alexandria was destroyed.","The Library of Alexandria held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.","The library was among the largest in the ancient world.","Alexandria was founded by Alexander the Great."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly addresses the ongoing scholarly debate about the library's destruction, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Bamboo can grow up to 91 centimeters in a single day.
• It is technically classified as a grass, not a tree.
• Some species are used in construction due to their strength-to-weight ratio.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight bamboo's remarkable growth rate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some bamboo species can grow as much as 91 centimeters in a single day.","Bamboo is classified as a grass rather than a tree.","Bamboo's strength-to-weight ratio makes it useful in construction.","Bamboo grows in a variety of climates around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states a growth-rate statistic, which directly accomplishes the stated goal of highlighting bamboo's growth speed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets in the solar system.
• A day on Venus is longer than its year.
• Its surface temperature is hot enough to melt lead.$q$, $q$The student wants to introduce a fact about Venus's unusual relationship between its day length and year length. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A single day on Venus lasts longer than a full Venusian year.","Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets.","Venus's surface is hot enough to melt lead.","Venus is the second planet from the sun."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer is the only option that addresses the specific relationship between day length and year length named in the prompt.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Sahara Desert was once a lush, green savanna.
• This period, called the African Humid Period, ended roughly 5,000 years ago.
• Cave paintings in the region depict animals like giraffes and hippos.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific piece of evidence to support the claim that the Sahara was once green. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cave paintings in the region depict animals such as giraffes and hippos, species associated with wetter climates.","Cave paintings have been found throughout the Sahara region.","The Sahara Desert was once a lush, green savanna.","The African Humid Period ended roughly 5,000 years ago."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer cites concrete evidence—animals tied to wetter climates—for the claim; the vague mention of cave paintings omits that specific detail, and the other two choices merely restate the claim itself or an indirect date rather than supporting evidence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honey never spoils if stored properly.
• Archaeologists have found 3,000-year-old honey in Egyptian tombs that was still edible.
• Honey's low moisture content and acidity prevent bacterial growth.$q$, $q$The student wants to provide a concrete historical example that illustrates honey's exceptional shelf life. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Archaeologists have discovered 3,000-year-old honey in Egyptian tombs that remained edible.","Honey's low moisture content helps prevent bacterial growth.","Honey is produced by bees from flower nectar.","Egyptian tombs have been found to contain many preserved goods."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer gives the specific historical example (ancient Egyptian honey) requested by the prompt, rather than the general explanation about honey's low moisture content.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Eiffel Tower grows about 15 centimeters taller in summer.
• This is due to thermal expansion of the iron.
• It was originally intended to be a temporary structure.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Eiffel Tower's height changes seasonally. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Eiffel Tower's iron structure expands in warmer temperatures, making it about 15 centimeters taller in summer.","The Eiffel Tower was originally meant to be a temporary structure.","The Eiffel Tower is a famous landmark located in Paris.","Iron is a metal that is commonly used in construction."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the cause (thermal expansion) to the effect (a 15-centimeter height change), directly explaining the phenomenon.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sea otters hold hands while sleeping to keep from drifting apart.
• They wrap themselves in kelp for the same reason.
• Sea otters are a keystone species in kelp forest ecosystems.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a specific behavior otters use to stay together while resting. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sea otters often hold hands while sleeping so they don't drift apart from one another.","Sea otters are considered a keystone species in their ecosystem.","Kelp forests support a wide diversity of marine life.","Sea otters live primarily along the Pacific coast."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer directly describes the specific resting behavior (holding hands) requested by the prompt.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was closed for repairs for six months. ______ , traffic congestion in the downtown area noticeably worsened.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closure caused the congestion, so a result/consequence transition is needed: 'As a result.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses have three hearts.
• Two hearts pump blood to the gills.
• One heart pumps blood to the rest of the body.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify how many hearts octopuses have. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Octopuses have three hearts: two that pump blood to the gills and one that pumps blood to the rest of the body.","Octopuses are known for their unusual circulatory systems.","Blood is pumped differently in octopuses than in most animals.","Octopuses have gills that allow them to breathe underwater."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific number of hearts (three) and their functions, directly accomplishing the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The novelist rarely gives interviews. ______ whenever she does speak publicly, journalists scrutinize every word.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nevertheless,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her rarely speaking publicly is the cause of the intense scrutiny when she does — a consequence relationship, signaled by 'Consequently.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• City A implemented a bike-share program in 2015; ridership reached 2 million trips per year by 2020.
• City B implemented a similar program in 2016; ridership reached 800,000 trips per year by 2020.
• Both cities have similar populations and climates.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight a key difference in outcomes between the two cities' programs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite similar populations and climates, City A's bike-share program achieved far higher ridership than City B's by 2020.","Both cities launched bike-share programs in the mid-2010s.","City A and City B have similar populations and climates.","City B's program began one year after City A's."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer both highlights the ridership gap (2 million vs. 800,000) and uses the similar-conditions detail to show the difference isn't explained by population or climate.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Proponents argue that the new tax would primarily affect large corporations. ______ , an independent analysis found that over 60% of the tax's burden would ultimately fall on consumers through higher prices.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","In contrast,","Granted,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The analysis directly rebuts the proponents' claim with contradicting data, which calls for a plain contrast word. 'In contrast' is reserved for comparing two distinct, parallel subjects rather than correcting a single claim, and 'Granted' concedes a point before a main argument rather than introducing rebutting evidence, so 'However' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A city replaced its streetlights with LED bulbs in 2018.
• Energy costs for street lighting fell by 40% over the next two years.
• Some residents complained the new lights were too bright.$q$, $q$The student wants to acknowledge both a benefit and a drawback of the streetlight replacement. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although the LED streetlights cut energy costs by 40% within two years, some residents found them uncomfortably bright.","The city replaced its streetlights with LED bulbs in 2018.","Energy costs for street lighting fell significantly after 2018.","Some residents were unhappy with the new streetlights."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines both the benefit (lower energy costs) and the drawback (complaints about brightness) that the prompt asks for.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The committee reviewed dozens of proposals before selecting a finalist. ______ , the selected proposal still required several rounds of revision before approval.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Therefore,","For example,","Moreover,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite being chosen as the finalist, the proposal still needed revision — a concession relationship signaled by 'Even so.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets in the solar system.
• A day on Venus is longer than its year.
• Scientists are unsure exactly why Venus's rotation is reversed.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how unusual Venus's rotation is compared to other planets. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Unlike most planets in the solar system, Venus rotates in the opposite direction, a mystery scientists have yet to fully explain.","Venus is the second planet from the sun.","A day on Venus lasts longer than a Venusian year.","Scientists continue to study the planets in the solar system."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer directly emphasizes the unusual reversed rotation and its unexplained cause, matching the student's stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Dust and pollen gradually accumulate on solar panels over the summer. ______ their energy output can drop by more than 15% if the panels are never cleaned.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","In contrast,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in output is a direct consequence of the dust buildup described first, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries by different dynasties.
• Its total length, including all branches, exceeds 21,000 kilometers.
• Contrary to popular belief, it is not actually visible to the naked eye from the Moon.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct a common misconception about the Great Wall. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although often claimed to be visible from the Moon, the Great Wall cannot actually be seen from that distance with the naked eye.","The Great Wall was constructed over the course of many centuries.","The Great Wall's total length exceeds 21,000 kilometers, including all its branches.","Several different Chinese dynasties contributed to building the wall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the popular misconception about visibility from the Moon, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city commissioned dozens of murals to beautify its downtown, expecting the project to reduce vandalism. ______ reports of graffiti and property damage actually increased in the months following the murals' completion.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the murals were expected to reduce vandalism but vandalism rose instead, the clause names what actually happened in place of that expectation, which 'Instead' signals. 'Nonetheless' would suggest the increase occurred despite some obstacle rather than as the opposite of a prediction, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The platypus is one of only five species of mammals that lay eggs.
• Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their hind legs.
• The platypus uses electroreception to detect prey underwater.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how rare it is for a mammal to lay eggs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The platypus is one of just five mammal species that lay eggs instead of giving birth to live young.","Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their hind legs.","The platypus uses electroreception to detect prey underwater.","Mammals are typically distinguished from other animals by giving birth to live young."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific rarity of egg-laying among mammals, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The archaeological team spent three seasons excavating the site without finding anything of note. ______ in the final week of the dig, they unearthed a cache of coins dating back over a thousand years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Predictably,","For instance,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Three fruitless seasons followed by a sudden discovery is a surprising turn, which 'Unexpectedly' signals; the other choices wrongly suggest the outcome was predictable or a logical consequence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Satellite data shows Antarctica lost about 40 billion tons of ice per year in the 1980s.
• By the 2010s, the annual ice loss had risen to roughly 250 billion tons per year.
• Rising ocean temperatures are believed to be accelerating the melting of ice shelves from below.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate how dramatically the rate of ice loss has accelerated over time. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Antarctica's annual ice loss climbed from about 40 billion tons in the 1980s to roughly 250 billion tons by the 2010s.","Rising ocean temperatures are believed to be melting ice shelves from below.","Antarctica lost about 40 billion tons of ice per year in the 1980s.","By the 2010s, Antarctica's annual ice loss had risen to roughly 250 billion tons per year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer pairs both figures to show the scale of change over time; the others give the cause of melting or just one data point, neither of which demonstrates the acceleration by itself.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bakery only opened its doors six months ago. ______ it already has a loyal following that lines up before sunrise on weekends.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Having a devoted following despite being so new is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Johannes Gutenberg introduced the movable-type printing press in Europe around 1440.
• Before the press, books were copied by hand, a process that could take months.
• The printing press allowed identical copies of a text to be produced quickly and cheaply.$q$, $q$The student wants to contrast book production before and after the printing press. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Before Gutenberg's press, copying a single book by hand could take months, but afterward, identical copies could be produced quickly and cheaply.","Johannes Gutenberg introduced the movable-type printing press around 1440.","The printing press is considered one of the most important inventions in history.","Gutenberg's original printing press used movable metal type."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer is the only option that directly contrasts the slow, laborious hand-copying process with the speed and low cost enabled by the press.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Economists at the central bank predicted that inflation would ease significantly by the end of the year. ______ the most recent consumer price data showed inflation holding steady at nearly the same rate as six months earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Nonetheless,","By contrast,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Economists' prediction of easing inflation is replaced by the opposite outcome, calling for 'Instead.' 'Nonetheless' implies the steady rate persisted despite some obstacle rather than contradicting a forecast, and 'By contrast' would require two distinct things being compared side by side, not one prediction proven wrong, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Arctic tern migrates from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year.
• This round trip covers roughly 70,000 kilometers.
• Arctic terns can live for more than 30 years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extraordinary distance covered by the Arctic tern's migration. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Arctic tern's annual round-trip migration between the Arctic and Antarctic covers roughly 70,000 kilometers.","Arctic terns can live for more than 30 years.","The Arctic tern breeds in the Arctic and spends its winters in the Antarctic.","Many bird species undertake long migrations each year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific 70,000-kilometer distance, which is the detail needed to emphasize the migration's extraordinary length.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Public health officials initially projected that a new vaccine would be available to the general public within three months of approval. ______ manufacturing delays pushed the wider rollout back by nearly a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","As anticipated,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lengthy delay contradicts the original three-month projection, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Everest stands at 8,849 meters, the highest point above sea level on Earth.
• The mountain is officially shared by Nepal and the Tibet Autonomous Region of China.
• Climbers face extreme risks from low oxygen levels and unpredictable weather near the summit.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the specific elevation that makes Everest the world's tallest peak above sea level. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At 8,849 meters, Mount Everest is the highest point above sea level on Earth.","Mount Everest sits on the border between Nepal and the Tibet Autonomous Region of China.","Climbers attempting Everest face serious risks from low oxygen and severe weather.","Many mountaineers dream of one day climbing Mount Everest."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer gives the precise elevation figure that directly supports the claim about Everest's status as the tallest peak above sea level.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The region experienced its driest summer in decades. ______ the drought-tolerant garden that the Alvarez family planted last spring stayed green throughout the season.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The garden thriving despite the severe drought is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Rosetta Stone contains the same decree written in three scripts: hieroglyphic, Demotic, and Greek.
• It was discovered by French soldiers in Egypt in 1799.
• Jean-François Champollion used the Greek text to help decipher Egyptian hieroglyphics in 1822.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Rosetta Stone made it possible to decode hieroglyphics. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Rosetta Stone repeated the same decree in Greek alongside hieroglyphics, Champollion was able to use the Greek to decipher the hieroglyphic script in 1822.","The Rosetta Stone was discovered by French soldiers in Egypt in 1799.","The Rosetta Stone contains a decree written in three different scripts.","Ancient Egyptian hieroglyphics were used for thousands of years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the stone's three-script format to the specific mechanism by which Champollion deciphered hieroglyphics, fulfilling the goal of explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The factory's new automated assembly line was expected to eliminate most manual labor positions. ______ the company ended up hiring more workers to operate and maintain the new machinery.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Hiring more workers directly contradicts the expectation of eliminating jobs, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honeybees perform a "waggle dance" to communicate the location of food sources to other bees.
• The angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates the direction of the food relative to the sun.
• The duration of the waggle run indicates the distance to the food source.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain precisely how the waggle dance conveys both direction and distance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Through the waggle dance, bees signal a food source's direction by the angle of the dance relative to vertical and its distance by the duration of the waggle run.","Honeybees perform a waggle dance to communicate with other bees.","The angle of a bee's waggle dance relative to vertical indicates a food source's direction relative to the sun.","The duration of a bee's waggle run indicates how far away a food source is."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer specifies both mechanisms—angle for direction and duration for distance—together; the choice saying bees dance to communicate is too vague to explain the mechanism at all, and the choices about angle alone or duration alone each capture only one of the two required pieces.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The violinist injured her hand in a car accident, and doctors doubted she would ever perform again. ______ she returned to the stage eighteen months later to a standing ovation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Because of this,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her triumphant return contrasts with the doctors' doubts, a relationship best signaled by 'Yet.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Titanic was, at the time, the largest passenger ship ever built.
• It sank on its maiden voyage in April 1912 after striking an iceberg.
• The ship carried far fewer lifeboats than were needed for all passengers and crew.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why so many people died when the Titanic sank. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Titanic carried far fewer lifeboats than needed for everyone aboard, a large share of its passengers and crew had no way to escape.","The Titanic was the largest passenger ship built at the time.","The Titanic sank in April 1912 after striking an iceberg on its maiden voyage.","Shipbuilding technology advanced rapidly in the early twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer identifies the specific cause — insufficient lifeboats — that explains the high death toll, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$For decades, archaeologists maintained that the stone circle served primarily as a burial ground. ______ recent excavations of nearby postholes suggest it may have functioned as an astronomical calendar.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["More recently,","Subsequently,","Eventually,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The newer excavation evidence revises a decades-old belief, which requires a transition marking an updated, present-day view—'More recently.' 'Subsequently' merely marks later timing without signaling revision, and 'Eventually' implies a final outcome of a process rather than a shift in scholarly understanding, so 'More recently' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coast redwoods can grow taller than 350 feet, making them the tallest trees on Earth.
• Their thick, fire-resistant bark can be over a foot thick.
• Redwoods can live for more than 2,000 years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the exceptional height of redwood trees. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Coast redwoods, which can exceed 350 feet in height, are the tallest trees on Earth.","Redwood bark can grow more than a foot thick, helping the trees survive fires.","Some individual redwood trees are more than 2,000 years old.","Redwood forests are found primarily along the coast of California and southern Oregon."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific height figure that fulfills the goal of emphasizing the redwoods' exceptional height.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Forecasts called for heavy rain throughout the weekend of the town fair. ______ the skies cleared just in time for the opening ceremony on Saturday morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Fortunately,","Consequently,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clearing skies are a welcome, contrasting turn against the rainy forecast, which 'Fortunately' captures.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• School A introduced a peer-tutoring program in 2019; its average math scores rose from 68% to 79% by 2022.
• School B did not introduce the program; its average math scores rose from 67% to 70% over the same period.
• Both schools serve similar student populations and have comparable funding levels.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that the tutoring program likely contributed to the improvement in scores. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although School A and School B serve similar populations and have comparable funding, School A's scores rose from 68% to 79% after adopting peer tutoring, far outpacing School B's gain from 67% to 70%.","School A introduced a peer-tutoring program in 2019.","School B's average math scores rose slightly between 2019 and 2022.","Both schools serve similar student populations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the comparable-conditions detail with both schools' score changes to suggest the tutoring program, rather than other factors, drove School A's larger gain.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The author rewrote the opening chapter of her novel eleven times before submitting the manuscript. ______ reviewers later singled out that very chapter as the strongest part of the book.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ultimately,","Similarly,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The repeated revision finally paid off, an eventual outcome that 'Ultimately' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE, burying the Roman city of Pompeii in volcanic ash.
• The ash preserved buildings, artifacts, and even plaster casts of victims' final positions.
• Excavations of Pompeii began in the eighteenth century and continue today.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Pompeii is such a valuable source of information for historians. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because volcanic ash from the 79 CE eruption preserved Pompeii's buildings, artifacts, and even the final positions of its victims, the site offers historians an unusually detailed record of Roman life.","Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE, burying Pompeii in ash.","Excavations of Pompeii began in the eighteenth century and continue today.","Pompeii was a city in the Roman Empire."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains why the site is valuable — the ash's preservation of detailed evidence — directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$After three rounds of talks ended without agreement, both sides publicly stated that a deal was unlikely before the deadline. ______ a last-minute compromise on the disputed clause allowed the agreement to be signed just hours before the deadline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Admittedly,","Instead,","As predicted,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A deal being signed despite the stated pessimism is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nonetheless' signals. 'Admittedly' would need to introduce the conceded fact itself rather than follow it, 'Instead' implies the agreement replaced an expectation rather than occurring despite one, and 'As predicted' asserts the opposite of what happened, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Emperor penguins endure Antarctic winters with temperatures as low as -60°C.
• They huddle together in large groups, rotating positions so each bird spends time in the warmer center.
• This behavior can significantly reduce individual heat loss compared to standing alone.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the specific method penguins use to conserve heat as a group. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Emperor penguins huddle in large groups and rotate positions so that every bird gets a turn in the warmer center, significantly cutting individual heat loss.","Emperor penguins can endure Antarctic winter temperatures as low as -60°C.","Emperor penguins are one of the few animals that breed during the Antarctic winter.","Antarctica is the coldest continent on Earth."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct answer is the only option that specifies the rotating-huddle mechanism, directly answering how penguins conserve heat as a group.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Light pollution from the nearby city has increased steadily over the past decade. ______ the observatory's astronomers have had to travel farther into the desert to capture clear images of faint galaxies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Having to travel farther is a direct consequence of the rising light pollution, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Thomas Edison did not invent the first electric light bulb; earlier inventors had already created working versions.
• Edison's major contribution was developing a longer-lasting, commercially practical filament.
• His bulb, using a carbonized filament, could burn for over 1,200 hours.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Edison invented the light bulb. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Edison is widely credited with inventing the light bulb, earlier inventors had already built working versions before he improved the filament.","Edison's carbonized filament allowed his bulb to burn for over 1,200 hours.","Edison developed a longer-lasting, commercially practical filament.","Electric lighting transformed daily life in the late nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly addresses and corrects the common misconception about who invented the light bulb, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Vincent van Gogh created more than 2,000 artworks during his lifetime.
• He sold only one painting while he was alive.
• Today his paintings sell for tens of millions of dollars at auction.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the contrast between Van Gogh's lack of commercial success during his life and the value of his work today. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though Van Gogh sold only one painting during his lifetime, his works now sell for tens of millions of dollars at auction.","Van Gogh created more than 2,000 artworks over the course of his life.","Van Gogh is considered one of the most influential painters in Western art history.","Auction houses regularly sell paintings by major artists for high prices."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer pairs the fact that Van Gogh sold just one painting in his life with the enormous value of his work today, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Garrett Morgan patented an improved traffic signal in 1923.
• His design added a caution position between "stop" and "go."
• Before his invention, many intersections relied only on manually operated stop signs.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify what made Morgan's traffic signal design innovative. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Garrett Morgan's 1923 traffic signal introduced a caution position between the stop and go signals.","Garrett Morgan patented his traffic signal design in 1923.","Many intersections once relied on manually operated stop signs.","Traffic signals are used to manage the flow of vehicles at intersections."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names the specific innovation, the added caution position, that made Morgan's design new, directly meeting the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Naked mole rats can live more than 30 years, far longer than similarly sized rodents.
• They rarely develop cancer, a trait scientists are studying closely.
• Unlike most mammals, they cannot regulate their own body temperature well.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize a trait that makes naked mole rats especially valuable to medical researchers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Naked mole rats rarely develop cancer, a resistance that scientists are studying closely for its medical value.","Naked mole rats can live more than 30 years, unusually long for a rodent their size.","Unlike most mammals, naked mole rats cannot regulate their own body temperature well.","Naked mole rats are rodents that live in large underground colonies."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer ties the cancer resistance directly to ongoing scientific study, matching the goal of highlighting a trait valuable to researchers.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Malcom McLean introduced standardized steel shipping containers in 1956.
• Before containers, cargo was loaded and unloaded piece by piece, a slow process called "break bulk" shipping.
• Standardization allowed goods to move seamlessly between ships, trains, and trucks.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why standardized containers made global shipping more efficient. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because standardized containers could move seamlessly between ships, trains, and trucks, they replaced the slow, piece-by-piece loading of break bulk shipping.","Malcom McLean introduced standardized steel containers in 1956.","Cargo was once loaded and unloaded piece by piece in a process called break bulk shipping.","Shipping containers are typically made of steel."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer links the containers' interoperability to the inefficient system they replaced, directly explaining the efficiency gain.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Concorde could cross the Atlantic Ocean in under 3.5 hours.
• It was retired from commercial service in 2003.
• Tickets on the Concorde cost far more than tickets on standard flights.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how fast the Concorde could travel. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Concorde could cross the Atlantic Ocean in under three and a half hours.","The Concorde was retired from commercial service in 2003.","Tickets for the Concorde cost far more than standard airline tickets.","Air travel changed significantly during the twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific travel time that demonstrates the Concorde's exceptional speed, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Antikythera mechanism was recovered from a shipwreck near a Greek island in 1901.
• Modern imaging revealed more than 30 interlocking bronze gears inside it.
• Researchers believe it was used to predict astronomical positions and eclipses decades in advance.$q$, $q$The student wants to support the claim that the device was mechanically far ahead of its time. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["With more than 30 interlocking bronze gears, the Antikythera mechanism could predict astronomical positions and eclipses decades in advance.","The Antikythera mechanism was recovered from a shipwreck near a Greek island in 1901.","The device could predict astronomical positions and eclipses decades in advance.","The mechanism's gears were made of bronze."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer pairs the gear count with the device's predictive power to show just how mechanically advanced it was; the choice stating only the predictive capability omits the mechanical complexity, and the shipwreck recovery and bronze material are incidental details.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Tomatoes were brought to Europe from the Americas in the 16th century.
• Many Europeans believed tomatoes were poisonous because they belong to the nightshade family.
• Tomatoes did not become a common food in Europe until the 18th century.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Europeans were initially reluctant to eat tomatoes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because tomatoes belong to the nightshade family, many Europeans believed the fruit was poisonous when it first arrived from the Americas.","Tomatoes were brought to Europe from the Americas in the 16th century.","Tomatoes did not become a common food in Europe until the 18th century.","Nightshade plants are native to both the Americas and Europe."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific reason, the nightshade connection, that explains European reluctance to eat tomatoes.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Dead Sea Scrolls were discovered by a shepherd in caves near Qumran in 1947.
• The scrolls include some of the oldest known copies of Hebrew biblical texts.
• Scholars have spent decades translating and studying the roughly 900 manuscripts.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify who first discovered the Dead Sea Scrolls and where. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Dead Sea Scrolls were discovered by a shepherd in caves near Qumran in 1947.","The scrolls include some of the oldest known copies of Hebrew biblical texts.","Scholars have spent decades translating roughly 900 manuscripts.","Ancient texts can reveal a great deal about early religious practices."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names both who found the scrolls and where, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Certain plankton species emit light when disturbed by movement in the water.
• This glow is a defense mechanism meant to startle or attract predators of the plankton's predators.
• The phenomenon is sometimes visible as glowing blue waves at night along certain coastlines.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the biological purpose behind plankton's glowing light. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some plankton glow when disturbed as a defense mechanism, a flash meant to startle predators or attract the predators' own enemies.","Glowing plankton can sometimes be seen lighting up waves along certain coastlines at night.","Plankton are tiny organisms that live throughout the world's oceans.","Many marine organisms are capable of producing their own light."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the defensive purpose of the glow rather than merely describing when it is visible, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Beethoven began losing his hearing in his late twenties.
• By the time he composed his Ninth Symphony, he was almost completely deaf.
• He reportedly felt the vibrations of the orchestra through the floor to sense the music.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific detail to illustrate how Beethoven adapted to his hearing loss while composing. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though almost completely deaf by the time he composed his Ninth Symphony, Beethoven reportedly sensed the orchestra's vibrations through the floor.","Beethoven began losing his hearing in his late twenties.","By the time he composed his Ninth Symphony, Beethoven was almost completely deaf.","Beethoven reportedly sensed the orchestra's vibrations through the floor."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer links the near-total deafness to the specific adaptation of sensing vibrations through the floor; the choices stating only his deafness or only the vibration-sensing each give half the picture, neither fully illustrating how he adapted.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Greenland sharks are estimated to live more than 300 years, and possibly up to 500.
• Scientists determine their age by analyzing proteins in the lens of the eye.
• They grow extremely slowly, adding less than a centimeter in length per year.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the exceptional lifespan of the Greenland shark. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Greenland sharks are estimated to live more than 300 years, and possibly as long as 500.","Scientists determine a Greenland shark's age by analyzing proteins in its eye lens.","Greenland sharks grow less than a centimeter in length per year.","Sharks are found in oceans throughout the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific lifespan figures, directly accomplishing the goal of emphasizing the shark's exceptional longevity.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Swiss engineer George de Mestral invented Velcro in the 1940s.
• He got the idea after noticing burrs stuck to his dog's fur during a walk.
• Velcro consists of two strips, one with tiny hooks and one with tiny loops.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what inspired de Mestral to invent Velcro. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["George de Mestral was inspired to invent Velcro after noticing burrs stuck to his dog's fur during a walk.","Velcro consists of two strips, one with tiny hooks and one with tiny loops.","George de Mestral was a Swiss engineer working in the 1940s.","Many household inventions were developed by observing patterns in nature."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names the specific observation, burrs on his dog's fur, that inspired the invention, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Several major cities installed pneumatic tube networks in the early 1900s to speed up mail delivery.
• Capsules carrying letters were propelled through underground tubes by compressed air.
• The systems were largely abandoned by the mid-20th century as trucks became cheaper and more flexible.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why cities eventually stopped using pneumatic tube mail systems. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cities largely abandoned their pneumatic tube mail systems by the mid-20th century as trucks became a cheaper, more flexible alternative.","Pneumatic tube systems propelled mail capsules through underground tubes using compressed air.","Several major cities installed pneumatic tube networks in the early 1900s.","Mail delivery has changed considerably over the past century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific reason, cheaper and more flexible trucks, that explains why the tube systems were abandoned.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles between North America and central Mexico.
• No single butterfly completes the entire round trip; the journey takes three or four generations.
• Scientists still debate exactly how later generations know the route their ancestors traveled.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific detail to highlight what makes the monarch migration especially puzzling to scientists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because no single monarch completes the full 3,000-mile round trip, scientists are still unsure how butterflies several generations removed from their ancestors know which route to follow.","Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles between North America and central Mexico.","The monarch migration takes three or four generations to complete, since no single butterfly makes the full round trip.","Scientists still debate exactly how later generations know the route their ancestors traveled."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the multigenerational relay to the specific mystery of route transmission; the choice about the multigenerational trip states the relay without the mystery, and the choice about scientists' debate states the mystery without explaining why it's puzzling in the first place.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Construction of the Panama Canal was completed in 1914 after a French attempt failed decades earlier.
• Thousands of workers died from yellow fever and malaria during construction.
• Discovering that mosquitoes spread these diseases allowed engineers to control outbreaks through sanitation measures.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how disease control efforts allowed construction of the canal to succeed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Once engineers discovered that mosquitoes spread yellow fever and malaria, sanitation measures to control the insects helped construction of the canal finally succeed.","Construction of the Panama Canal was completed in 1914.","Thousands of workers died from yellow fever and malaria during construction.","An earlier French attempt to build the canal had failed decades before."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the mosquito discovery to the sanitation measures that allowed construction to succeed, directly meeting the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Researchers have used 3D printers to create simple tissue structures using living cells as "ink."
• Fully functional, transplantable organs have not yet been successfully bioprinted.
• Scientists hope the technology could eventually reduce reliance on organ donor waiting lists.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify the current limitation of bioprinting technology. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fully functional, transplantable organs have not yet been successfully created using bioprinting technology.","Researchers have used 3D printers to create simple tissue structures from living cells.","Scientists hope bioprinting could eventually reduce reliance on organ donor waiting lists.","3D printing has been applied to many different fields in recent years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the current limitation directly, that full organs cannot yet be bioprinted, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coffee cultivation spread from Ethiopia to the Arabian Peninsula by the 15th century.
• Coffeehouses became popular gathering places for discussion and business in the Ottoman Empire.
• European traders later introduced coffee cultivation to colonies in the Americas and Asia.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how coffeehouses contributed to the drink's cultural significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Coffeehouses in the Ottoman Empire became popular gathering places where people met to discuss ideas and conduct business.","Coffee cultivation spread from Ethiopia to the Arabian Peninsula by the 15th century.","European traders introduced coffee cultivation to colonies in the Americas and Asia.","Coffee is one of the most widely traded commodities in the world today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the cultural role of coffeehouses as gathering places, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• City A introduced mandatory curbside composting in 2017; its landfill waste fell by 25% by 2022.
• City B introduced a similar program in 2018 but made participation optional; its landfill waste fell by only 6% over the same period.
• Both cities have comparable populations and household sizes.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that mandatory participation was likely responsible for City A's greater reduction in landfill waste. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although City A and City B have comparable populations, City A's mandatory composting program cut landfill waste by 25% by 2022, far more than City B's optional program, which achieved only a 6% reduction.","Both City A and City B introduced curbside composting programs in the late 2010s.","City B made participation in its composting program optional.","City A and City B have similar populations and household sizes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the comparable-conditions detail with both cities' outcomes to suggest mandatory participation, not population differences, drove the gap.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, on October 4, 1957.
• The satellite was about the size of a beach ball and weighed 83.6 kilograms.
• Its launch triggered the United States to accelerate funding for science education and space research.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the effect Sputnik's launch had on the United States. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sputnik's launch prompted the United States to accelerate funding for science education and space research.","The Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1 on October 4, 1957.","Sputnik 1 was about the size of a beach ball and weighed 83.6 kilograms.","The Space Race was a period of intense competition between two nations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific effect on the United States, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A popular blender model was found to have a faulty switch that could overheat. ______ the manufacturer recalled more than 200,000 units within a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The recall was a direct consequence of the faulty switch, so a cause-and-effect transition, 'As a result,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The marathon runner tore a ligament in her ankle during the race's ninth mile. ______ she pushed through the pain and crossed the finish line under her goal time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Remarkably,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Finishing under her goal time despite a serious injury is a surprising outcome, which 'Remarkably' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Critics gave the newly opened restaurant lukewarm reviews in its first month. ______ within a year it was fully booked most weekends.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Becoming fully booked despite an unenthusiastic start is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The young chess player lost every match at his first major tournament, drawing ridicule from commentators who doubted his talent. ______ within two years he became the youngest grandmaster in his country's history.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Instead,","In contrast,","Because of this,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Becoming a grandmaster after a ridiculed start is a direct contrast between two facts about the same person, which plain 'Yet' signals. 'Instead' would wrongly suggest the early losses never happened, and 'In contrast' is reserved for comparing two separate subjects, not one person's turnaround, so 'Yet' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The museum spent two years digitizing its entire photo archive and posting it online for free public access. ______ website traffic increased more than tenfold within the first six months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The traffic increase directly resulted from the digitization effort, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The small mountain town had no access to high-speed internet for over a decade. ______ its residents relied on satellite connections that were slow and unreliable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Relying on slow satellite connections was a direct consequence of lacking high-speed internet, so 'As a result' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Meteorologists predicted an unusually mild winter for the region based on ocean temperature patterns. ______ the area experienced record snowfall and weeks of below-freezing temperatures.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The record snowfall contradicts the prediction of a mild winter, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$City officials initially assured taxpayers that the new library wing would be completed within its original budget. ______ internal documents later revealed that costs had exceeded projections by nearly 40 percent before construction even began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","However,","Instead,","As anticipated,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The internal documents don't just contrast with the officials' assurance—they expose the concealed truth behind it, which the intensifying transition 'In fact' best captures. Plain 'However' would only mark contrast without that revealing force, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the cost overrun replaced the assurance rather than exposing it as false, so 'In fact' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city council banned single-use plastic bags at all retail checkouts last year. ______ sales of reusable canvas totes at local stores have tripled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Nevertheless,","For instance,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in reusable tote sales is a direct result of the plastic bag ban, so 'Accordingly' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The neighborhood bakery closed its walk-in storefront and switched to online-only orders. ______ many longtime customers say they miss browsing the display case in person.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The customers' complaints contrast with the bakery's business decision, so 'However' correctly signals that contrast.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The startup's first product update was plagued by bugs and drew harsh criticism from early users. ______ after months of fixes, the app became one of the most downloaded productivity tools of the year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Eventually,","Similarly,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The app's rise to popularity after a rocky start describes a final outcome, which 'Eventually' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$After a test rocket exploded during a routine engine trial, engineers warned that a successful launch was likely years away. ______ the redesigned rocket completed a flawless orbital launch just eight months later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Contrary to expectations,","Surprisingly,","Nonetheless,","As predicted,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase must explicitly tie the flawless launch back to the engineers' specific warning, which 'Contrary to expectations' does directly. 'Surprisingly' marks only the writer's reaction without referencing the stated prediction, and 'Nonetheless' suggests the launch succeeded despite a general obstacle rather than refuting a specific forecast, so 'Contrary to expectations' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The farmer switched his entire wheat crop to organic growing methods, despite warnings from neighbors that yields would suffer. ______ his harvest that year was the largest he had ever recorded.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A record harvest directly contradicts the neighbors' warning of reduced yields, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The airline routinely overbooked its most popular routes to offset last-minute cancellations. ______ it ended up paying millions of dollars in compensation to bumped passengers each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The compensation costs were a direct consequence of the overbooking practice, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The volcano had shown no signs of activity for over three centuries and was widely considered extinct. ______ it erupted without warning, forcing the evacuation of nearby villages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Predictably,","For instance,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An eruption after centuries of dormancy is a surprising turn, which 'Unexpectedly' signals correctly.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$For most of her career, the painter was celebrated for meticulously realistic portraits. ______ her final decade of work embraced bold abstraction that alienated many longtime collectors before eventually reshaping her critical reputation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","However,","Instead,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence explicitly sets two distinct career phases side by side—realistic portraits versus bold abstraction—which calls for the parallel-comparison transition 'By contrast.' 'However' would mark contrast too generally without emphasizing the two-phase comparison, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the later style erased or replaced the earlier one rather than simply differing from it, so 'By contrast' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Dozens of free language-learning apps are available online. ______ a paid subscription app with no free version has attracted millions of loyal users.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Attracting millions of paying users despite abundant free competition is a concession, which 'Nonetheless' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The publisher printed only 2,000 copies of the debut novel, expecting modest interest given its unconventional structure. ______ word-of-mouth recommendations drove the book to sell over a million copies within its first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","As predicted,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The runaway sales directly replace the publisher's expectation of modest interest, which 'Instead' signals precisely. 'Nonetheless' would suggest success happened despite some stated obstacle rather than contradicting a forecast, and 'As predicted' asserts the opposite of what occurred, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$For over a century, a live keeper reliably tended the town's lighthouse each night. ______ the harbor authority replaced the position with an automated light sensor in 2005.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","As a result,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite the keeper's long, reliable service, the position was still eliminated — a concession relationship signaled by 'Even so.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Marine biologists expected the bleached reef to take decades to recover. ______ coral cover rebounded to pre-bleaching levels within just six years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The quick rebound contradicts the biologists' expectation of a decades-long recovery, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The public library eliminated late fees on overdue books last year. ______ the number of returned items increased noticeably in the following months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in returns is a direct consequence of eliminating late fees, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A single saguaro cactus can store hundreds of gallons of water in its stem after a rainstorm. ______ it can survive more than a year without any additional rainfall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Surviving a year without rain is a direct consequence of the cactus's water storage, so 'Consequently' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Developers promised the new wind farm would power every home in the county. ______ it currently supplies electricity to barely a third of local households.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Similarly,","For this reason,","As anticipated,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence reveals the actual, disappointing reality behind the developers' promise, which 'In fact' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The company advertised the new update as a minor bug fix. ______ it introduced a complete redesign of the user interface that confused many longtime users.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A complete redesign contradicts the description of a minor fix, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$City planners promised the new rail line would open within two years. ______ permitting disputes and funding shortfalls delayed the opening by nearly a decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lengthy delay contradicts the planners' two-year promise, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city council passed one of the strictest noise ordinances in the state last year. ______ complaints about late-night noise from the entertainment district have barely declined.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Granted,","Instead,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Complaints persisting despite a newly strict ordinance is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nonetheless' signals. 'Granted' would need to introduce a concession before a main claim rather than follow one, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the complaints replaced the ordinance rather than continuing despite it, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A nonprofit installed beehives on dozens of city rooftops to help pollinator populations. ______ local honey production in the city has increased sharply.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in honey production is a direct consequence of the rooftop hives, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Snow leopards are notoriously difficult to spot in the wild due to their camouflage and remote habitat. ______ a wildlife photographer managed to capture video of three leopards together in a single week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Remarkably,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Capturing three elusive leopards in one week is a striking, unexpected feat, which 'Remarkably' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The renowned conductor fell ill just hours before the sold-out performance. ______ his twenty-four-year-old assistant stepped in and led the orchestra through a flawless rendition of the symphony.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Instead,","In contrast,","Because of this,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A flawless performance despite the conductor's sudden illness is a direct, single-storyline contrast, which plain 'Yet' signals. 'In contrast' is for comparing two separate subjects side by side, not one crisis-and-recovery story, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the illness never actually happened, so 'Yet' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A shipping delay cut off the roastery's usual supply of green coffee beans for nearly two months. ______ the owners began sourcing beans from three new regional farms they had never worked with before.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sourcing new farms was a direct response to the supply disruption, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$For decades, historians believed the village was abandoned before the famine struck. ______ pottery shards unearthed at the site in 2019 suggest residents remained for at least another generation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["More recently,","Subsequently,","Eventually,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$New 2019 evidence revises a long-held historical belief, which requires the updating transition 'More recently.' 'Subsequently' marks mere sequence without signaling a revision of prior belief, and 'Eventually' implies the final stage of a single process rather than a shift in scholarly consensus, so 'More recently' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city spent millions reinforcing its seawalls after the last major hurricane. ______ the barriers held firm when an even stronger storm struck three years later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The seawalls holding firm is a direct consequence of the earlier reinforcement, so 'As a result' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The fitness company predicted its new training app would attract a million users within the first month. ______ fewer than 50,000 people had downloaded it by the end of that period.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The low download number contradicts the company's prediction, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The Hendersons installed a drip irrigation system on their small farm during a severe drought. ______ their crop yields held steady even as neighboring farms without the system suffered losses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Steady yields during the drought were a direct consequence of the irrigation system, so 'Consequently' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The pianist did not begin formal lessons until she was sixteen, far later than most concert musicians. ______ she won a major international competition just four years later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For example,","That is,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Winning a major competition despite such a late start is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The recycling plant's engineers claimed their new sorting system would cut contamination rates in half. ______ independent audits found contamination rates had barely changed one year after installation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Independent audit data directly refutes the engineers' claim, a straightforward contradiction best marked by 'However.' 'Instead' would suggest the claim is being replaced by a parallel alternative rather than disproven by evidence, and 'Nonetheless' implies the claim held true despite some obstacle, which misstates the relationship, so 'However' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Heavy rains delayed construction on the park renovation for most of the spring. ______ the project was still completed before the summer festival opened.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Finishing on time despite the delays is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The submersible was designed only to survey known shipwreck sites at moderate depths. ______ during one dive it detected an entirely uncharted vent ecosystem nearly five kilometers down.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Predictably,","For instance,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Detecting an uncharted ecosystem far outside the sub's intended purpose is a surprising turn, which 'Unexpectedly' signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$State transportation officials built a wildlife overpass above the busiest stretch of the highway. ______ animal-vehicle collisions in that section dropped by more than 90 percent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in collisions is a direct consequence of building the overpass, so 'As a result' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Many schools phased out handwriting instruction in favor of typing lessons over the past decade. ______ several districts have recently begun reintroducing cursive writing to the curriculum.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Reintroducing cursive reverses the earlier trend away from handwriting, a contrast signaled by 'However.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The mechanic estimated the vintage car's restoration would take about six months. ______ sourcing original parts for the engine stretched the project to nearly three years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Similarly,","For this reason,","As anticipated,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence reveals what actually happened, far exceeding the original estimate, which 'In fact' signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Ticket sales at the century-old theater had declined steadily for a decade as streaming services grew popular. ______ a single viral clip of a local performance sent sales soaring by 300 percent within a month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Remarkably,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The transition must capture just how startling a 300% jump is after a decade of decline, which 'Remarkably' emphasizes. 'Nonetheless' implies the rise happened despite the decline continuing, and 'Instead' merely marks replacement without conveying the dramatic scale of the reversal, so 'Remarkably' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A sharp decline in wild bee populations has worried farmers across the region for years. ______ one orchard began renting managed honeybee hives each spring and has seen pollination rates recover fully.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The orchard's full recovery contrasts with the region-wide decline described first, so 'However' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Landslides cut off the mountain village's only road for several weeks each spring. ______ residents have grown accustomed to stockpiling supplies well before the rainy season begins.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Stockpiling supplies is a direct response to the recurring road closures, so 'Consequently' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Engineers warned that a failure to deploy the probe's solar panels would end the mission within days. ______ ground controllers remotely triggered a backup mechanism that unfolded the panels and saved the mission.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Fortunately,","Remarkably,","Nonetheless,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The backup mechanism's success is framed as a lucky, welcome break from the engineers' warning, which 'Fortunately' captures through its positive evaluation. 'Remarkably' notes surprise without that favorable framing, and 'Nonetheless' implies success despite a persisting obstacle rather than a rescue from one, so 'Fortunately' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The vacant lot had been an eyesore filled with debris for over a decade. ______ neighborhood volunteers transformed it into a thriving community garden within a single summer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Transforming the lot despite its decade of neglect is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The manufacturer advertised that the new phone's battery would last two full days on a single charge. ______ most reviewers found it barely lasted through a single day of normal use.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The reviewers' findings directly contradict the manufacturer's claim, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The ice cream shop had guarded its signature flavor recipe for over thirty years. ______ a former employee published the full recipe online last month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Because of this,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The recipe becoming public despite decades of secrecy is a contrast, best signaled by 'Yet.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The regional almanac predicted an unusually mild, frost-free spring for the area. ______ an unexpected cold snap in late April destroyed nearly a third of the season's fruit blossoms.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The devastating cold snap replaces the almanac's prediction of a mild spring with the opposite reality, which 'Instead' signals precisely. 'Nonetheless' would suggest the damage happened despite some obstacle rather than contradicting a forecast, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Tardigrades can survive temperatures ranging from nearly -272°C to 150°C.
• They can survive more than a decade without water by entering a dried-out state called desiccation.
• Tardigrades have survived direct, unprotected exposure to the vacuum of space.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how extreme the range of conditions tardigrades can survive is. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tardigrades can withstand temperatures from nearly -272°C to 150°C and even survive unprotected exposure to the vacuum of space.","Tardigrades can survive more than a decade without water by entering a dried-out state.","Tardigrades are often studied by scientists interested in extreme biology.","Many tiny organisms can survive in harsh environments."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the extreme temperature range with survival in the vacuum of space, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing the range of conditions tardigrades endure.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Nikola Tesla developed and promoted alternating current (AC) electrical systems in the late nineteenth century.
• Thomas Edison was a major proponent of direct current (DC) and worked to discredit AC.
• AC eventually became the standard for long-distance electricity transmission because it loses less energy over long distances.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why AC ultimately won out over DC for long-distance power transmission. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["AC became the long-distance standard because it loses less energy than DC over long distances.","Nikola Tesla developed alternating current systems in the late nineteenth century.","Thomas Edison worked to discredit alternating current in favor of direct current.","Electricity transmission has changed considerably since the nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific technical reason — lower energy loss — that explains why AC became the long-distance standard.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Komodo dragons are the largest living species of lizard.
• Their saliva contains venom that prevents a victim's blood from clotting.
• A single bite can incapacitate prey many times the dragon's own size.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Komodo dragons are able to take down prey much larger than themselves. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because a Komodo dragon's venomous saliva stops blood from clotting, a single bite can incapacitate prey many times its own size.","Komodo dragons are the largest living species of lizard.","Many large predators rely on a powerful bite to subdue their prey.","Large lizards are found in a variety of habitats around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the venom's anti-clotting effect to the specific outcome of incapacitating much larger prey, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Stonehenge's smaller "bluestones" were transported from quarries in Wales, roughly 200 kilometers away.
• Some of the stones weigh as much as 4 tons.
• Researchers believe the stones were moved using sledges and timber rails rather than wheeled vehicles.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how builders are believed to have transported the massive stones without wheels. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers believe builders used sledges and timber rails, rather than wheeled vehicles, to move stones weighing up to 4 tons from quarries 200 kilometers away.","Stonehenge's bluestones came from quarries in Wales, roughly 200 kilometers from the site.","Some of Stonehenge's stones weigh as much as 4 tons.","Ancient monuments like Stonehenge continue to intrigue archaeologists."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names the specific transport method, sledges and timber rails, that answers how the wheel-less builders moved the massive stones.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Ada Lovelace worked with Charles Babbage on his proposed Analytical Engine in the 1840s.
• She wrote a detailed algorithm for calculating Bernoulli numbers for the machine.
• Many historians consider her notes to contain the first published computer program.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the specific reason Ada Lovelace is often called the first computer programmer. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Historians often call Ada Lovelace the first computer programmer because her notes contain what is considered the first published algorithm written for a computing machine.","Ada Lovelace worked with Charles Babbage on his Analytical Engine in the 1840s.","The Analytical Engine was a mechanical computing device proposed in the 1840s.","Computing technology has advanced dramatically since the nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific reason for Lovelace's title — her published algorithm — directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Hubble Space Telescope launched in 1990 with a flawed primary mirror.
• Images from the telescope were initially blurrier and far less sharp than expected.
• A 1993 space shuttle mission installed corrective optics that fixed the problem.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how astronauts corrected the flaw in Hubble's original design. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Astronauts fixed the flaw in Hubble's 1990 mirror by installing corrective optics during a 1993 space shuttle mission.","The Hubble Space Telescope launched in 1990 with a flawed primary mirror.","Images from Hubble were initially blurrier than expected.","Space telescopes allow astronomers to observe without atmospheric interference."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the specific corrective action, installing new optics in 1993, that fixed the original mirror flaw.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A 2021 volcanic eruption in Iceland occurred in a relatively accessible, unpopulated valley.
• Lava flows were slow-moving and posed little danger to visitors who kept a safe distance.
• Tourist visits to the eruption site topped 100,000 within the first two months.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why this eruption drew unusually large crowds of visitors rather than triggering a mass evacuation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the eruption occurred in an accessible, unpopulated valley with slow-moving, low-danger lava, more than 100,000 visitors flocked to see it within two months rather than being evacuated.","A volcanic eruption occurred in Iceland in 2021.","Lava flows from the eruption moved slowly and posed little danger to visitors.","The eruption occurred in a relatively accessible, unpopulated valley."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines accessibility, low danger, and the visitor count to fully explain why crowds came rather than evacuees fled; the choices about slow-moving lava or the accessible valley each give only one piece of that explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Silk Road was a network of trade routes connecting China to the Mediterranean.
• Traders carried silk, spices, and precious metals along the routes.
• The routes also facilitated the spread of religions, technologies, and diseases between distant regions.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the Silk Road spread more than just physical goods. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Beyond carrying goods like silk and spices, the Silk Road's routes also spread religions, technologies, and diseases across distant regions.","The Silk Road connected China to the Mediterranean through a network of trade routes.","Traders along the Silk Road carried silk, spices, and precious metals.","Many ancient trade routes connected distant civilizations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explicitly contrasts physical goods with the non-material things the Silk Road spread, directly meeting the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Leonardo da Vinci filled thousands of notebook pages with sketches, scientific observations, and inventions.
• Much of his writing was done in mirror script, readable only when held up to a mirror.
• Many of his engineering designs, such as flying machines, were never built during his lifetime.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the unusual way da Vinci recorded many of his notes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Da Vinci recorded much of his writing in mirror script, text that is legible only when reflected in a mirror.","Da Vinci filled thousands of notebook pages with sketches and scientific observations.","Many of da Vinci's engineering designs were never built during his lifetime.","Leonardo da Vinci is remembered as both an artist and an inventor."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the unusual mirror-script method itself, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The U.S. unemployment rate reached roughly 25% at the height of the Great Depression in 1933.
• Thousands of banks failed in the early 1930s, wiping out many families' savings.
• The New Deal introduced programs intended to create jobs and stabilize the economy.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the specific scale of joblessness during the worst year of the Great Depression. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At the depth of the Great Depression in 1933, roughly one in four American workers was unemployed.","Thousands of banks failed in the early 1930s, wiping out many families' savings.","The New Deal introduced programs meant to create jobs and stabilize the economy.","The Great Depression was one of the most severe economic downturns in American history."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific unemployment figure, directly fulfilling the goal of describing the scale of joblessness.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The 1986 Chernobyl disaster forced the permanent evacuation of the surrounding area.
• Human absence has allowed wolf, lynx, and elk populations in the exclusion zone to rebound.
• Some radiation levels in the zone remain too high for long-term human habitation.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific detail to support the claim that wildlife has thrived in the absence of people. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Since humans were evacuated after the 1986 disaster, wolf, lynx, and elk populations within the Chernobyl exclusion zone have rebounded.","The 1986 Chernobyl disaster forced the permanent evacuation of the surrounding area.","Some radiation levels within the exclusion zone remain too high for long-term human habitation.","Without people around, wildlife in the exclusion zone has generally thrived."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names the specific species that rebounded and ties that rebound to the human evacuation; the choice saying wildlife has generally thrived makes the same claim without the concrete evidence, and the choices about the evacuation or lingering radiation don't mention wildlife at all.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Axolotls are a species of salamander that can regenerate entire lost limbs.
• They can also regrow parts of their heart, spinal cord, and even portions of their brain.
• Unlike most amphibians, axolotls typically remain in an aquatic, larval-like form their entire lives.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the range of body parts axolotls are able to regenerate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Axolotls can regenerate not only entire limbs but also parts of their heart, spinal cord, and brain.","Axolotls are a species of salamander that lives underwater.","Unlike most amphibians, axolotls remain in a larval-like form for their entire lives.","Salamanders are amphibians known for their varied life cycles."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer lists multiple regenerated body parts, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing the range of axolotl regeneration.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Venus flytrap has small trigger hairs inside each of its hinged leaves.
• The trap only snaps shut if two hairs are touched within about 20 seconds of each other.
• This two-touch requirement helps the plant avoid wasting energy on false alarms like raindrops.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Venus flytrap avoids closing in response to non-prey stimuli like rain. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the Venus flytrap only closes when two trigger hairs are touched within about 20 seconds of each other, it avoids wasting energy snapping shut for false alarms like raindrops.","The Venus flytrap has small trigger hairs inside each of its hinged leaves.","Venus flytraps are carnivorous plants that capture insects.","Many plants have evolved specialized structures to capture prey."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the two-touch mechanism and connects it to avoiding false alarms, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Inca Empire built a road network spanning more than 30,000 kilometers across the Andes.
• The roads connected the empire without the use of wheeled vehicles.
• Relay runners called chasquis carried messages along the roads at high speed.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the scale of the Inca road network. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Inca Empire's road network stretched more than 30,000 kilometers across the Andes.","The Inca roads connected the empire without the use of wheeled vehicles.","Relay runners called chasquis carried messages along the Inca roads.","The Inca Empire was one of the largest empires in pre-Columbian America."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific length of the network, directly fulfilling the goal of conveying its scale.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed that mold had contaminated one of his bacterial culture plates.
• The mold, later identified as Penicillium, appeared to be killing the surrounding bacteria.
• It took over a decade before penicillin was mass-produced as an antibiotic.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Fleming's discovery was accidental rather than the result of a deliberate search. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fleming discovered penicillin's antibacterial effect by chance, after noticing that mold had contaminated and was killing bacteria on one of his culture plates.","Alexander Fleming made his discovery in 1928.","It took more than a decade before penicillin was mass-produced as an antibiotic.","Antibiotics have saved countless lives since their introduction."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer frames the discovery as a chance observation of contamination, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing its accidental nature.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Mariana Trench is the deepest known point in the world's oceans, reaching nearly 11,000 meters.
• Water pressure at the bottom exceeds 1,000 times the pressure at sea level.
• Specialized submersibles are required to withstand the crushing pressure at that depth.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extreme water pressure found at the bottom of the trench. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At the bottom of the Mariana Trench, water pressure exceeds 1,000 times what it is at sea level.","The Mariana Trench reaches a depth of nearly 11,000 meters.","Specialized submersibles are needed to explore the trench's depths.","Because it is so deep, the Mariana Trench requires specialized submersibles for exploration."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the actual pressure figure—1,000 times sea-level pressure—that the goal asks to emphasize; the others describe the trench's depth or the need for submersibles without quantifying the pressure itself.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jane Goodall began observing wild chimpanzees in Tanzania in 1960.
• She documented chimpanzees stripping leaves from twigs to fish termites out of mounds.
• This was among the first documented instances of tool use by a non-human animal.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the significance of Goodall's termite-fishing observation for the scientific understanding of animal behavior. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Goodall's observation of chimpanzees stripping twigs to fish termites from mounds was among the first documented cases of tool use in a non-human animal.","Jane Goodall began observing wild chimpanzees in Tanzania in 1960.","Chimpanzees are closely related to humans among primates.","Many animals exhibit complex social behaviors in the wild."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the specific observation to its scientific significance as early evidence of non-human tool use, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Terracotta Army was discovered by farmers digging a well in 1974.
• It consists of thousands of life-sized clay soldiers buried to guard the tomb of China's first emperor.
• No two soldiers' faces are exactly alike.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify who first discovered the Terracotta Army and how. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Terracotta Army was discovered in 1974 by farmers who were digging a well.","The Terracotta Army consists of thousands of life-sized clay soldiers.","No two soldiers in the Terracotta Army have exactly the same face.","China's first emperor is remembered for several large construction projects."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names both who made the discovery and the circumstances of how, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Namib desert beetle lives in one of the driest deserts on Earth, which receives almost no rainfall.
• Its back is covered in bumps that collect tiny water droplets from the morning fog.
• Grooves between the bumps channel the collected water directly to the beetle's mouth.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how this beetle is able to survive with almost no rainfall in its habitat. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because bumps on its back collect fog droplets, which grooves then channel directly to its mouth, the beetle can get water even though its desert habitat sees almost no rainfall.","The Namib desert beetle lives in one of the driest deserts on Earth, which receives almost no rainfall.","Bumps on the beetle's back collect tiny water droplets from the morning fog.","Grooves between the bumps channel collected water to the beetle's mouth."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the full fog-harvesting mechanism, from collection to delivery; the choices about the bumps or the grooves each give only half of that mechanism, and the choice about the desert's dryness states the problem without any solution.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail developed Morse code in the 1830s and 1840s.
• Common letters like "E" and "T" were assigned the shortest codes.
• This design allowed telegraph operators to transmit everyday messages more quickly.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why assigning short codes to common letters made the telegraph system more efficient. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By assigning the shortest codes to common letters like \"E\" and \"T,\" Morse code let operators transmit everyday messages more quickly.","Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail developed Morse code in the 1830s and 1840s.","Telegraph operators used Morse code to send messages over long distances.","Communication technology advanced rapidly in the nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the short-code design for common letters to the resulting gain in transmission speed, directly explaining the efficiency.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Norse explorers established a settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows in present-day Newfoundland around 1000 CE.
• This was roughly 500 years before Columbus's voyages to the Americas.
• The settlement was abandoned after only a few years, likely due to conflicts with Indigenous peoples.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much earlier Norse explorers reached North America compared to Columbus. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Norse explorers reached North America at L'Anse aux Meadows around 1000 CE, roughly 500 years before Columbus's voyages.","The Norse settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows was abandoned after only a few years.","Conflicts with Indigenous peoples likely contributed to the settlement's abandonment.","Many explorers have sailed across the Atlantic Ocean over the centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific 500-year gap between the Norse arrival and Columbus, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mantis shrimp have among the most complex eyes in the animal kingdom, with up to 16 types of photoreceptors.
• Each eye can move independently and perceive depth on its own.
• Some species can also detect polarized light, which most animals cannot see.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how unusual the mantis shrimp's visual system is compared to most animals. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["With up to 16 types of photoreceptors and the ability to detect polarized light invisible to most animals, the mantis shrimp has one of the most unusual visual systems in the animal kingdom.","Each of a mantis shrimp's eyes can move independently of the other.","Mantis shrimp are known for their powerful, fast-striking claws.","Many marine animals have evolved specialized sensory systems."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the photoreceptor count with the polarized-light ability, together showing just how unusual the mantis shrimp's vision is.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Bermuda Triangle is a region of the Atlantic Ocean associated with supposed mysterious disappearances.
• Insurance records, such as those of Lloyd's of London, show no unusual rate of shipping losses in the area.
• The region sees heavy marine traffic and seasonal tropical storms, both of which can account for ordinary accidents.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that the Bermuda Triangle has an unusually high rate of disappearances. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although the Bermuda Triangle is famous for supposed mysterious disappearances, insurance records show no unusual rate of shipping losses in the area.","The Bermuda Triangle is a region of the Atlantic Ocean.","The region experiences heavy marine traffic and seasonal tropical storms.","Many legends have grown up around dangerous stretches of ocean."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the popular misconception using the insurance-record evidence, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A hummingbird's heart can beat more than 1,200 times per minute during flight.
• Hummingbirds are the only birds capable of sustained hovering flight.
• They must feed on nectar every ten to fifteen minutes while awake to maintain their energy.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how fast a hummingbird's heart beats during flight. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["During flight, a hummingbird's heart can beat more than 1,200 times per minute.","Hummingbirds are the only birds capable of sustained hovering flight.","Hummingbirds must feed on nectar every ten to fifteen minutes while awake.","A hummingbird's heart beats very quickly during flight."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific rate—more than 1,200 beats per minute—that the goal asks to emphasize; the choice saying the heart beats very quickly makes the same claim without a number, and the hovering and feeding facts are true but unrelated to heart rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Louis Braille began developing his raised-dot reading system in the 1820s, after losing his sight as a child.
• Each Braille character is formed from a grid of up to six raised dots.
• The system was initially resisted by some educators before gaining widespread adoption decades later.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what personal experience motivated Braille to invent the system. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Louis Braille, who had lost his sight as a child, began developing his raised-dot system in the 1820s.","Each Braille character is formed from a grid of up to six raised dots.","The Braille system was initially resisted by some educators.","Braille eventually gained widespread adoption among educators."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects Braille's own childhood vision loss to his motivation for developing the system, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Cattle produce methane during digestion, a gas with a much stronger short-term warming effect than carbon dioxide.
• Some ranchers have begun adding seaweed supplements to cattle feed.
• Early trials show certain seaweed additives can cut cattle methane emissions by over 80%.$q$, $q$The student wants to use a specific piece of evidence to support the claim that diet changes can meaningfully reduce cattle's climate impact. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Early trials show that adding certain seaweed supplements to cattle feed can cut methane emissions from digestion by over 80%.","Cattle produce methane during digestion, a gas with a strong short-term warming effect.","Some ranchers have begun adding seaweed supplements to cattle feed.","Because cattle produce a strong-warming gas during digestion, some ranchers have begun adding seaweed to their feed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer cites the trial result—an 80% emissions cut—that actually supports the claim; the choice that ranchers have begun adding seaweed mentions the intervention without evidence it works, and the choices about cattle methane explain the problem or the attempted fix without the outcome data.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Roman aqueducts carried water across long distances using only gravity, with no pumps.
• Engineers maintained a gradual, precise downward slope over many kilometers to keep water flowing.
• Some aqueducts included underground sections to protect water quality and avoid obstacles.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Roman aqueducts moved water without any mechanical pumps. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Roman engineers moved water for kilometers without pumps by maintaining a gradual, precise downward slope that let gravity do the work.","Some Roman aqueducts included underground sections to protect water quality.","Roman aqueducts carried water across long distances.","Ancient Romans built many large public works projects."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the precise-slope technique that allowed gravity alone to move the water, directly answering how pumps were avoided.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Cleopatra VII is believed to have been fluent in at least seven languages.
• She reportedly used these language skills to negotiate directly with foreign dignitaries without translators.
• She was the last active ruler of the Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Cleopatra's linguistic ability gave her a diplomatic advantage. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because she was fluent in at least seven languages, Cleopatra could negotiate directly with foreign dignitaries without relying on translators.","Cleopatra VII was the last active ruler of the Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt.","Cleopatra VII is believed to have been fluent in at least seven languages.","Cleopatra reportedly negotiated with foreign dignitaries without using translators."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer links the specific skill (fluency in seven languages) to its specific diplomatic payoff (no need for translators); the choices about her fluency alone or her translator-free negotiations alone each state only half of that cause-and-effect link.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Town A installed community-owned wind turbines in 2016; local electricity costs fell by 18% by 2022.
• Town B installed turbines owned by an outside energy company in 2016; local electricity costs fell by only 4% over the same period.
• Both towns have similar populations and wind conditions.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that community ownership, rather than wind conditions, explains Town A's larger drop in electricity costs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite having similar populations and wind conditions, Town A's community-owned turbines cut electricity costs by 18% by 2022, far more than the 4% drop in Town B, where an outside company owned the turbines.","Town A and Town B both installed wind turbines in 2016.","Town B's turbines were owned by an outside energy company.","Town A and Town B have similar wind conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the comparable-conditions detail with both towns' cost changes to suggest ownership structure, not wind conditions, drove the gap.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Region A invested heavily in public fast-charging stations starting in 2019; EV adoption reached 22% of new car sales by 2023.
• Region B made no comparable investment; EV adoption reached only 7% of new car sales over the same period.
• Both regions offered similar purchase tax credits for EVs.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to suggest that charging infrastructure, rather than tax incentives, drove the difference in EV adoption. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although both regions offered similar EV tax credits, Region A's heavy investment in fast-charging stations coincided with EV adoption reaching 22% of new car sales by 2023, compared to just 7% in Region B.","Region A began investing in public fast-charging stations in 2019.","Both regions offered similar purchase tax credits for electric vehicles.","Electric vehicle sales have grown in many parts of the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the matching tax-credit detail with both regions' adoption rates to suggest infrastructure, not incentives, explains the gap.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Colony collapse disorder refers to the sudden, unexplained disappearance of most worker bees from a hive.
• Affected colonies are often found with a live queen and plenty of food but almost no worker bees.
• Researchers suspect a combination of pesticides, parasites, and habitat loss may contribute to the disorder.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the specific pattern that defines colony collapse disorder. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Colony collapse disorder is marked by hives that still have a live queen and ample food but have lost nearly all of their worker bees.","Researchers suspect pesticides, parasites, and habitat loss may all contribute to colony collapse disorder.","Many pollinator species face population declines around the world.","Honeybee colonies play an important role in pollinating crops."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the specific defining pattern — a surviving queen and food supply alongside missing workers — rather than possible causes or unrelated facts.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city installed brighter LED streetlights along the riverfront path. ______ reports of nighttime bicycle accidents there fell sharply.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in accidents is a direct consequence of the brighter lighting, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The small hardware store has no website or online ordering system. ______ it continues to outsell every big-box competitor in the county.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Outselling larger competitors despite lacking an online presence is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Engineers expected the new battery chemistry to double the phone's battery life. ______ independent testing found almost no improvement over the previous model.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The test results contradict the engineers' expectation, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The orchestra's ticket prices rose sharply ahead of the gala season. ______ subscription renewals fell to their lowest level in a decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in renewals was a direct result of the price increase, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Several small businesses in the district have struggled since the highway bypass opened. ______ the century-old hardware store closed permanently last spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","As a result,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The hardware store's closure is offered as a specific case of the broader struggle, so 'For example' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The negotiations between the two unions dragged on for fourteen months without a breakthrough. ______ both sides reached a contract that satisfied neither side completely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Eventually,","Subsequently,","Gradually,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The contract capped fourteen months of stalled talks, so the transition must signal a long-awaited final outcome—'Eventually.' 'Subsequently' marks mere sequence without that sense of a drawn-out resolution, and 'Gradually' describes a slow process itself rather than its eventual result, so 'Eventually' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Critics predicted the remake would tarnish the beloved original film's legacy. ______ it was praised by longtime fans as a faithful and inventive tribute.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Fan praise directly replaces critics' prediction of damage to the film's legacy, which 'Instead' captures precisely. 'Nonetheless' would imply the praise occurred despite some stated obstacle rather than contradicting a forecast, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Heavy snowfall collapsed the roof of the community ice rink last winter. ______ the rink has remained closed for repairs ever since.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The prolonged closure is a direct consequence of the roof collapse, so 'As a result' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The pharmaceutical company's lead drug candidate showed promising results in early animal trials. ______ human trials revealed a serious side effect that halted development.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The halted development directly contradicts the earlier promising results, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The remote village has no paved roads connecting it to the nearest city. ______ it has become an unlikely hub for a thriving online handicraft business.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Admittedly,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A thriving online business despite total road isolation is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nevertheless' signals. 'Admittedly' would need to introduce the conceded limitation itself rather than follow it, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the isolation was replaced rather than simply not preventing success, so 'Nevertheless' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Streaming services have changed how many musicians earn income. ______ some independent artists now rely primarily on royalties from online plays rather than album sales.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","However,","As a result,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence gives a specific case illustrating the broader change described first, so 'For instance' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The airline switched to lighter aircraft seats to cut fuel costs. ______ it was able to reduce ticket prices on several popular routes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lower ticket prices are a direct consequence of the fuel savings, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The mayor's office claimed the new parking garage would be completed on budget. ______ city records later showed the project had run $12 million over its original estimate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","However,","Instead,","As anticipated,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$City records don't just contrast with the mayor's office's claim—they expose the concealed truth behind it, which the revealing transition 'In fact' captures. Plain 'However' would mark contrast without that exposing force, so 'In fact' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The university extended library hours during final exam week. ______ student visits to the library nearly tripled compared to a typical week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The surge in visits is a direct consequence of the extended hours, so 'As a result' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The weather service forecast clear skies for the entire week of the air show. ______ a sudden storm grounded every performance on opening day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The storm grounding the show contradicts the forecast of clear skies, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The small nonprofit operates with only two full-time staff members. ______ it has distributed meals to over 50,000 families since its founding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Serving so many families with such a small staff is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Sales of the board game had declined every year since its release in the 1990s. ______ a viral video review sent sales soaring to an all-time high last year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Predictably,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A sudden, record-breaking reversal after decades of decline is a genuinely surprising turn, which 'Unexpectedly' captures. 'Nonetheless' implies the rise happened despite the decline continuing rather than reversing it, and 'Predictably' asserts the opposite of what happened, so 'Unexpectedly' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The factory switched to biodegradable packaging for all its products. ______ shipping costs rose slightly due to the new material's extra weight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in shipping costs is a direct consequence of switching materials, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The startup rejected dozens of acquisition offers over five years, insisting on remaining independent. ______ it accepted a buyout from a much larger competitor last month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ultimately,","Similarly,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The buyout describes the eventual, final outcome after years of refusals, which 'Ultimately' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The island's economy depends heavily on a handful of industries. ______ tourism alone accounts for nearly half of all local jobs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","However,","As a result,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The tourism statistic is a specific example supporting the general claim made first, so 'For instance' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The panel of judges unanimously predicted the underdog team would be eliminated in the first round. ______ the team advanced all the way to the championship match.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Yet,","Nonetheless,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The team's championship run replaces the judges' prediction of early elimination with the opposite outcome, which 'Instead' signals precisely. 'Nonetheless' would imply the advance happened despite a stated obstacle rather than contradicting a prediction, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The hospital introduced a new scheduling system to reduce patient wait times. ______ average wait times fell by nearly 40 percent within three months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in wait times is a direct consequence of the new scheduling system, so 'As a result' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The violin had been stored in an attic for over sixty years and showed signs of neglect. ______ once restored, it produced a tone that rivaled instruments costing ten times as much.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Because of this,","Specifically,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Producing such a fine tone despite decades of neglect is a contrast, best signaled by 'Yet.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The company's product recall cost tens of millions of dollars and drew widespread media criticism. ______ customer loyalty surveys taken a year later showed little lasting damage to the brand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Accordingly,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The brand suffering little lasting damage despite the costly, criticized recall is a concession, which 'Nonetheless' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-185$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Geologists long assumed the canyon had formed gradually over millions of years of erosion. ______ new sediment analysis suggests a single catastrophic flood carved most of it in a matter of weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$New sediment analysis directly overturns geologists' long-standing assumption, a straightforward contradiction best marked by 'However.' 'Instead' fits when a stated plan or expectation is replaced by what actually happened, but here geologists hold a belief about the past that new evidence revises, and 'Nonetheless' implies the gradual-erosion view held true despite some obstacle, so 'However' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-186$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city planted thousands of trees along its main boulevards over the past decade. ______ summer temperatures downtown have measurably dropped compared to nearby areas.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in temperatures is a direct consequence of the tree planting, so 'As a result' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-187$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Several countries have begun taxing single-use plastics to curb waste. ______ one European nation reduced plastic bag use by 90 percent within two years of introducing such a tax.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","As a result,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nation's experience is offered as a specific case of the broader trend described first, so 'For example' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-188$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The app's founders launched it expecting a niche audience of hobbyist gardeners. ______ it was downloaded more than ten million times within its first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","However,","Nonetheless,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The founders' plan for a niche audience is replaced by the opposite reality of mass downloads, which calls for 'Instead' rather than the belief-correcting 'However' or the obstacle-despite framing of 'Nonetheless.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-189$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The new noise-canceling headphones use a stronger microphone array than earlier models. ______ they block significantly more ambient sound than their predecessor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Blocking more sound is a direct consequence of the stronger microphone array, so 'As a result' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-190$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The contract required the construction crew to finish the renovation within sixty days. ______ unexpected structural damage extended the project to nearly five months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","In addition,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lengthy extension contradicts the sixty-day contractual deadline, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-191$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The small-town diner has kept the same menu for forty years without a single addition. ______ it remains one of the most popular restaurants in the entire region.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Consequently,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Remaining popular despite never updating the menu is a concession, which 'Even so' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-192$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Alexander Graham Bell patented the telephone in 1876.
• His assistant, Thomas Watson, heard the first intelligible words transmitted over the device from another room.
• Bell was also deeply involved in deaf education throughout his life.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the first successful demonstration of Bell's telephone. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 1876, Bell's assistant Thomas Watson heard the first intelligible words ever transmitted over Bell's telephone, from another room.","Alexander Graham Bell was deeply involved in deaf education throughout his life.","Alexander Graham Bell patented the telephone in 1876.","Communication technology changed rapidly in the late nineteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the specific first successful demonstration, Watson hearing words from another room, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-193$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Frederick Banting and Charles Best isolated insulin from dog pancreases in 1921.
• Before this discovery, a diagnosis of diabetes was often fatal within a few years.
• The first human patient treated with insulin showed rapid improvement within hours.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the significance of the 1921 discovery for diabetes patients. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Before Banting and Best isolated insulin in 1921, a diabetes diagnosis was often fatal within a few years, but the first patient treated with it improved within hours.","Frederick Banting and Charles Best isolated insulin from dog pancreases.","Diabetes remains a serious chronic illness today.","Medical research in the early twentieth century led to many new treatments."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer contrasts the earlier fatal prognosis with the rapid improvement insulin produced, directly explaining the discovery's significance.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-194$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Golden Gate Bridge opened in 1937 after four years of construction.
• It was the longest suspension bridge span in the world at the time.
• Its distinctive orange color was chosen partly for visibility in San Francisco's fog.$q$, $q$The student wants to state why the bridge's color was chosen. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Golden Gate Bridge's orange color was chosen in part to make it more visible in San Francisco's frequent fog.","The Golden Gate Bridge opened in 1937 after four years of construction.","The Golden Gate Bridge was the longest suspension bridge span in the world when it opened.","San Francisco is known for its foggy weather."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific reason behind the bridge's color, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Severe drought combined with poor farming practices stripped topsoil across the Great Plains in the 1930s.
• Massive dust storms, called "black blizzards," sometimes blocked out the sun for hours.
• Hundreds of thousands of families migrated west, many to California, seeking work.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate the severity of the 1930s dust storms with a specific detail. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["So severe were the 1930s dust storms that these \"black blizzards\" could block out the sun for hours at a time.","Hundreds of thousands of families migrated west during the Dust Bowl.","Poor farming practices contributed to the stripping of topsoil in the 1930s.","The Great Plains experienced a severe drought during the 1930s."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific, vivid detail about the sun being blocked out, directly illustrating the storms' severity.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-196$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Manhattan Project employed over 130,000 people at its peak.
• Many workers did not know the ultimate purpose of the facilities where they worked.
• The project's total cost exceeded $2 billion, an enormous sum at the time.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how compartmentalized and secretive the project's workforce was. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite employing more than 130,000 people, the Manhattan Project kept many workers unaware of the ultimate purpose of their own facilities.","The Manhattan Project's total cost exceeded $2 billion.","The Manhattan Project employed more than 130,000 people at its peak.","Many workers did not know the ultimate purpose of the facilities where they worked."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the massive workforce with workers' ignorance of the project's purpose to show how compartmentalized it was; the choices about the workforce size or the workers' ignorance each give only half of that picture, and the $2 billion cost is unrelated to secrecy.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
