• No confirmed wreckage of her plane has ever been found.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the unresolved mystery surrounding Earhart's disappearance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite extensive searches, no confirmed wreckage of Amelia Earhart's plane has ever been found since she vanished over the Pacific in 1937.","Amelia Earhart was the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic Ocean.","Amelia Earhart was attempting to circumnavigate the globe in 1937.","Aviation in the early twentieth century involved considerable risk."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly addresses the unresolved mystery, the unfound wreckage, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-204$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jacques Cousteau co-developed the Aqua-Lung breathing device in 1943.
• It allowed divers to breathe underwater for extended periods without being tethered to the surface.
• Cousteau later used underwater film to bring ocean exploration to a wide public audience.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what made the Aqua-Lung an improvement over earlier diving equipment. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cousteau's 1943 Aqua-Lung let divers breathe underwater for long stretches without being tethered to the surface.","Jacques Cousteau used underwater film to bring ocean exploration to a wide audience.","Jacques Cousteau co-developed the Aqua-Lung in 1943.","Diving equipment has continued to improve since the mid-twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific improvement, untethered breathing, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-205$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Olmec and Maya civilizations were among the first to cultivate cacao, over 3,000 years ago.
• Early preparations of cacao were usually bitter, unsweetened drinks, often mixed with chili.
• Sugar was not commonly added to chocolate until Europeans began producing it in the 16th and 17th centuries.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that chocolate has always been a sweet treat. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although chocolate is associated with sweetness today, early cacao drinks prepared by the Olmec and Maya were typically bitter and unsweetened, often spiced with chili.","The Olmec and Maya civilizations cultivated cacao over 3,000 years ago.","Europeans began adding sugar to chocolate in the 16th and 17th centuries.","Chocolate is made from the cacao plant, which grows in tropical regions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the misconception, contrasting chocolate's sweet reputation with its bitter origins.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-206$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Giant squid have the largest eyes of any known animal, up to 10 inches across.
• They were not filmed alive in their natural habitat until 2012.
• Scientists believe their large eyes help them detect faint light and movement in the deep ocean.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why giant squid likely evolved such large eyes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Scientists believe giant squid evolved eyes up to 10 inches across, the largest of any animal, to detect faint light and movement in the deep ocean.","Giant squid were not filmed alive in their natural habitat until 2012.","Giant squid have the largest eyes of any known animal, up to 10 inches across.","Scientists believe large eyes help giant squid detect faint light and movement in the deep ocean."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the eyes' record size to their specific function to explain why they evolved that way; the choices about the eyes' record size alone or their light-detecting function alone each give only half, and the 2012 filming is unrelated to the question of why the eyes evolved.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-207$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Pistol shrimp snap a specialized claw shut fast enough to create a popping bubble.
