• Engineers completed a major stabilization project in the 1990s and 2000s that reduced the tilt.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the tower began leaning in the first place. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Leaning Tower of Pisa began tilting during construction because it was built on soft, unstable soil.","Construction of the Leaning Tower of Pisa took nearly 200 years to complete.","Engineers stabilized the Leaning Tower of Pisa in the 1990s and 2000s.","The Leaning Tower of Pisa is a popular tourist attraction today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific cause, unstable soil, that explains why the tower began leaning during construction.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-218$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Hindenburg was a hydrogen-filled airship that caught fire while landing in New Jersey in 1937.
• Of the 97 people aboard, 35 died, along with one worker on the ground.
• The disaster effectively ended the era of passenger airship travel.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the lasting effect the Hindenburg disaster had on airship travel. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The 1937 Hindenburg disaster effectively brought the era of passenger airship travel to an end.","The Hindenburg was a hydrogen-filled airship that caught fire in New Jersey in 1937.","Thirty-five of the 97 people aboard the Hindenburg died in the disaster.","Air travel has changed considerably since the early twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the disaster's lasting consequence, the end of passenger airships, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-219$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Hiram Bingham was guided to Machu Picchu by local residents in 1911.
• The site had never been completely unknown to people living in the region.
• Bingham's accounts introduced the site to a wide international audience.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Bingham discovered a site unknown to anyone. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Bingham's 1911 visit introduced Machu Picchu to an international audience, local residents who guided him there already knew of the site.","Hiram Bingham visited Machu Picchu in 1911.","Machu Picchu later became known to a wide international audience.","Many ancient sites in South America attract researchers and tourists."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the misconception, noting that local residents already knew of the site before Bingham's visit.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-220$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jonas Salk developed an injectable polio vaccine, announced successful in 1955.
• Salk refused to patent the vaccine, saying it belonged to the public.
• Mass vaccination campaigns sharply reduced polio cases in the United States within a few years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize Salk's decision not to profit personally from his discovery. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Jonas Salk refused to patent his 1955 polio vaccine, insisting that it belonged to the public rather than to him.","Jonas Salk's polio vaccine was announced successful in 1955.","Mass vaccination campaigns sharply reduced polio cases in the United States.","Vaccines have eliminated or greatly reduced many once-common diseases."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly states Salk's refusal to patent the vaccine, fulfilling the goal of emphasizing his decision not to profit from it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-221$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• District A extended public library hours to include weekends starting in 2018; childhood literacy test scores rose 12 points by 2023.
• District B kept traditional weekday-only hours; literacy scores rose just 3 points over the same period.
• Both districts have comparable school funding and student populations.$q$, $q$The student wants to use the data to argue that expanded library access, not funding differences, likely drove District A's larger literacy gains. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite comparable funding and student populations, District A's literacy scores rose 12 points after extending library hours to weekends, compared to just a 3-point rise in District B, which kept traditional hours.","District A extended its public library hours to include weekends starting in 2018.","District B kept its traditional weekday-only library hours.","District A and District B have comparable school funding."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the comparable-funding detail with both districts' score changes to suggest library access, not funding, drove the gap.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-222$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The sandwich is popularly named after John Montagu, the 4th Earl of Sandwich.
• According to legend, he requested meat between bread slices so he could keep eating while gambling.
• Historians note that similar bread-and-filling meals likely existed in various cultures long before Montagu.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Montagu invented the first-ever sandwich. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although the sandwich is popularly named for the Earl of Sandwich, historians note that similar bread-and-filling meals likely existed in other cultures long before him.","John Montagu was the 4th Earl of Sandwich.","According to legend, Montagu requested meat between bread slices while gambling.","Sandwiches are a popular food eaten in many cultures today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the misconception, noting that similar meals predated Montagu, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-223$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The QWERTY layout was designed in the 1870s for early mechanical typewriters.
• One common explanation is that it helped reduce jamming by separating frequently paired letters.
• Despite newer layouts claiming greater efficiency, QWERTY remains the dominant standard today.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain one theory behind why the QWERTY layout was originally designed the way it was. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["One common explanation for QWERTY's original design is that separating frequently paired letters helped reduce jamming in early mechanical typewriters.","QWERTY remains the dominant keyboard standard despite newer, competing layouts.","The QWERTY layout was designed in the 1870s.","Typewriters were eventually replaced by computer keyboards."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific jam-reducing rationale behind QWERTY's original design, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-224$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The small vineyard suffered its worst grape harvest in twenty years after an unexpected spring frost. ______ the wine produced from that very harvest went on to win a national award.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Consequently,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A ruined harvest producing an award-winning wine is an unexpected contrast, which 'Yet' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-225$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Edward Jenner tested his smallpox vaccine in 1796 using material from a cowpox sore.
• He inoculated a young boy, who then proved immune to smallpox exposure.
• His method is considered the foundation of modern vaccination.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify the biological source Jenner used to create his smallpox vaccine. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In 1796, Edward Jenner created his smallpox vaccine using material taken from a cowpox sore.","Edward Jenner inoculated a young boy to test his vaccine.","Jenner's method is considered the foundation of modern vaccination.","Smallpox was once a widespread and deadly disease."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names the specific source, a cowpox sore, that the prompt asks for, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-226$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city's new bus rapid transit line cost nearly twice its original budget to build. ______ average commute times across the city dropped by almost 20 minutes once it opened.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Specifically,","In other words,","As expected,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite the costly overrun, the line still delivered a real benefit, a concession relationship signaled by 'Even so.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-227$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Hoover Dam was completed in 1936 on the Colorado River.
• At the time, it was the largest concrete structure ever built.
• It took about five years and employed thousands of workers during the Great Depression.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that building the dam provided jobs during a period of economic hardship. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Built during the Great Depression, Hoover Dam provided jobs for thousands of workers at a time when employment was scarce.","Hoover Dam was completed in 1936 on the Colorado River.","At the time of its completion, Hoover Dam was the largest concrete structure ever built.","The dam took approximately five years to construct."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the dam's construction to the economic hardship of the Great Depression, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-228$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The ceramics studio's kiln malfunctioned just two days before the holiday craft fair. ______ the owner spent three sleepless nights firing pieces in a borrowed kiln across town.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Scrambling to use a borrowed kiln is a direct consequence of the malfunction, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-229$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Pluto was discovered by Clyde Tombaugh in 1930 and classified as the ninth planet.
• In 2006, the International Astronomical Union reclassified it as a "dwarf planet."
• The reclassification was based on a new, stricter definition of what counts as a planet.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct a common misconception that Pluto is still officially classified as a planet. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although many people still think of Pluto as the ninth planet, astronomers reclassified it as a dwarf planet in 2006.","Pluto was discovered by Clyde Tombaugh in 1930.","The International Astronomical Union adopted a stricter definition of what counts as a planet.","Pluto orbits the sun from the outer edge of the solar system."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the misconception that Pluto remains officially classified as a planet.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-230$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The university's physics department predicted its new telescope would detect only faint, distant galaxies. ______ the telescope's very first image captured a previously unknown comet much closer to home.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Likewise,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Capturing a nearby comet rather than distant galaxies contradicts the prediction, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-231$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Higgs boson was first proposed theoretically in 1964.
• It took nearly 50 years before scientists at CERN confirmed its existence experimentally.
• The discovery was announced in 2012 using data from the Large Hadron Collider.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the long gap between the theoretical prediction and its experimental confirmation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Nearly 50 years passed between the Higgs boson's theoretical proposal in 1964 and its experimental confirmation at CERN in 2012.","The Higgs boson was first proposed theoretically in 1964.","CERN scientists used data from the Large Hadron Collider to confirm the discovery.","The Large Hadron Collider is located near Geneva, Switzerland."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly states the nearly 50-year gap between prediction and confirmation, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-232$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The ski resort invested heavily in snowmaking machines after several winters of disappointingly low snowfall. ______ natural snowfall the following winter broke a decade-old record.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ironically,","Unexpectedly,","Remarkably,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The costly snowmaking investment being followed by a record natural snowfall is a self-undermining irony, not just a surprising fact, so 'Ironically' fits best. 'Unexpectedly' and 'Remarkably' both note surprise or scale but miss the specific paradox of the wasted preparation, so 'Ironically' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-233$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• James Watson and Francis Crick published the double-helix model of DNA in 1953.
• Their model relied heavily on X-ray diffraction images produced by Rosalind Franklin.
• Franklin's contribution was not widely credited until years after the discovery.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Watson and Crick alone were responsible for discovering DNA's structure. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Watson and Crick are often credited alone, their 1953 double-helix model relied heavily on Rosalind Franklin's X-ray diffraction images.","James Watson and Francis Crick published their model of DNA's structure in 1953.","Watson and Crick's model relied heavily on X-ray diffraction images.","Rosalind Franklin's contribution went largely uncredited for years after the discovery."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explicitly names Franklin and ties her X-ray images to the model's creation, directly correcting the misconception; the choice about the model relying on X-ray images omits her name and the choice about her going uncredited omits her specific contribution, so neither fully corrects it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-234$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The puppet theater's lead performer quit just two days before opening night. ______ an understudy who had never performed the role stepped in and delivered a flawless show.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Consequently,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A flawless show despite the sudden loss of the lead performer is a contrast in expectations, which 'Nevertheless' signals correctly.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-235$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Stephanie Kwolek invented Kevlar in 1965 while researching lightweight materials for tires.
• Kevlar is five times stronger than steel by weight.
• It is now used in bulletproof vests, helmets, and cables.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much stronger Kevlar is relative to its weight. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Kevlar, invented by Stephanie Kwolek in 1965, is five times stronger than steel by weight.","Kevlar was originally developed while researching lightweight materials for tires.","Kevlar is now used in products such as bulletproof vests and helmets.","Stephanie Kwolek worked as a chemist for several decades."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific strength-to-weight comparison that fulfills the goal of emphasizing Kevlar's strength.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-236$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Regulators cut the region's cod fishing quotas in half to protect the declining population. ______ cod numbers continued to fall even after the stricter limits took effect.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","However,","Instead,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cod numbers continuing to fall despite stricter quotas is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nonetheless' signals. 'However' would fit correcting a mistaken claim rather than describing an action that failed to reverse a trend, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the decline replaced some other expected outcome, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-237$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The 1883 eruption of Krakatoa was heard roughly 3,000 miles away.
• The eruption triggered tsunamis that killed tens of thousands of people.
• Volcanic ash from the eruption affected global temperatures for several years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how far the sound of the eruption traveled. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The 1883 eruption of Krakatoa was so loud that it was heard roughly 3,000 miles away.","Tsunamis triggered by the eruption killed tens of thousands of people.","Volcanic ash from Krakatoa affected global temperatures for years.","Krakatoa is a volcanic island located in present-day Indonesia."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific distance the sound traveled, directly fulfilling the goal of emphasizing its reach.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-238$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The small studio spent six years developing its debut video game, promoting it as a future genre classic. ______ it sold fewer than a thousand copies in its first month on sale.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The weak sales directly contradict the studio's own marketing claim, a straightforward correction best marked by 'However.' 'Nonetheless' would imply poor sales occurred despite some stated obstacle, and 'Instead' suggests a plan was replaced outright rather than a claim being disproven by the outcome, so 'However' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-239$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Thomas Newcomen built early steam engines in the early 1700s for pumping water out of mines.
• James Watt improved the design in 1776, making it far more fuel-efficient.
• Watt's improvements helped power the factories of the Industrial Revolution.$q$, $q$The student wants to clarify that Watt improved an existing design rather than inventing the steam engine from scratch. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although Watt is often credited with inventing the steam engine, he actually improved a design that Thomas Newcomen had built decades earlier.","James Watt's 1776 improvements made the steam engine far more fuel-efficient.","Watt's improved engine helped power factories during the Industrial Revolution.","Thomas Newcomen's engines were primarily used for pumping water out of mines."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly corrects the misconception by naming Newcomen's earlier design and Watt's later improvement.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-240$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A landslide damaged the national park's most popular summit trail, closing it indefinitely. ______ visitors can still reach the summit by a longer, less direct route.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Fortunately,","Consequently,","Similarly,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The alternate route is a welcome piece of good news following the closure, which 'Fortunately' captures.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-241$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Construction of the Trans-Siberian Railway began in 1891.
• The completed line stretches more than 9,000 kilometers, linking Moscow to Russia's Pacific coast.
• It remains the longest single railway line in the world.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the railway's exceptional length. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Trans-Siberian Railway stretches more than 9,000 kilometers, making it the longest single railway line in the world.","Construction of the Trans-Siberian Railway began in 1891.","The railway links Moscow to Russia's Pacific coast.","Russia spans eleven time zones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific length figure that fulfills the goal of emphasizing the railway's exceptional scale.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-242$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The public art installation downtown was intended to be temporary, lasting only a single summer. ______ residents petitioned to keep it permanently, and the city agreed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The piece becoming permanent contradicts its original temporary plan, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-243$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Elisha Otis demonstrated his safety elevator brake at a New York exposition in 1854.
• During the demonstration, he had the elevator's rope cut while he stood on the platform.
• The brake stopped the platform from falling, proving the device worked.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the specific demonstration Otis used to prove his invention worked. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Otis proved his safety brake worked by having the elevator's rope cut while he stood on the platform, a fall the brake successfully stopped.","Elisha Otis demonstrated his invention at a New York exposition in 1854.","Otis's invention was a brake designed for elevators.","Elevators became common in buildings after Otis's invention."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the specific rope-cutting demonstration, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-244$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The volunteer fire department relies on donations for nearly all of its equipment budget. ______ it maintains some of the newest trucks in the entire county.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Remarkably,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Having some of the county's newest trucks on donations alone is an impressively surprising fact, which 'Remarkably' emphasizes. 'Nonetheless' would frame the donation-reliance as an obstacle merely overcome, understating how striking the outcome is, and 'Instead' implies a replacement rather than an achievement, so 'Remarkably' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-245$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount St. Helens erupted violently on May 18, 1980, in Washington State.
• The eruption flattened forests across more than 200 square miles.
• It was the deadliest and most economically destructive volcanic eruption in U.S. history.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extent of the damage the eruption caused to the surrounding landscape. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The 1980 eruption of Mount St. Helens flattened forests across more than 200 square miles.","Mount St. Helens erupted on May 18, 1980, in Washington State.","The eruption is considered the deadliest in U.S. history.","Mount St. Helens is part of the Cascade Range."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific scale of forest destruction, fulfilling the goal of emphasizing the eruption's damage.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-246$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The craft brewery's seasonal pumpkin ale became unexpectedly popular on social media. ______ demand outpaced production, and the brewery sold out within a week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Selling out is a direct consequence of the surge in demand, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-247$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The earliest magnetic compasses were developed in China, possibly as early as the 2nd century BCE.
• They were originally used for divination and geomancy rather than navigation.
• Compasses were not widely adapted for sea navigation until centuries later.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that compasses were invented for navigation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although compasses are associated with navigation, the earliest Chinese compasses were used for divination and geomancy.","Magnetic compasses were first developed in China around the 2nd century BCE.","Compasses were not widely used for sea navigation until centuries after their invention.","Divination practices were common in ancient Chinese culture."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the misconception about the compass's original purpose.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-248$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A small studio trained its chess-playing program using only amateur-level games. ______ the program went on to defeat several grandmasters in exhibition matches.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Consequently,","For instance,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Defeating grandmasters after training only on amateur games is an unexpected outcome, which 'Surprisingly' signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-249$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Elias Howe patented a lockstitch sewing machine design in 1846.
• Isaac Singer later developed a more practical version and built a commercial company around it.
• The two inventors became rivals and were involved in a lengthy patent lawsuit.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Howe and Singer became rivals. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because Singer's commercially successful machine drew on Howe's earlier lockstitch patent, the two became rivals in a lengthy lawsuit.","Elias Howe patented a lockstitch sewing machine design in 1846.","Isaac Singer later developed a more practical version and built a commercial company around it.","The two inventors were involved in a lengthy patent lawsuit."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects Singer's commercial success to Howe's earlier patent to explain the cause of their rivalry; the lawsuit choice merely states that they were rivals without explaining why, and the choices about Howe's 1846 patent or Singer's commercial company each describe one inventor without connecting them.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-250$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The farmers' market relocated to a much smaller lot after losing its original parking agreement. ______ vendor turnout and average weekly sales both increased at the new location.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Accordingly,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sales increasing despite the smaller, less convenient location is an unexpected result, which 'Surprisingly' signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-251$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Galileo improved the design of the telescope in 1609.
• He used it to observe four moons orbiting Jupiter.
• This observation supported the idea that not everything in the universe orbits Earth.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Galileo's observation of Jupiter's moons was significant. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By observing four moons orbiting Jupiter, Galileo provided evidence that not everything in the universe orbits Earth.","Galileo improved the design of the telescope in 1609.","Galileo observed four moons orbiting the planet Jupiter.","Telescopes allow distant objects to be viewed more closely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the observation to its broader significance, fulfilling the goal of explaining its importance.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-252$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The opera house's board voted to cancel the entire season after a severe budget shortfall. ______ an anonymous donor stepped forward with enough funding to save every scheduled performance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Consequently,","Specifically,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The donor's rescue directly contradicts the board's decision to cancel, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-253$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• For centuries, many scholars believed the city of Troy was purely legendary.
• In the 1870s, Heinrich Schliemann excavated a site in modern Turkey that matched descriptions from ancient texts.
• His excavation uncovered multiple layers of ancient settlement at the site.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that Troy was purely legendary. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although long considered legendary, Troy was shown to be a real place when Schliemann excavated a matching site in the 1870s.","Heinrich Schliemann excavated a site in modern Turkey in the 1870s.","Schliemann's excavation revealed multiple layers of ancient settlement.","Ancient texts contain descriptions of the city of Troy."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the misconception that Troy was purely legendary.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-254$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$An early prototype of the toy required four different batteries and constant recharging to operate. ______ the final retail version runs on a single battery for over a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","As a result,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The retail version's efficiency is the opposite of the power-hungry prototype, so a contrast transition, 'By contrast,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-255$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Natural rubber supplies were disrupted for the United States during World War II.
• Chemists rapidly developed synthetic rubber as a substitute for military and civilian use.
• Production of synthetic rubber scaled from almost nothing to hundreds of thousands of tons within a few years.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why synthetic rubber production expanded so quickly during the war. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because wartime disruptions cut off natural rubber supplies, chemists scaled synthetic rubber production to hundreds of thousands of tons within a few years.","Natural rubber supplies were disrupted during World War II.","Synthetic rubber was used for both military and civilian purposes.","Rubber is used in a wide range of manufactured products."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer links the wartime cause, a disrupted supply, to the specific effect, rapid scale-up, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-256$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Researchers were thrilled to discover a new species of frog in a remote rainforest. ______ habitat loss in the same region threatens to drive the species to extinction before it can be fully studied.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The looming extinction threat undercuts the researchers' excitement, a direct contradiction best marked by 'However.' 'Instead' would wrongly suggest the threat replaces the discovery itself, and 'Nonetheless' awkwardly implies the threat persists despite the thrill rather than undercutting it, so 'However' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-257$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Erie Canal opened in 1825, connecting the Hudson River to Lake Erie.
• It reduced the cost of shipping goods between the Midwest and New York City by more than 90%.
• The canal helped establish New York City as a major commercial port.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much the canal reduced shipping costs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Erie Canal, which opened in 1825, cut the cost of shipping goods between the Midwest and New York City by more than 90%.","The Erie Canal connected the Hudson River to Lake Erie.","The canal helped establish New York City as a major commercial port.","Canals were an important form of transportation in the 19th century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific cost reduction figure, fulfilling the goal of emphasizing its economic impact.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-258$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city expanded its bike lane network by thirty miles over two years. ______ the number of cycling accidents reported at major intersections dropped by nearly half.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drop in accidents is a direct consequence of the expanded bike lanes, so 'As a result' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-259$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Louis Pasteur developed a rabies vaccine in 1885.
• He first tested it on a boy who had been bitten by a rabid dog.
• The boy survived, and the treatment became a landmark in preventive medicine.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the circumstances under which Pasteur's rabies vaccine was first tested on a person. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Pasteur's rabies vaccine was first tested in 1885 on a boy who had been bitten by a rabid dog, and the boy survived.","Louis Pasteur developed a rabies vaccine in 1885.","The treatment became a landmark in preventive medicine.","Rabies is a viral disease that affects the nervous system."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the specific first human test, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-260$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Mechanical problems delayed the amusement park's newest roller coaster for over a year past its planned opening. ______ it broke the park's single-day attendance record within its first week of operation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","However,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Setting an attendance record despite a lengthy delay is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Nonetheless' signals. 'However' fits correcting a mistaken claim, not describing success following an obstacle, and 'Instead' would wrongly suggest the record replaced the delay rather than following it, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-261$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Levi Strauss began selling denim work pants to miners during the California Gold Rush.
• Jacob Davis, a tailor, added copper rivets to reinforce the pants' stress points.
• Strauss and Davis patented the riveted design together in 1873.$q$, $q$The student wants to specify the innovation that made the riveted jeans design distinctive. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Jacob Davis's addition of copper rivets to reinforce stress points made the jeans he and Strauss patented in 1873 distinctive.","Levi Strauss began selling denim pants to miners during the Gold Rush.","Strauss and Davis patented their design together in 1873.","Denim work pants were popular among manual laborers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names the specific innovation, copper rivets, that made the design distinctive, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-262$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The vineyard's hillside plot is too steep and rocky for any harvesting machine to operate safely. ______ every grape on that plot must be picked by hand each autumn.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Hand-picking is a direct consequence of the terrain being unsuitable for machinery, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-263$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Tasmanian tiger, or thylacine, was a carnivorous marsupial once widespread across Australia.
• Competition with dingoes and hunting by humans drove it to extinction on the mainland.
• The last known thylacine died in captivity in 1936.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the thylacine went extinct on the Australian mainland. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Competition with dingoes, combined with hunting by humans, drove the thylacine to extinction on the Australian mainland.","The thylacine was a carnivorous marsupial once widespread across Australia.","The last known thylacine died in captivity in 1936.","Marsupials are mammals that typically carry their young in a pouch."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer identifies the specific causes of the thylacine's mainland extinction, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-264$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$A coral nursery program initially struggled to keep transplanted fragments alive in increasingly warm waters. ______ researchers have developed heat-resistant coral strains with much higher survival rates.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Since then,","Similarly,","For example,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The improved strains represent a later development following the initial struggles, a sequence correctly signaled by 'Since then.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-265$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Canada geese fly in a V-shaped formation during long migrations.
• Each bird benefits from reduced air resistance created by the bird ahead of it.
• Geese periodically rotate the lead position because it requires the most energy.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why geese rotate which bird leads the formation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Geese rotate the lead position during flight because leading the V-formation requires more energy than drafting behind another bird.","Canada geese fly in a V-shaped formation during long migrations.","Birds flying behind the leader benefit from reduced air resistance.","Canada geese migrate long distances each year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the specific reason for the rotation, the higher energy cost of leading, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-266$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The debate coach required her students to argue positions they personally disagreed with during practice rounds. ______ several students later said the exercise sharpened their ability to anticipate counterarguments.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","However,","Nonetheless,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The students' testimony doesn't just contrast with the exercise's discomfort—it confirms and reinforces its value, which the intensifying 'In fact' captures. Plain 'However' would only mark a contrast, and 'Nonetheless' frames the benefit as happening despite the exercise rather than growing out of it, so 'In fact' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-267$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Louis-Sébastien Lenormand demonstrated an early parachute jump from a tower in 1783.
• He coined the word "parachute" from Greek and French roots meaning "against falling."
• His design predated practical aircraft by over a century.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the origin of the word "parachute." Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Lenormand coined the term \"parachute\" by combining Greek and French roots meaning \"against falling.\"","Louis-Sébastien Lenormand demonstrated a parachute jump from a tower in 1783.","Lenormand's design predated practical aircraft by over a century.","Lenormand coined a new word to describe his invention."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific Greek and French roots and their meaning, directly explaining the word's origin; the choice saying he coined a new word doesn't say what it meant, and the 1783 tower jump and the design predating aircraft are real but irrelevant to etymology.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-268$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A city zoo's panda breeding program had failed for over a decade despite consulting international experts. ______ last spring, a female panda gave birth to twins conceived through artificial insemination.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Finally,","Consequently,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The twins' birth is the long-awaited culmination of a decade of failed attempts, an eventual outcome that 'Finally' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-269$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Angkor Wat was built in the 12th century by the Khmer Empire in present-day Cambodia.
• It was originally constructed as a Hindu temple before later becoming a Buddhist site.
• It remains one of the largest religious monuments in the world by land area.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the temple's exceptional size. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Angkor Wat remains one of the largest religious monuments in the world by land area.","Angkor Wat was built in the 12th century by the Khmer Empire.","The temple was originally built for Hindu worship before becoming a Buddhist site.","Angkor Wat is located in present-day Cambodia."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly states the temple's exceptional scale, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-270$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The tech conference's keynote speaker canceled just hours before the event was set to begin. ______ organizers scrambled to find a replacement able to address the same topic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The scramble for a replacement is a direct consequence of the cancellation, so 'Consequently' correctly signals cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-271$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Early chewing gum in the United States was often made from spruce tree resin.
• In the 1870s, Thomas Adams began experimenting with chicle, a natural latex from the sapodilla tree.
• Chicle produced a smoother, longer-lasting chew than resin-based gum.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Adams's chicle-based gum was an improvement over earlier gum. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Adams's chicle-based gum, developed in the 1870s, offered a smoother, longer-lasting chew than earlier gum made from spruce resin.","Early chewing gum in the United States was often made from spruce tree resin.","Chicle is a natural latex derived from the sapodilla tree.","Chewing gum remains a popular product today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific improvement, a smoother, longer-lasting chew, over the earlier resin-based gum, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-272$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The urban garden co-op charges members a small annual fee to help maintain shared tools and soil. ______ the co-op has waived that fee entirely for first-time gardeners under eighteen.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Consequently,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The fee waiver for young gardeners is an exception to the general fee policy, a contrast correctly signaled by 'However.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-273$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Eli Whitney patented the cotton gin in 1794.
• The machine could clean cotton fifty times faster than doing so by hand.
• Its efficiency led plantation owners to expand cotton production and reliance on enslaved labor.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain an unintended social consequence of the cotton gin's efficiency. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the cotton gin cleaned cotton fifty times faster than hand labor, plantation owners expanded production and deepened their reliance on enslaved labor.","Eli Whitney patented the cotton gin in 1794.","The cotton gin could clean cotton far faster than doing so by hand.","The gin's efficiency led plantation owners to expand cotton production."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the gin's speed to the specific social consequence of deepened reliance on enslaved labor; the choice about owners expanding production states only half of that consequence, and the 1794 patent and the gin's speed describe the invention without any social effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-274$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Organizers rerouted the marathon at the last minute after a water main broke along the original course. ______ runners found that the new route included a far steeper hill than organizers had anticipated.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Unexpectedly,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Finding a steeper hill than organizers anticipated is a surprising discovery, which 'Unexpectedly' captures directly. 'Nonetheless' implies the discovery occurred despite some other obstacle, and 'Instead' suggests a planned outcome was replaced rather than an unplanned complication being discovered, so 'Unexpectedly' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-275$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sea turtles can detect subtle variations in Earth's magnetic field.
• Hatchlings use this sense to navigate the open ocean after leaving their birth beach.
• Many sea turtles return to lay eggs on the same beach where they themselves hatched.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how hatchling sea turtles are able to navigate the open ocean. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Hatchling sea turtles navigate the open ocean by detecting subtle variations in Earth's magnetic field.","Sea turtles can detect subtle variations in Earth's magnetic field.","Many sea turtles return to lay eggs on the beach where they hatched.","Sea turtles face numerous threats throughout their long migrations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly explains the mechanism, magnetic field detection, that hatchlings use to navigate, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-276$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The antique watch show typically draws a modest crowd of regional collectors each year. ______ this year's show attracted serious collectors from three different countries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Consequently,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Drawing international collectors breaks from the show's usual modest pattern, a contrast correctly signaled by 'However.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-277$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Richard Drew worked for 3M as a young engineer in the 1920s.
• He invented masking tape in 1925 after noticing painters struggled with uneven paint lines.
• He later invented transparent cellophane tape, marketed as Scotch tape, in 1930.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what inspired Drew to invent masking tape. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Drew invented masking tape in 1925 after observing painters struggle to create clean, even paint lines.","Richard Drew worked as a young engineer for 3M in the 1920s.","Drew later invented transparent cellophane tape in 1930.","Scotch tape became a widely used household product."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific observation that inspired the invention, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-278$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The chemistry teacher had assigned the same lab experiment every year without any changes. ______ this past year she redesigned it entirely after students kept reporting confusing instructions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Finally,","Consequently,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The redesign after years of repeating the same experiment unchanged is a long-delayed action, which 'Finally' correctly signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-279$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jesse Reno patented an early escalator design in 1892.
• His version was more like an inclined moving ramp than a staircase.
• It was first installed as a novelty ride at Coney Island in 1896.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe how Reno's early escalator was first used. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Reno's early escalator design was first installed not in a building but as a novelty ride at Coney Island in 1896.","Jesse Reno patented an early escalator design in 1892.","Reno's version resembled an inclined moving ramp more than a staircase.","Escalators are now common in shopping malls and transit stations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the specific, surprising first use of the invention, as an amusement ride, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-280$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Analysts predicted that a sudden drop in oil prices would cripple the small nation's economy. ______ earlier investments in tourism and manufacturing cushioned most of the expected losses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The economy being cushioned rather than crippled contradicts the analysts' prediction, so a contrast transition, 'Instead,' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-281$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Berlin Wall was erected in 1961 to divide East and West Berlin.
• It stood as a physical symbol of the Cold War division of Europe for nearly three decades.
• The wall fell in 1989 after mass protests and political upheaval in East Germany.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how quickly the wall's fall followed decades of division. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["After standing as a symbol of Cold War division for nearly three decades, the Berlin Wall fell within months of the 1989 protests in East Germany.","The Berlin Wall was erected in 1961 to divide East and West Berlin.","The wall fell in 1989 after mass protests in East Germany.","The wall stood as a symbol of Cold War division for nearly three decades."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer sets the decades of division against the comparatively fast collapse to emphasize the contrast; the choices about the 1989 fall or the three decades of division each state one half of that contrast, and the 1961 construction date describes only the wall's origin.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-282$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The botanical garden's rarest seeds lose viability if left at room temperature for more than a few hours. ______ staff now store every rare seed sample in a climate-controlled vault kept near freezing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For this reason,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The climate-controlled vault is a direct response to the seeds' fragility, so a cause-and-effect transition, 'For this reason,' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-283$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Roy Plunkett accidentally discovered Teflon in 1938 while researching refrigerant gases.
• He noticed that a sample had polymerized into a slippery, heat-resistant solid.
• Teflon is now best known for its use in non-stick cookware.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how Plunkett came to discover Teflon. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Plunkett discovered Teflon by accident in 1938 when a sample he was researching as a refrigerant gas polymerized into a slippery solid.","Roy Plunkett was researching refrigerant gases in 1938.","Teflon is now best known for its use in non-stick cookware.","Teflon is a synthetic material with a wide range of uses."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the accidental circumstances of the discovery, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-284$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The two rival chefs had spent years publicly criticizing each other's restaurants in local reviews. ______ they agreed to co-host a single charity dinner, cooking side by side for one night only.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,","Nonetheless,","Instead,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two longtime public rivals cooking together is a genuinely surprising turn given their history, which 'Surprisingly' emphasizes. 'Nonetheless' frames the dinner as happening despite an obstacle rather than as an unexpected reversal, and 'Instead' implies a plan was replaced rather than an unlikely event occurring, so 'Surprisingly' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-285$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A narwhal's tusk is actually an elongated front tooth that can grow over 8 feet long.
• The tusk is covered in nerve endings connected to the narwhal's brain.
• Scientists believe the tusk may help narwhals sense changes in water conditions.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that a narwhal's tusk is a horn. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although often mistaken for a horn, a narwhal's tusk is actually an elongated front tooth that can grow over 8 feet long.","A narwhal's tusk is covered in nerve endings connected to its brain.","Scientists believe the tusk may help narwhals sense changes in water conditions.","Narwhals live primarily in Arctic waters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the misconception that the tusk is a horn.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-286$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Gray whales migrate between Arctic feeding grounds and warmer breeding lagoons in Mexico.
• The round trip can cover more than 12,000 miles, among the longest of any mammal migration.
• Mother gray whales nurse their calves in the shallow, protected lagoons before heading north.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extraordinary length of the gray whale's migration. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A gray whale's round-trip migration between the Arctic and Mexico can cover more than 12,000 miles, among the longest of any mammal.","Gray whales migrate between Arctic feeding grounds and breeding lagoons in Mexico.","Mother gray whales nurse their calves in shallow, protected lagoons.","A gray whale's migration between the Arctic and Mexico is among the longest of any mammal."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the actual distance—more than 12,000 miles—that quantifies the migration's extraordinary length; the choice calling it among the longest of any mammal makes the same claim without a number, and the choices about the Arctic-to-Mexico route or calf-nursing lagoons describe the route or calf-rearing without addressing its length.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-287$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The chef spent months refining a single sauce recipe. ______ the dish became the restaurant's signature item.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sauce's refinement directly caused the dish's new status, so a cause-and-effect transition is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-288$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The museum's new wing was expected to open by spring. ______ construction delays pushed the opening to late fall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Therefore,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The delayed opening contradicts the original expectation, so the contrast word 'Instead' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-289$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The orchestra rehearsed the symphony for nearly six weeks. ______ the opening night performance still contained several rough passages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","As a result,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite extensive rehearsal, problems remained, a concession best signaled by 'Even so.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-290$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Heavy rains saturated the hillside for nearly a week. ______ a large section of the slope collapsed overnight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Meanwhile,","Similarly,","Admittedly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The saturation directly caused the collapse, so the cause-and-effect transition 'Consequently' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-291$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The new library branch offers free wifi and several quiet study rooms. ______ it hosts weekly workshops on resume writing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","However,","Instead,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds another service to the one named first, so an addition transition is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-292$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Many coastal towns have adapted to rising sea levels in unconventional ways. ______ one town built a seawall almost entirely from recycled shipping containers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Nevertheless,","Meanwhile,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence gives a specific example of the general claim in the first, so 'For instance' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-293$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The engineer insisted that the bridge's design met every safety standard. ______ independent inspectors later found several welds that failed to meet code.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The inspectors' findings directly disprove the engineer's claim with contradicting evidence, which 'However' signals. 'Instead' would suit a plan replaced by what actually happened, not a claim corrected by inspection results, so 'However' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-294$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The volcano had shown no signs of activity for nearly three decades. ______ it erupted violently without warning in 1991.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Specifically,","Similarly,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sudden eruption contrasts with decades of apparent dormancy, so 'Nevertheless' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-295$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The runner trained through the hottest months of summer. ______ she finished the marathon nearly ten minutes faster than her previous best.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","In contrast,","For example,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her improved time resulted directly from the training, so the cause-and-effect transition fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-296$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The new vaccine proved highly effective against the original viral strain. ______ it also provided partial protection against a newly emerged variant.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","However,","Instead,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds another benefit, so an addition transition like 'Moreover' is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-297$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The startup's founders predicted rapid growth within the first year. ______ the company struggled to attract even a handful of early customers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Similarly,","For instance,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The company's struggles directly contradict the founders' prediction, so 'Instead' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-298$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Engineers have proposed several ways to extend smartphone battery life. ______ one team is developing a battery that recharges using ambient light.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","Nonetheless,","Therefore,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence offers a specific example of the general proposals mentioned first.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-299$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The region experienced its driest growing season in forty years. ______ crop yields fell by nearly a third.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","By contrast,","For instance,","Admittedly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The drought directly caused the drop in yields, so a cause-and-effect transition is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-300$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Critics have long argued that online courses cannot replicate the engagement of in-person seminars. ______ a recent study found that online students in small discussion groups participated just as actively as their in-person peers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A study's contradicting findings disprove the critics' long-standing claim, which calls for the belief-correcting 'However.' 'Instead' fits a plan replaced by reality, not a claim overturned by research, so 'However' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-301$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The subway line was first proposed in the 1970s. ______ it did not break ground until nearly thirty years later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Therefore,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The long delay between proposal and construction is an unexpected contrast, which 'Yet' signals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-302$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Migratory songbirds rely on stars to navigate at night. ______ many species also sense the Earth's magnetic field.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","Nevertheless,","Instead,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds another navigational tool to the one named first, so an addition transition fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-303$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The sculptor was celebrated during her lifetime for strict, minimalist forms. ______ her private sketchbooks reveal an exuberant, almost chaotic imagination.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","However,","Instead,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence explicitly sets two sides of the same artist—her public minimalist work and her private sketchbooks—side by side, which the parallel-comparison 'By contrast' captures precisely. 'However' would mark contrast more generally, and 'Instead' wrongly implies one erased the other rather than both coexisting, so 'By contrast' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-304$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The translator chose to preserve the poem's unusual line breaks rather than smooth them into conventional sentences. ______ she prioritized the original rhythm over easy readability.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In other words,","However,","For example,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence restates the first in different terms, so 'In other words' is the appropriate transition.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-305$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A software error shut down the telescope's guidance system for six hours. ______ astronomers lost a rare chance to observe the comet's closest approach.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","Similarly,","In contrast,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The shutdown directly caused the lost observation opportunity, so the cause-and-effect transition is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-306$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Economists generally agree that raising interest rates slows inflation. ______ the policy also risks slowing economic growth enough to cause a recession.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["At the same time,","However,","Nonetheless,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The recession risk is an additional concern that holds alongside the agreed-upon benefit, not a contradiction of it, so the simultaneity marker 'At the same time' fits best. 'However' would wrongly frame the risk as negating the benefit, and 'Nonetheless' implies the risk persists despite some obstacle, so 'At the same time' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-307$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Sleep researchers have identified several habits that improve sleep quality. ______ keeping a consistent wake-up time appears to matter more than total hours slept.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Nonetheless,","Thus,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence gives a specific example of a general claim about helpful habits, so 'For instance' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-308$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The redesigned park added shaded walking paths throughout the grounds. ______ it introduced a splash pad for young children.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Additionally,","Instead,","Therefore,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence adds another feature of the redesign, so an addition transition is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-309$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The piano was invented in the early 1700s as a quieter alternative to the harpsichord. ______ by the late 1800s it had become the dominant keyboard instrument in concert halls.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Eventually,","Meanwhile,","For example,","Admittedly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence describes a later outcome in a sequence of events, so 'Eventually' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-310$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Adding a small amount of catalyst lowered the reaction's activation energy substantially. ______ the reaction that once took hours now completed in minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","In contrast,","Nonetheless,","Granted,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The dramatically faster reaction is a direct, logical effect of the lowered activation energy, which 'Consequently' signals. 'In contrast' would require two different reactions being compared side by side, and 'Nonetheless' implies the speedup happened despite an obstacle rather than because of the catalyst, so 'Consequently' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-311$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Participants who studied material right before sleeping were expected to remember it poorly. ______ they outperformed participants who studied the same material in the morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Similarly,","For example,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The actual result contradicts the expectation stated first, so the contrast word 'Instead' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-312$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The defense conceded that the contract's wording was ambiguous. ______ it argued that the ambiguity should be resolved in its client's favor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","Admittedly,","Instead,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite conceding the ambiguity, the defense still pressed its own interpretation, a concession-and-persistence relationship signaled by 'Nonetheless.' 'Admittedly' would redundantly repeat the concession rather than pivot to the argument that follows it, so 'Nonetheless' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-313$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The newsroom cut its print staff by half over the past decade. ______ it tripled the size of its digital team.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Meanwhile,","Therefore,","For example,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The two staffing changes happened over the same period, so the simultaneity transition 'Meanwhile' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-314$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Traditional brick construction insulates buildings well but takes months to complete. ______ prefabricated panel construction can be finished in a matter of weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Similarly,","As a result,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Prefabricated construction is presented as the opposite of traditional brick construction in speed, so 'By contrast' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-315$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Regional dialects often preserve words that have vanished from standard usage. ______ some rural communities still use a term for 'icicle' that dates back centuries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","Consequently,","In short,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence gives a specific illustration of the general claim about preserved words, so 'For example' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-316$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Visiting the national park in winter means dealing with closed trails and limited services. ______ the crowds that overwhelm the park in summer are entirely absent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","Similarly,","Therefore,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The absence of summer crowds is an upside that contrasts with the winter drawbacks mentioned first, so 'However' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-317$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The factory replaced its manual assembly line with robotic arms capable of working around the clock. ______ output nearly doubled within the first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","In contrast,","Admittedly,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The near-doubling of output is a direct, logical effect of switching to round-the-clock robotic automation, which 'As a result' signals. 'In contrast' would require two different factories or systems being compared, and 'Admittedly' would concede a limitation before a main claim, neither of which fits a straightforward cause-and-effect result, so 'As a result' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-318$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honeybees communicate the location of food sources through a "waggle dance."
• The dance encodes both the distance and direction of the source relative to the sun.
• Scout bees perform the dance inside the hive after returning from a successful search.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the dance conveys precise spatial information. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Through the waggle dance, scout honeybees encode both the distance and direction of a food source relative to the sun.","Scout bees perform the waggle dance inside the hive after a successful search.","Honeybees are important pollinators of many crops.","Only scout bees that have found food perform the waggle dance."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific distance and direction information the dance conveys, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-319$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Johannes Gutenberg introduced the movable-type printing press in Europe around 1440.
• Before the press, books were copied by hand, a process that could take months.
• Within fifty years, printing presses had spread to over 200 European cities.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how quickly the technology spread across Europe. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Within just fifty years of its introduction, Gutenberg's printing press had spread to more than 200 cities across Europe.","Before the press, scribes copied books by hand, a process that could take months.","Gutenberg introduced his press in Europe around 1440.","Books became a popular form of entertainment during the Renaissance."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the rapid spread of the press across hundreds of cities, directly fulfilling the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-320$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses can change both the color and texture of their skin in under a second.
• Specialized skin cells called chromatophores control the pigment changes.
• Unlike most camouflaging animals, octopuses achieve this despite being colorblind.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the surprising fact that octopuses camouflage effectively despite lacking color vision. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Remarkably, octopuses change skin color and texture in under a second despite being colorblind.","Chromatophores are specialized skin cells that control pigment changes.","Octopuses are among the most intelligent invertebrates.","Octopus skin can change texture as well as color."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines the speed of camouflage with the colorblindness detail, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-321$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Rosalind Franklin produced X-ray diffraction images that revealed DNA's helical structure.
• Her photograph, known as "Photo 51," was shown to Watson and Crick without her knowledge.
• Watson and Crick used her data to help build their 1953 model of DNA.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Franklin's contribution was used without her consent. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Franklin's X-ray image, Photo 51, was shown to Watson and Crick without her knowledge and used to help build their 1953 DNA model.","Watson and Crick published their model of DNA's structure in 1953.","Franklin's X-ray diffraction images revealed DNA's helical structure.","DNA carries genetic information in the form of a double helix."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states both the unauthorized sharing of her data and its use, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-322$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Iceland sits atop a volcanically active region with abundant underground heat.
• Roughly 90% of Icelandic homes are heated using geothermal energy.
• Geothermal plants also generate about a quarter of the country's electricity.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how widely geothermal energy is used for home heating specifically. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Roughly 90% of homes in Iceland rely on geothermal energy for heating.","Iceland sits atop a volcanically active region with abundant underground heat.","Geothermal plants generate about a quarter of Iceland's electricity.","Iceland has a small population relative to its land area."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific heating statistic requested by the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-323$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Roman concrete structures like the Pantheon have survived for nearly 2,000 years.
• Modern concrete structures often begin to crack and deteriorate within decades.
• Researchers found that Roman concrete contains volcanic ash that reacts with seawater to self-heal tiny cracks.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Roman concrete has lasted so much longer than modern concrete. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Roman concrete contains volcanic ash that reacts with seawater to self-heal tiny cracks, helping explain why structures like the Pantheon have survived nearly 2,000 years.","Modern concrete structures often crack and deteriorate within decades.","The Pantheon is one of the best-preserved buildings from ancient Rome.","Researchers continue to study ancient Roman building techniques."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the causal mechanism, the self-healing volcanic ash, that explains Roman concrete's durability.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-324$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Female anglerfish can be over sixty times the size of males.
• Males attach themselves permanently to a female's body shortly after finding her.
• Once attached, a male's eyes and internal organs gradually degrade, and he survives mainly by drawing nutrients from the female's bloodstream.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extreme physical transformation males undergo after attaching to a female. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["After attaching to a female, a male anglerfish's eyes and internal organs gradually degrade as he comes to survive mainly on nutrients drawn from her bloodstream.","Female anglerfish can be over sixty times the size of males.","Males attach themselves permanently to a female shortly after finding her.","After attaching, a male anglerfish survives mainly by drawing nutrients from the female's bloodstream."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes both the degrading of the male's eyes and organs and his reliance on the female's bloodstream, capturing the full extremity of the transformation; the choice about males attaching permanently states only the attachment, and the choice about drawing nutrients from the female's bloodstream gives the survival mechanism without the degeneration.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-325$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Some bamboo species can grow up to 35 inches in a single day.
• Bamboo is technically a type of grass, not a tree.
• Its rapid growth makes it a popular material for sustainable construction.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how unusually fast bamboo can grow. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Certain bamboo species can grow as much as 35 inches in a single day.","Bamboo is technically a grass rather than a tree.","Bamboo's rapid growth makes it popular for sustainable construction.","Many homebuilders are turning to sustainable materials."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific daily growth figure that directly supports the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-326$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• English longbowmen at the Battle of Agincourt in 1415 could fire roughly ten to twelve arrows per minute.
• Drawing a war longbow required a pull of up to 150 pounds of force.
• Skeletal remains of longbowmen show enlarged, asymmetrical bones in their drawing arms.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the physical toll that using a longbow took on archers' bodies. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Skeletal remains of longbowmen reveal enlarged, asymmetrical bones in their drawing arms, a result of repeatedly pulling up to 150 pounds of force.","Longbowmen at Agincourt could fire ten to twelve arrows per minute.","The Battle of Agincourt took place in 1415.","Drawing a war longbow required a pull of up to 150 pounds of force."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer pairs the skeletal evidence with the 150-pound pull it resulted from to show the lasting physical toll; the 150-pound-pull choice states the strain without the evidence of damage, and the firing rate and the 1415 date are unrelated to the archers' bodies.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-327$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Swiss engineer George de Mestral invented Velcro after noticing burrs clinging to his dog's fur and his clothing during a hike.
• He spent nearly a decade refining the hook-and-loop design before it became commercially viable.
• Velcro later became essential for securing equipment in zero-gravity spacecraft.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Velcro's invention was inspired by observing nature. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["George de Mestral invented Velcro after noticing how burrs clung to his dog's fur and his own clothing during a hike.","De Mestral spent nearly a decade refining the hook-and-loop design.","Velcro later became essential for securing equipment in zero-gravity spacecraft.","Velcro is widely used in clothing and footwear today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the natural observation, burrs clinging to fur, that inspired the invention.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-328$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Everest's peak was first accurately measured in 1856 by a survey team using triangulation.
• Modern GPS measurements place its height at 29,031.7 feet.
• The mountain's height changes slightly each year due to tectonic activity and snowfall.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Everest's height is not perfectly fixed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Everest's height actually changes slightly from year to year because of tectonic activity and snowfall.","Everest's peak was first accurately measured in 1856 using triangulation.","Modern GPS measurements place Everest's height at 29,031.7 feet.","Many climbers attempt to summit Everest each year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states that the height varies and explains why, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-329$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest repositories of knowledge in the ancient world.
• Ships arriving in Alexandria's harbor were sometimes required to surrender scrolls to be copied for the library.
• Much of the library's collection was lost over centuries to fires, neglect, and conflict.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the aggressive methods used to build the library's collection. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Ships arriving in Alexandria's harbor were sometimes required to surrender scrolls so that copies could be added to the library's collection.","The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest repositories of knowledge in the ancient world.","Much of the library's collection was lost to fires, neglect, and conflict over the centuries.","Alexandria was an important center of trade in the ancient Mediterranean."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the forced surrender of scrolls, the specific acquisition method named in the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-330$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coral gets its color from symbiotic algae living in its tissue.
• When water temperatures rise even slightly above normal for extended periods, corals expel these algae.
• Without the algae, coral turns white and loses its main source of nutrients, often leading to death if conditions don't improve quickly.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the mechanism by which warm water causes coral to die. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Prolonged exposure to slightly elevated water temperatures causes coral to expel its algae, leaving it without its main nutrient source and vulnerable to death.","Coral gets its color from symbiotic algae living in its tissue.","Bleached coral turns white when it loses its algae.","Coral can die if it loses its main source of nutrients and conditions don't improve quickly."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer traces the full chain from warm water to algae expulsion to the risk of death, as the mechanism goal requires; the choices about algae giving coral its color, bleached coral turning white, or coral dying without nutrients each name only one link in that chain.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-331$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Archaeologists have found pots of honey in ancient Egyptian tombs that remained edible after 3,000 years.
• Honey's low moisture content and acidity make it inhospitable to most bacteria.
• Bees produce honey by repeatedly regurgitating and evaporating nectar.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how remarkably long honey can remain preserved. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Archaeologists have found pots of honey in ancient Egyptian tombs that remained edible after 3,000 years.","Honey's low moisture content and acidity make it inhospitable to most bacteria.","Bees produce honey by repeatedly regurgitating and evaporating nectar.","Honey has been used as a natural sweetener for thousands of years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the striking 3,000-year preservation figure that directly fulfills the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-332$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Beethoven began losing his hearing in his late twenties.
• He composed many of his most celebrated works, including his Ninth Symphony, after becoming almost completely deaf.
• He reportedly sawed the legs off his piano so he could feel the vibrations of the notes through the floor.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how Beethoven adapted physically to compose despite his hearing loss. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Beethoven reportedly sawed the legs off his piano so he could feel the vibrations of the notes through the floor.","Beethoven began losing his hearing in his late twenties.","Beethoven composed his Ninth Symphony after becoming almost completely deaf.","Beethoven is considered one of the most influential composers in history."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes a concrete physical adaptation Beethoven used to continue composing, matching the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-333$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Scientists extract long cylinders of ice, called ice cores, from deep within Antarctic glaciers.
• Trapped air bubbles in the ice preserve samples of the atmosphere from thousands of years ago.
• The oldest continuous ice cores recovered so far record roughly 800,000 years of climate history.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how far back in time ice cores allow scientists to study Earth's climate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The oldest continuous ice cores recovered so far allow scientists to study roughly 800,000 years of Earth's climate history.","Scientists extract long cylinders of ice, called ice cores, from Antarctic glaciers.","Trapped air bubbles in the ice preserve samples of ancient atmosphere.","Antarctica is the coldest continent on Earth."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific 800,000-year span that fulfills the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-334$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In the 1630s, the price of some rare tulip bulbs in the Netherlands rose to many times a skilled worker's annual salary.
• Contracts to buy tulip bulbs were traded on futures markets before the flowers even bloomed.
• The market collapsed abruptly in February 1637, leaving many traders with worthless contracts.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how disconnected tulip prices had become from the flowers' practical value. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At the height of the 1630s tulip craze, some rare bulbs sold for prices many times a skilled worker's annual salary.","Contracts to buy tulip bulbs were traded on futures markets before the flowers bloomed.","The tulip market collapsed abruptly in February 1637.","Tulip bulb prices rose to very high levels during the 1630s."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific comparison—many times a worker's annual salary—that shows how disconnected prices were from practical value; the choice saying prices rose to very high levels makes the same claim without that comparison, and the futures contracts and the 1637 collapse describe speculation or the crash rather than the price disconnect itself.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-335$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Construction of the Great Wall spanned many centuries and several different dynasties.
• The wall is not a single continuous structure but a network of walls and fortifications.
• Popular claims that it is visible from the Moon with the naked eye have been debunked by astronauts.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the common misconception that the Great Wall is visible from the Moon. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite popular claims, astronauts have confirmed that the Great Wall is not actually visible from the Moon with the naked eye.","Construction of the Great Wall spanned many centuries and several dynasties.","The wall is a network of walls and fortifications rather than one continuous structure.","The Great Wall attracts millions of tourists every year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly names and corrects the specific misconception stated in the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-336$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The platypus is one of only a few mammals that lay eggs.
• Its bill contains thousands of electroreceptors that detect tiny electrical fields produced by prey muscles.
• Platypuses hunt with their eyes, ears, and nostrils closed, relying entirely on this electrical sense underwater.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how completely the platypus depends on electroreception while hunting. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["While hunting underwater, platypuses close their eyes, ears, and nostrils and rely entirely on electroreceptors in their bill to detect prey.","The platypus is one of only a few mammals that lay eggs.","The platypus's bill contains thousands of electroreceptors.","Platypuses are found only in Australia."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the complete sensory shutdown and total reliance on electroreception, matching the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-337$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Alexander Fleming noticed in 1928 that mold had contaminated one of his bacterial culture plates.
• The mold, Penicillium, had created a zone around itself where no bacteria could grow.
• It took more than a decade after Fleming's observation before penicillin was mass-produced for medical use.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Fleming's discovery was accidental. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fleming's discovery began almost by accident, when he noticed in 1928 that mold had contaminated one of his bacterial culture plates.","The mold created a zone around itself where no bacteria could grow.","It took more than a decade before penicillin was mass-produced.","Penicillin is still used to treat bacterial infections today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer frames the discovery as accidental contamination, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-338$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Both Voyager probes, launched in 1977, carry a gold-plated phonograph record.
• The record includes greetings in 55 languages, natural sounds like whale calls, and musical selections from around the world.
• The probes are expected to keep traveling through interstellar space long after Earth itself may no longer exist in its current form.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extraordinary timescale over which the record might outlast Earth as we know it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Voyager probes are expected to keep carrying their golden records through interstellar space long after Earth itself may no longer exist in its current form.","The record includes greetings in 55 languages and natural sounds like whale calls.","Both Voyager probes were launched in 1977.","The record contains musical selections from around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the extraordinary timescale outlasting Earth, directly matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-339$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The now-familiar looped wire paperclip design was patented in 1899 by Norwegian inventor Johan Vaaler.
• Earlier versions of paper fasteners date back to the early 1800s but were far less practical.
• The paperclip's simple design has remained largely unchanged for over a century.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how little the paperclip's design has changed since its creation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The paperclip's simple looped design has remained largely unchanged for more than a century.","The looped wire paperclip was patented in 1899 by Johan Vaaler.","Earlier versions of paper fasteners date back to the early 1800s.","Paperclips are commonly used to organize loose papers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly states the persistence of the design, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-340$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Some desert wildflower seeds can remain dormant in the soil for decades, waiting for enough rainfall to germinate.
• A single unusually wet season can trigger a "superbloom," when millions of dormant seeds sprout at once.
• Superblooms can transform an entire desert landscape with color for just a few weeks.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how long desert seeds can wait before germinating. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some desert wildflower seeds can lie dormant in the soil for decades before enough rainfall finally triggers germination.","A wet season can trigger a \"superbloom\" of millions of seeds at once.","Superblooms can transform a desert landscape with color for a few weeks.","Deserts receive very little annual rainfall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the decades-long dormancy period that fulfills the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-341$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Roman Empire built an estimated 50,000 miles of paved roads at its height.
• Roads were constructed with multiple layers, including a foundation of large stones beneath finer gravel and paving.
• Many sections of Roman road remain usable or visible today, nearly 2,000 years after construction.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the durability of Roman road construction. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Many sections of Roman road remain usable or visible today, nearly 2,000 years after they were built.","The Roman Empire built an estimated 50,000 miles of paved roads.","Roman roads were constructed with multiple layers, including large foundation stones.","Roman roads connected cities across the empire for trade and military use."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the roads' survival across nearly 2,000 years, directly fulfilling the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-342$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Antikythera mechanism, recovered from a shipwreck in 1901, is an ancient Greek device dated to around 100 BCE.
• It used a complex system of at least 30 bronze gears to predict astronomical positions and eclipses.
• Devices of comparable mechanical complexity would not reappear in Europe for well over a thousand years.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how far ahead of its time the device's technology was. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Devices of comparable mechanical complexity to the Antikythera mechanism would not reappear in Europe for well over a thousand years.","The Antikythera mechanism was recovered from a shipwreck in 1901.","It used at least 30 bronze gears to predict astronomical positions.","The device is dated to around 100 BCE."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer makes the explicit historical comparison showing how far ahead of its time the device was.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-343$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Elephants have the largest number of genes dedicated to smell receptors of any mammal studied.
• Researchers estimate elephants can detect some odors from several kilometers away.
• Elephants use smell to locate water sources and distinguish between individual herd members.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how far elephants can detect scent. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Researchers estimate that elephants can detect some odors from several kilometers away.","Elephants have the largest number of smell-receptor genes of any mammal studied.","Elephants use smell to distinguish between individual herd members.","Elephants are the largest land animals on Earth."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific detection distance that fulfills the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-344$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Ancient Babylonian mathematicians used a placeholder symbol for zero as early as 300 BCE, but did not treat it as a number itself.
• Indian mathematicians, including Brahmagupta in the 7th century, were among the first to define zero as a number with its own arithmetic rules.
• The concept of zero as a true number later spread to the Islamic world and eventually to Europe.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that Indian mathematicians were the first to treat zero as a genuine number rather than just a placeholder. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Indian mathematicians, including Brahmagupta in the 7th century, were among the first to define zero as a true number with its own arithmetic rules.","Babylonian mathematicians used a placeholder symbol for zero as early as 300 BCE.","The concept of zero later spread to the Islamic world and then Europe.","Zero is essential to modern mathematics and computing."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer credits Indian mathematicians specifically with defining zero as a true number, matching the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-345$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles between Canada and central Mexico each year.
• No single monarch completes the entire round trip; the journey takes several successive generations.
• Scientists still do not fully understand how later generations find the same overwintering sites their ancestors used.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the puzzling fact that monarchs find overwintering sites they themselves have never visited. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Remarkably, scientists still do not fully understand how monarch generations that have never visited the overwintering sites manage to find them.","Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles between Canada and Mexico each year.","No single monarch completes the entire round trip alone.","Monarch caterpillars feed exclusively on milkweed plants."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the unexplained navigation puzzle across generations, matching the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-346$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Challenger Deep, located in the Mariana Trench, is the deepest known point in the ocean at nearly 36,000 feet.
• Water pressure at that depth is more than 1,000 times greater than at sea level.
• Only a handful of crewed descents to the Challenger Deep have ever been completed.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the extreme pressure conditions at the bottom of the Challenger Deep. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["At the bottom of the Challenger Deep, water pressure is more than 1,000 times greater than it is at sea level.","The Challenger Deep is the deepest known point in the ocean, at nearly 36,000 feet.","Only a handful of crewed descents to the Challenger Deep have been completed.","Because it is so deep, only a handful of crewed descents to the Challenger Deep have been completed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the actual pressure figure the goal asks to emphasize; the others describe the trench's depth or the rarity of descents without quantifying the pressure itself.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-347$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The oldest surviving photograph was taken by Joseph Nicephore Niepce in 1826 or 1827.
• Because the exposure took several hours, moving objects like people and carriages did not appear in the final image.
• The photograph shows the view from an upstairs window of Niepce's estate in France.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the earliest photograph shows no people, even though it was taken from a window overlooking a presumably busy area. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the exposure took several hours, moving objects like people and carriages simply did not appear in the final image.","The photograph was taken by Joseph Nicephore Niepce in 1826 or 1827.","The photograph shows the view from an upstairs window of Niepce's estate.","Photography developed rapidly as a technology throughout the 19th century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the reason, the hours-long exposure, that explains why no moving people appear in the image.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-348$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Cuneiform, developed in ancient Mesopotamia around 3400 BCE, is among the earliest known writing systems.
• Scribes pressed wedge-shaped marks into soft clay tablets using a reed stylus.
• Clay tablets, unlike papyrus or parchment, survive well over thousands of years, especially when baked or burned.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why so many cuneiform tablets have survived to the present day. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because clay tablets survive well over thousands of years, especially when baked or burned, many cuneiform texts have endured to the present day.","Cuneiform was developed in ancient Mesopotamia around 3400 BCE.","Scribes pressed wedge-shaped marks into clay using a reed stylus.","Cuneiform is considered among the earliest known writing systems."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the material explanation, clay's durability, for why so many tablets have survived.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-349$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A massive volcanic eruption in 1815 spewed ash high into the atmosphere, blocking sunlight across much of the globe. ______ crops failed in many regions the following year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The crop failures were a direct consequence of blocked sunlight, so the cause-and-effect transition 'As a result' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-350$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$An engineer once predicted that the wooden footbridge would collapse within a decade. ______ it has stood for more than fifty years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","In addition,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bridge's long survival contradicts the engineer's prediction, so the contrast transition 'Instead' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-351$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The museum opened a new wing devoted to contemporary sculpture. ______ it extended its weekend hours to accommodate more visitors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","However,","Instead,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Extending weekend hours is a second, related change alongside the new wing, so the addition transition 'In addition' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-352$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The chef built a reputation for unusual flavor pairings. ______ one popular dessert combines dark chocolate with chili powder.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Nevertheless,","As a result,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The dessert is a specific example of the chef's unusual pairings, so 'For instance' correctly introduces it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-353$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Rainfall across the valley fell far below average for three consecutive years. ______ reservoir levels dropped to their lowest point in decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Low reservoir levels are the effect of the sustained low rainfall, so the cause-and-effect transition 'Consequently' is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-354$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Critics dismissed the novel as derivative when it was first published. ______ it went on to sell millions of copies worldwide.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","As a result,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The novel's commercial success contrasts with its harsh reception, so the concession transition 'Nevertheless' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-355$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Finnish and Estonian both evolved from a common Uralic ancestor language. ______ both languages rely heavily on noun case endings rather than prepositions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","However,","As a result,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence states another point of resemblance between the languages, so 'Similarly' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-356$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The biologist first drafted a hypothesis about the bird's migration pattern. ______ she spent two summers tracking tagged birds to test it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Afterward,","However,","In other words,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Tracking the birds happened after drafting the hypothesis, so the sequence transition 'Afterward' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-357$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city has become known for its vibrant street art scene. ______ one downtown alley is covered wall to wall with murals by local artists.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In particular,","Nevertheless,","As a result,","Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The alley is a specific instance of the broader street art scene, so 'In particular' correctly narrows the focus.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-358$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The company switched to biodegradable packaging for all of its products. ______ its shipping costs rose because the new materials were bulkier than the old plastic wrap.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rise in shipping costs is a direct effect of the packaging switch, so 'As a result' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-359$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Economists widely expected a recession once interest rates rose sharply. ______ the economy grew faster than it had in the previous three years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","As a result,","Similarly,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The economy's unexpected growth directly contradicts the predicted recession, so the contrast transition 'Instead' is needed, not a cause-and-effect or addition word.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-360$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The new sequencing method takes nearly twice as long as the older technique. ______ it produces results with far fewer errors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The accuracy gain holds despite the slower speed, so the concessive transition 'Even so' is needed rather than a cause-and-effect or addition word.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-361$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Regulators tightened emissions standards for new vehicles. ______ automakers accelerated development of electric models.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","Nevertheless,","For instance,","In the same way,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Automakers' response was a direct consequence of the new standards, so 'Accordingly' correctly signals that cause-and-effect relationship.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-362$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The artist spent decades perfecting her technique in oil painting. ______ she mastered bronze casting well into her seventies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,","Instead,","As a result,","Nonetheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Mastering bronze casting is an additional, impressive accomplishment alongside painting, so the intensifying addition transition 'Moreover' fits best.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-363$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Students in the morning discussion section scored consistently well on the exam. ______ those in the afternoon section struggled with the same material.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Similarly,","As a result,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The afternoon section's performance directly opposes the morning section's, so the contrast transition 'By contrast' is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-364$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The budget phone costs significantly less than its competitors. ______ its camera and battery life fall noticeably short of industry standards.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["That said,","As a result,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The weaker specs qualify the praise of the low price, so the concessive transition 'That said' is needed rather than a result or example word.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-365$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The ancient manuscript was exposed to damp conditions for centuries. ______ most of its pages have become too brittle to handle without specialized equipment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","However,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The brittleness is a direct effect of centuries of dampness, so the cause-and-effect transition 'As a result' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-366$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The team lost three starting players to injury midseason. ______ it still managed to qualify for the playoffs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,","As a result,","For example,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Qualifying for the playoffs happened despite the injuries, so the concessive transition 'Nonetheless' is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-367$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The region experienced its driest growing season on record. ______ many farmers had to sell off portions of their livestock to cover their losses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a consequence,","However,","For example,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Selling livestock was a direct consequence of the severe drought, so 'As a consequence' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-368$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The orchestra's new conductor has reshaped its sound dramatically. ______ she slowed the tempo of several classical pieces to emphasize their emotional weight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Specifically,","However,","In addition,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slowing the tempo explains precisely how the conductor reshaped the sound, so 'Specifically' fits better than a word suggesting a separate added action.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-369$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$One proposal would fund the repairs through a property tax increase. ______ the alternative plan would rely on a one-time fee charged to new construction projects.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["On the other hand,","As a result,","For instance,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second plan is presented as an alternative to the first, so the contrast transition 'On the other hand' is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-370$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Parents complained for years about the school's rigid dress code. ______ the district revised the policy to allow more flexibility.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In response,","For example,","Similarly,","Nonetheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The policy revision was a reaction to the complaints, so 'In response' correctly signals that cause-and-effect relationship.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-371$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Engineers warned repeatedly that the dam's spillway was undersized for a major flood. ______ officials delayed funding the upgrade for another decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even then,","As a result,","For instance,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Officials ignored the warnings despite them, so the concessive transition 'Even then' is needed rather than a cause-and-effect word.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-372$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Researchers first identified the compound in a soil sample from a remote rainforest. ______ laboratory tests revealed that it had powerful antifungal properties.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Subsequently,","However,","Similarly,","In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The lab tests happened after the initial identification, so the sequence transition 'Subsequently' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-373$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The committee's decision was procedurally sound but substantively flawed. ______ the committee followed every required step yet reached a conclusion unsupported by the evidence it gathered.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["That is,","Namely,","Specifically,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second clause restates the same idea in concrete terms—procedurally fine but substantively unsupported—rather than introducing a new example or a particular item, so the restating transition 'That is' fits best. 'Namely' and 'Specifically' both introduce a specific instance rather than paraphrase the whole claim, and 'However' wrongly suggests a contradiction rather than a clarification, so 'That is' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-374$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The startup's app undeniably simplified scheduling for small medical clinics. ______ few of those clinics could justify its steep monthly subscription fee once a free alternative appeared.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Granted,","Instead,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clinics dropping the app despite its real usefulness is a concession-and-persistence relationship, which 'Even so' signals. 'Granted' would need to introduce the concession itself rather than follow it, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the usefulness was replaced rather than simply not enough to keep subscribers, so 'Even so' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-375$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The sculpture's rough, unfinished appearance is not the result of neglect. ______ the artist deliberately left tool marks visible to emphasize the carving process.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Rather,","Nonetheless,","Admittedly,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence rejects one explanation (neglect) and replaces it with the true one (deliberate artistic choice), a corrective relationship signaled by 'Rather.' 'Admittedly' would wrongly concede the neglect explanation as true, and 'Nonetheless' implies the deliberate choice persisted despite an obstacle rather than replacing a false explanation, so 'Rather' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-376$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The algorithm's accuracy plateaus once the training set exceeds roughly ten million examples, a point of diminishing returns. ______ beyond that threshold, adding more data barely improves its performance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In other words,","Namely,","In short,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence just paraphrases the single idea—diminishing returns past a threshold—in simpler words, which 'In other words' signals precisely. 'Namely' would introduce a specific named example rather than a paraphrase, and 'In short' implies condensing several prior points into a brief conclusion rather than restating one idea, so 'In other words' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-377$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$In coastal towns, rising sea levels have forced many residents to relocate inland over the past decade. ______ in some inland floodplains, the same decade has seen a surge of new residential development despite the flood risk.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Conversely,","However,","Instead,","Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The inland surge in development is the exact opposite of the coastal retreat just described, a parallel reversal that 'Conversely' captures precisely. 'However' marks contrast more generally without that explicit mirror-opposite framing, and 'Instead' wrongly implies the coastal retreat is being replaced rather than mirrored by an opposite trend elsewhere, so 'Conversely' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-378$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Historians have long criticized the treaty's vague wording as an invitation to future disputes. ______ the treaty held firm for nearly a century without a single major violation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,","Nonetheless,","In contrast,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The treaty's long, violation-free durability is a direct contrast with historians' criticism of its vague wording—two coexisting facts in tension—which plain 'Yet' signals. 'In contrast' is reserved for comparing two distinct subjects side by side, not one treaty's own track record, so 'Yet' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-379$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Bypass surgery carries well-documented risks for patients over eighty. ______ for Mr. Alvarez, forgoing treatment carried a far higher risk of sudden cardiac failure.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Admittedly,","Nonetheless,","In contrast,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence first concedes a general truth (surgery's documented risk) before pivoting to a specific case where the opposite calculus applies, which the concession-before-claim 'Admittedly' signals. 'Nonetheless' would need to follow the conceded fact with something happening despite it, not pivot to a specific contrasting case, so 'Admittedly' is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-380$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sourdough starter is a mixture of flour and water that captures wild yeast and bacteria from the surrounding air.
• Lactobacillus bacteria in the starter ferment sugars in the dough and produce lactic acid as a byproduct.
• The lactic acid is responsible for sourdough bread's distinctive tangy flavor.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why sourdough bread tastes tangy. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Lactobacillus bacteria in the starter produce lactic acid, which gives sourdough bread its distinctive tangy flavor.","Sourdough starter is a mixture of flour and water that captures wild yeast and bacteria from the air.","Sourdough bread has been made using natural fermentation for thousands of years.","Bakers must feed a sourdough starter regularly to keep it active."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names the lactic acid byproduct that directly explains the tangy flavor; the others describe the starter or its history without explaining the taste.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-381$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honeybee scouts perform a figure-eight waggle dance inside the hive after finding a good food source.
• The angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates the direction of the food relative to the sun.
• The duration of the waggling portion of the dance indicates how far away the food source is.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how other bees learn the distance to a food source from the waggle dance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The duration of the waggling portion of the dance tells other bees how far away the food source is.","Honeybee scouts perform a figure-eight dance after finding a good food source.","The angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates the food's direction relative to the sun.","Bees rely on dances to communicate information inside the hive."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer specifically links the dance's duration to communicating distance, which is the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-382$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The aurora borealis occurs when charged particles from the sun collide with gases in Earth's atmosphere.
• Oxygen collisions tend to produce green or red light, while nitrogen collisions tend to produce blue or purple light.
• The aurora is most visible near the magnetic poles, where Earth's magnetic field funnels the charged particles.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what causes the different colors seen in the aurora borealis. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Oxygen collisions tend to produce green or red light, while nitrogen collisions tend to produce blue or purple light.","The aurora borealis occurs when charged particles from the sun collide with atmospheric gases.","The aurora is most visible near Earth's magnetic poles.","Earth's magnetic field funnels charged particles toward the poles."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer identifies which gases produce which colors, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-383$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coast redwoods can grow bark more than a foot thick at the base of the trunk.
• The thick bark contains very little resin, which makes it difficult for fire to ignite.
• The bark also provides insulation that protects the tree's living tissue from heat during a forest fire.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why coast redwoods often survive forest fires that kill other trees. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Their thick, low-resin bark resists ignition and insulates the tree's living tissue from the heat of a forest fire.","Coast redwoods can grow bark more than a foot thick at the base of the trunk.","Coast redwoods are among the tallest trees in the world.","Forest fires periodically sweep through coastal redwood forests."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer combines both the fire-resistant and insulating properties of the bark to explain the trees' survival.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-384$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Early clothing fasteners like buttons and hooks required time and dexterity to fasten one at a time.
• The modern zipper, patented in the early twentieth century, uses interlocking teeth that close with a single pull of a slider.
• Zippers quickly became popular for boots, jackets, and later a wide range of clothing.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the zipper was a convenient improvement over earlier fasteners. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Unlike buttons and hooks, which had to be fastened one at a time, the zipper closes an entire seam with a single pull of a slider.","The modern zipper was patented in the early twentieth century.","Zippers quickly became popular for boots and jackets.","Buttons and hooks were used on clothing for centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly contrasts the zipper's one-motion closure with the slower, one-at-a-time fastening it replaced.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-385$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Many bat species are active at night and hunt flying insects in near-total darkness.
• Bats emit high-frequency calls and listen for the echoes that bounce off nearby objects.
• By analyzing the timing and pitch of the returning echoes, a bat can judge an object's distance, size, and movement.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how bats are able to hunt successfully in the dark. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By emitting calls and analyzing the timing and pitch of the returning echoes, bats can judge the distance, size, and movement of nearby objects even in darkness.","Many bat species are active at night and hunt flying insects.","Bats emit high-frequency calls that are often inaudible to humans.","Bats are found on every continent except Antarctica."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the mechanism, echo analysis, that lets bats hunt without relying on sight.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-386$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1928, Alexander Fleming returned from vacation to find a petri dish of bacteria contaminated with mold.
• Fleming noticed that the bacteria near the mold had been killed, while bacteria elsewhere on the dish had grown normally.
• This chance observation led Fleming to isolate the substance, later named penicillin, that the mold produced.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how accidental Fleming's discovery of penicillin was. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Fleming's discovery began with an accidental observation: bacteria near a stray mold contamination had died, while bacteria elsewhere on the dish had not.","Alexander Fleming isolated the substance that the mold produced and later named it penicillin.","Fleming returned from vacation in 1928 to examine his petri dishes.","Penicillin became one of the most important medical discoveries of the twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer highlights the unplanned contamination and chance observation that make the discovery accidental.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-387$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Arctic tern breeds in the Arctic during the northern summer.
• It then migrates to spend the southern summer near Antarctica, following a round-trip route of roughly 60,000 miles.
• This is considered the longest annual migration of any animal.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how extreme the Arctic tern's migration distance is. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Arctic tern's round-trip migration covers roughly 60,000 miles each year, the longest annual journey of any animal.","The Arctic tern breeds in the Arctic during the northern summer.","The Arctic tern spends the southern summer near Antarctica.","Many bird species migrate long distances between breeding and non-breeding seasons."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific extreme distance and its record-setting status, matching the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-388$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Before standardized shipping containers, cargo was loaded and unloaded piece by piece by dockworkers, a slow and labor-intensive process.
• Standardized steel containers, introduced in the mid-twentieth century, could be lifted by crane directly between ships, trains, and trucks.
• This standardization cut loading times from days to hours and sharply reduced shipping costs.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much the standardized shipping container improved the efficiency of global trade. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By allowing cargo to move directly between ships, trains, and trucks, standardized containers cut loading times from days to hours and sharply reduced shipping costs.","Before standardized containers, cargo was loaded and unloaded piece by piece by dockworkers.","Standardized steel shipping containers were introduced in the mid-twentieth century.","Global trade expanded significantly over the second half of the twentieth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific efficiency gains, time and cost, that fulfill the goal of emphasizing improved efficiency.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-389$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Before the printing press, books were copied by hand, a slow process that kept them scarce and expensive.
• Gutenberg's movable-type press, developed around 1440, allowed identical pages to be printed quickly and in large numbers.
• Within decades, the price of books fell sharply, and significantly more people across Europe learned to read.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the printing press contributed to rising literacy rates in Europe. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the press could print identical pages quickly and in large numbers, book prices fell sharply, and significantly more people learned to read.","Before the printing press, books were copied by hand, a slow and expensive process.","Gutenberg developed his movable-type press around 1440.","Handwritten books were scarce and valuable before the printing press existed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer links the press's output and falling prices directly to the rise in literacy, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-390$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Everest's summit is covered by a layer of snow and ice whose depth can change from year to year.
• Surveyors must also account for slight shifts in the Himalayas caused by ongoing tectonic activity.
• Different surveying expeditions have measured the mountain's height as anywhere from about 29,000 to just over 29,035 feet.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why different surveys have measured slightly different heights for Mount Everest. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the snow and ice layer on the summit varies and the Himalayas shift slightly due to tectonic activity, surveys have measured the mountain's height differently.","Mount Everest's summit is covered by a layer of snow and ice.","Different surveying expeditions have measured the mountain's height as anywhere from about 29,000 to just over 29,035 feet.","The Himalayas were formed by the collision of tectonic plates."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the two reasons, variable snow depth and tectonic shifts, that explain the measurement differences; the choice listing heights from about 29,000 to just over 29,035 feet merely restates the discrepancy without explaining it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-391$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Scientists drill long cylindrical cores of ice from the Antarctic ice sheet, where layers of snow have compacted into ice over hundreds of thousands of years.
• Tiny bubbles of ancient air trapped in each layer preserve a sample of the atmosphere at the time the snow fell.
• By measuring carbon dioxide levels in these trapped air bubbles, researchers can reconstruct how the atmosphere's composition has changed over time.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how ice cores allow scientists to study the ancient atmosphere. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tiny bubbles of ancient air trapped in each ice layer preserve a sample of the atmosphere from when the snow fell, letting researchers measure past carbon dioxide levels.","Scientists drill long cylindrical cores of ice from the Antarctic ice sheet.","Layers of snow in Antarctica have compacted into ice over hundreds of thousands of years.","Antarctica holds the majority of the world's fresh water in its ice sheet."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the trapped-air mechanism that lets scientists study the ancient atmosphere, directly fulfilling the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-392$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Flamingos are often seen standing on a single leg for extended periods, including while asleep.
• Researchers found that flamingos use less muscular effort to balance on one leg than on two, likely due to how their leg joints lock in place.
• Standing on one leg also allows a flamingo to keep the other leg tucked against its body, reducing heat loss in cold water.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a functional benefit of the one-legged stance beyond balance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tucking one leg against the body while standing on the other helps a flamingo reduce heat loss in cold water.","Flamingos are often seen standing on a single leg for extended periods.","Flamingos use less muscular effort to balance on one leg than on two.","Flamingo leg joints can lock in place while the bird stands."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal asks for a benefit beyond balance; only the correct answer names the heat-conservation benefit, while the other relevant-sounding choices concern balance, not warmth.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-393$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Rosetta Stone, discovered in 1799, contains the same decree inscribed in three scripts: hieroglyphic, Demotic, and ancient Greek.
• Scholars at the time could already read ancient Greek fluently.
• By comparing the Greek text to the hieroglyphic text word by word, researchers were gradually able to decode the meanings of Egyptian hieroglyphs.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the Rosetta Stone allowed scholars to decipher Egyptian hieroglyphs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because scholars could already read the stone's Greek inscription, comparing it word by word to the hieroglyphic text let them gradually decode the hieroglyphs' meanings.","The Rosetta Stone was discovered in 1799.","The Rosetta Stone contains the same decree in three different scripts.","Ancient Greek was already a well-understood language among scholars of the time."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the comparative method, using known Greek to decode unknown hieroglyphs, that fulfills the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-394$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The SR-71 Blackbird reconnaissance jet cruised at speeds above Mach 3, generating extreme frictional heat on its skin.
• Engineers coated the aircraft's titanium skin with a special black paint.
• The black paint helped the skin radiate away excess heat more effectively and also reduced the aircraft's radar visibility.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the SR-71's black paint was functionally useful, not merely a stylistic choice. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The black paint helped the Blackbird's skin radiate away the extreme heat generated at high speed more effectively.","The SR-71 Blackbird cruised at speeds above Mach 3.","Engineers coated the aircraft's titanium skin with a special black paint.","The SR-71 Blackbird was used for reconnaissance missions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer ties the paint to a specific functional benefit, heat radiation, rather than simply restating that the paint was applied.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-395$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venice is built on a cluster of marshy islands in a lagoon, and its foundations rest on wooden piles driven into soft sediment.
• Decades of groundwater pumping in the twentieth century caused the sediment beneath the city to compact and settle.
• At the same time, global sea levels have been rising, increasing the frequency of flooding in the city's lowest-lying squares.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain that Venice's flooding problem results from two separate factors. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Venice's flooding stems both from the city's own sediment settling due to past groundwater pumping and from the broader rise in global sea levels.","Venice is built on a cluster of marshy islands in a lagoon.","Venice's foundations rest on wooden piles driven into soft sediment.","Global sea levels have been rising in recent decades."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names both contributing factors, local subsidence and global sea-level rise, as the goal requires.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-396$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Cats commonly purr when content, such as while being petted or resting near their owners.
• Cats have also been observed purring while injured, giving birth, or near death.
• Researchers have proposed that purring may aid healing through vibration, but no single explanation has been confirmed for all cases.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that scientists do not fully understand why cats purr. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although purring may aid healing through vibration, researchers have not confirmed a single explanation that accounts for all the situations in which cats purr.","Cats commonly purr when content, such as while being petted.","Cats have been observed purring while injured or giving birth.","Purring may help cats heal through the vibrations it produces."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explicitly states that no confirmed explanation exists, matching the goal of emphasizing scientific uncertainty.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-397$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Many forest trees are connected underground by networks of mycorrhizal fungi attached to their roots.
• Through these fungal networks, a tree can send sugars to a neighboring tree that is shaded or struggling.
• Some researchers have also found evidence that trees send chemical warning signals through the same network when attacked by insects.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a specific way trees use the fungal network to help a struggling neighbor. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A tree can send sugars through the fungal network to a neighboring tree that is shaded or struggling.","Many forest trees are connected underground by networks of mycorrhizal fungi.","Trees may send chemical warning signals through the fungal network when attacked by insects.","Mycorrhizal fungi attach themselves to the roots of forest trees."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer describes the specific nutrient-sharing behavior that helps a struggling neighboring tree, as the goal requires.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-398$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Many people assume the Leaning Tower of Pisa was designed to lean.
• In fact, the tower began tilting during construction in the twelfth century because its foundation was laid in unstable, soft soil.
• Engineers in the twentieth century installed counterweights and extracted soil from beneath the foundation to stabilize the tilt without making the tower perfectly straight.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the common misconception that the tower's lean was intentional. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Contrary to popular belief, the tower began tilting during construction because its foundation was laid in unstable, soft soil.","Engineers in the twentieth century installed counterweights to stabilize the tower.","Soil was extracted from beneath the tower's foundation to reduce its tilt.","The Leaning Tower of Pisa was built in the twelfth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly states that the lean was accidental, correcting the misconception named in the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-399$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Roman aqueducts carried water for miles using a continuous, gentle downhill slope, often elevated on stone arches.
• Maya water systems in cities like Palenque instead used covered, pressurized channels that could run water uphill over short distances.
• Both civilizations built their systems primarily to supply cities with drinking water and support agriculture.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight a key engineering difference between the Roman and Maya water systems. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["While Roman aqueducts relied on a continuous downhill slope, Maya systems used covered, pressurized channels that could move water uphill.","Roman aqueducts carried water for miles, often elevated on stone arches.","Maya water systems were used in cities such as Palenque.","Both civilizations built water systems to supply their cities with drinking water."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer directly contrasts the two engineering approaches, slope-based versus pressurized, as the goal requires.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-400$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Most flying insects beat all of their wings using a shared set of muscles, limiting independent control.
• Dragonflies have separate muscles controlling each of their four wings individually.
• This independent control lets dragonflies hover, fly backward, and change direction abruptly while chasing prey.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how dragonflies achieve such precise aerial maneuverability. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because separate muscles control each of their four wings individually, dragonflies can hover, fly backward, and change direction abruptly.","Most flying insects beat all of their wings using a shared set of muscles.","Dragonflies have four wings, unlike many other flying insects.","Dragonflies often chase prey while in flight."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer ties the independent wing-muscle control to the specific maneuvers, explaining the mechanism the goal asks for.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-401$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Atacama Desert in Chile receives less than half an inch of rainfall per year, making it one of the driest places on Earth.
• Dry air contains little water vapor, which would otherwise absorb and distort incoming light and radio signals.
• Several major astronomical observatories, including large radio telescope arrays, have been built in the Atacama because of these conditions.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why astronomers chose the Atacama Desert as a site for major observatories. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because the desert's extremely dry air contains little water vapor to distort incoming light and radio signals, astronomers have built major observatories there.","The Atacama Desert in Chile receives less than half an inch of rainfall per year.","The Atacama Desert is one of the driest places on Earth.","Several major astronomical observatories have been built in the Atacama Desert."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explains the causal link between dry air and clearer observations, matching the goal of explaining why astronomers chose the site.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-402$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Many people assume that international internet traffic travels mainly through satellites.
• In reality, a vast network of fiber-optic cables laid along the ocean floor carries the large majority of international data.
• These submarine cables can transmit far more data, more quickly and cheaply, than satellite links can.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct the misconception that satellites carry most international internet traffic. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Contrary to popular belief, a vast network of fiber-optic cables laid along the ocean floor, not satellites, carries the large majority of international internet traffic.","Submarine cables can transmit data more quickly and cheaply than satellite links can.","Many people assume that international internet traffic travels mainly through satellites.","Fiber-optic cables are laid along the ocean floor to connect continents."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explicitly states the correct fact in contrast to the misconception, fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-403$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Korean printers cast movable metal type and used it to print the Jikji, a Buddhist text, in 1377.
• Johannes Gutenberg's movable-type press in Europe is often credited as the first of its kind, beginning around 1440.
• The Korean system used individual characters for a script with thousands of characters, making large-scale printing more difficult than it was for the European alphabet.$q$, $q$The student wants to challenge the common assumption that Gutenberg's press was the first movable-type printing technology. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Korean printers were already using movable metal type to print the Jikji in 1377, more than sixty years before Gutenberg's press appeared in Europe.","Johannes Gutenberg's movable-type press is often credited as the first of its kind.","The Korean printing system had to account for a script with thousands of characters.","European printing relied on an alphabet with a comparatively small number of characters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific date showing Korean movable type predated Gutenberg's press, directly challenging the assumption named in the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-404$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Before the Panama Canal opened in 1914, a ship traveling between New York and San Francisco had to sail around the southern tip of South America.
• That route added roughly 8,000 miles compared with crossing through the Panama Canal.
• The canal uses a system of locks to raise and lower ships between sea level and a man-made lake roughly 85 feet above sea level.$q$, $q$The student wants to quantify how much distance the Panama Canal saved on a specific shipping route. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["By allowing ships to avoid sailing around South America, the canal shortened the New York-to-San Francisco route by roughly 8,000 miles.","The Panama Canal opened in 1914.","The canal uses a system of locks to raise and lower ships to a lake roughly 85 feet above sea level.","Ships traveling between the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans once had to sail around South America."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer states the specific route and the exact distance saved; the choice about ships once sailing around South America describes the old route without the quantified comparison the goal requires.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-405$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Carbon dating measures the decay of carbon-14, a radioactive isotope, to estimate the age of organic material.
• Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years, meaning half of it decays in that span.
• Because so little carbon-14 remains after roughly 50,000 years, the method becomes unreliable for dating objects older than that.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a specific limitation of carbon dating as a method. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because so little carbon-14 remains after roughly 50,000 years, carbon dating becomes unreliable for objects older than that.","Carbon dating measures the decay of carbon-14 to estimate an object's age.","Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years.","Because carbon-14 decays at a fixed, known rate, scientists can use it to estimate an object's age."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names the specific age threshold beyond which the method becomes unreliable, directly stating a limitation; the others explain how carbon dating works or cite a related fact without identifying where it breaks down.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-406$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1971, computer engineer Ray Tomlinson sent what is widely considered the first email between two separate computers on a network.
• Tomlinson later said he could not remember the exact content of the message, though he believed it was something unremarkable like a string of letters.
• The @ symbol Tomlinson chose to separate the username from the computer's name is still used in email addresses today.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the first email's content was unremarkable compared to its lasting significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tomlinson could not even recall the exact content of his 1971 message, likely an unremarkable string of letters, yet the system he built still underlies email today.","In 1971, Ray Tomlinson sent what is widely considered the first email between two computers.","Tomlinson chose the @ symbol to separate the username from the computer's name.","The @ symbol is still used in email addresses today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explicitly pairs the forgettable content with the system's lasting significance, matching the specific contrast the goal asks for.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-407$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Leonardo da Vinci often blended colors and tones gradually, a technique called sfumato, to soften outlines and create a hazy depth.
• Sandro Botticelli, by contrast, favored crisp, clearly defined outlines and flat areas of color with little blending.
• Both painters worked in Florence during the Italian Renaissance and were influenced by some of the same patrons.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight a specific stylistic difference between da Vinci's and Botticelli's painting techniques. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["While da Vinci blended colors gradually to create a hazy depth, Botticelli favored crisp outlines and flat areas of color with little blending.","Leonardo da Vinci's technique of blending colors gradually is called sfumato.","Both painters worked in Florence during the Italian Renaissance.","Da Vinci and Botticelli were influenced by some of the same patrons."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer names both painters' contrasting techniques side by side, fulfilling the specific stylistic-contrast goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-408$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Gothic cathedrals were built with tall stone walls containing large stained-glass windows, which left less stone to bear the roof's weight.
• Flying buttresses are external stone arches that lean against the upper walls from outside the building.
• By transferring some of the roof's outward thrust to the ground through these external arches, flying buttresses allowed the walls themselves to be thinner and support larger windows.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the specific structural problem that flying buttresses solved. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Flying buttresses transferred the roof's outward thrust to the ground, allowing walls weakened by large stained-glass windows to remain stable even though they were thinner.","Flying buttresses are external stone arches that lean against the upper walls from outside the building.","Gothic cathedrals were built with tall stone walls containing large stained-glass windows.","Flying buttresses transferred some of a roof's outward thrust to the ground."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer connects the buttresses' thrust-transferring function to the specific problem of walls weakened by large windows; the choice about tall walls with large windows states the problem alone, the choice about transferring some outward thrust states the mechanism too generally, and the choice about external stone arches just describes what buttresses are.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-409$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Dead Sea's water is roughly ten times saltier than ordinary ocean water, which is why almost no fish or plants can survive in it.
• The Dead Sea earned its name because of this near-total absence of aquatic life.
• Despite its name, the Dead Sea is a popular tourist destination, largely because its extreme salinity makes swimmers float with unusual ease.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight an ironic contrast between the Dead Sea's name and its appeal to tourists. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Despite being named for its near-total absence of life, the Dead Sea draws tourists precisely because its extreme salinity lets them float with unusual ease.","The Dead Sea's water is roughly ten times saltier than ordinary ocean water.","Almost no fish or plants can survive in the Dead Sea.","The Dead Sea earned its name because of its near-total absence of aquatic life."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer explicitly juxtaposes the 'dead' name with the lively tourist appeal, capturing the irony the goal asks for.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-410$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Pantheon in Rome, completed around 128 CE, still has the world's largest unreinforced concrete dome.
• Roman concrete included volcanic ash, which modern researchers have found reacts with seawater and rainwater to grow new mineral crystals that fill in tiny cracks over time.
• Modern concrete, by contrast, typically weakens and cracks further once water penetrates it.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Roman concrete structures like the Pantheon have survived for nearly two thousand years. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Volcanic ash in Roman concrete reacts with water to grow new mineral crystals that fill in cracks over time, unlike modern concrete, which tends to weaken as water penetrates it.","The Pantheon in Rome was completed around 128 CE.","The Pantheon still has the world's largest unreinforced concrete dome.","Modern concrete typically weakens and cracks further once water penetrates it."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct answer gives the specific self-healing mechanism, and contrasts it with modern concrete, explaining the longevity the goal asks about.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-411$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The hiking trail was closed after a landslide. ______ the park service posted detour signs for visitors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,","For example,","Similarly,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The signs are a consequence of the closure, so 'As a result' is logical.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-412$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Mia studied for weeks. ______ she earned the highest score in her class.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Meanwhile,","Nevertheless,","Instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Her high score follows from her studying, so a cause-effect transition fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-413$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Dogs have been domesticated for thousands of years. ______ cats were domesticated much later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,","Therefore,","Likewise,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentences contrast the long history of dogs with the later history of cats.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-414$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Many birds migrate south in winter. ______ the Arctic tern travels from pole to pole each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Otherwise,","For example,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The Arctic tern is an example of a migrating bird.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-415$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The restaurant is famous for its desserts. ______ its lunch menu is also very popular.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","In contrast,","Instead,","Moreover,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence adds further praise, so an additive transition fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-416$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The team practiced in the rain all week. ______ they were ready for Saturday's game.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Rather,","Previously,","Alternatively,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Practice led to readiness.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-417$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Solar panels have become much cheaper over the past decade. ______ the share of homes that use them has risen steadily.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Accordingly,","In other words,","Instead,","Regardless,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Lower cost plausibly explains the rise in adoption.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-418$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The novel received mixed reviews on its release. ______ it has since become one of the most widely taught books in the country.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Similarly,","For example,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The later success contrasts with the mixed initial reception.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-419$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Most plants need sunlight to grow. ______ some fungi thrive in complete darkness.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Similarly,","For instance,","As a result,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Fungi that thrive in the dark contrast with plants needing sunlight.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-420$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Researchers suspected that the new medication would help patients sleep. ______ the trial showed no improvement over a placebo.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Specifically,","However,","Accordingly,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The trial result contradicted the expectation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-421$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The city introduced a bike-sharing program last year. ______ it added 40 miles of protected bike lanes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Additionally,","Nevertheless,","Conversely,","Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding bike lanes is another step in the same direction.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-422$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Octopuses can squeeze through tiny openings because they have no bones. ______ a large octopus can pass through a hole no bigger than its eye.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Rather,","Meanwhile,","Otherwise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence strengthens the first with a striking example.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-423$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was designed to withstand strong winds. ______ it was not tested against earthquakes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Likewise,","However,","Moreover,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The wind design contrasts with the lack of earthquake testing.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-424$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Early telescopes produced blurry images of distant objects. ______ improvements in lens grinding eventually yielded much sharper views.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For instance,","Instead,","Conversely,","Over time,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The improvement happened gradually.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-425$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Some students prefer to study in silence. ______ many others concentrate better with background music.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","For example,","By contrast,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentences contrast two preferences.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-426$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The study found no link between screen time and academic performance. ______ the authors caution that the study measured only total hours, not the content viewed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Even so,","Similarly,","For this reason,","In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The caution qualifies the null finding, so a concessive transition fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-427$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The orchestra's budget has shrunk by a third. ______ it has cut the number of concerts it offers rather than reducing the pay of its musicians.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","In contrast,","Even so,","To cope,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Cutting concerts is the orchestra's response to the budget cut.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-428$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Critics initially dismissed the painter as a mere imitator of her teacher. ______ her mature style, with its bold geometry, shows little trace of that influence.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In sum,","As a result,","Similarly,","In truth,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence corrects the earlier dismissal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-429$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Salt lowers the freezing point of water, which is why it is spread on icy roads. ______ it becomes ineffective when temperatures fall far below zero.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise","Hence","Thus","Yet"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The limitation contrasts with the effectiveness just described.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-430$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The mayor promised to reduce commute times. ______ the plan she proposed, which adds only one express bus line, would likely affect a small share of commuters.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Subsequently,","In practice,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence contrasts the promise with the likely real-world effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-431$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The museum was crowded on Saturday. ______ on Tuesday it was nearly empty.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By comparison,","As a result,","In particular,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Saturday and Tuesday attendance are being contrasted.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-432$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Fewer than a dozen copies of the book survive. ______ it is considered one of the most valuable books in private hands.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Nevertheless,","Unsurprisingly,","On the other hand,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The scarcity logically explains the value.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-433$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$I forgot my umbrella. ______ I got soaked on the walk home.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Meanwhile,","As a result,","In contrast,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Getting wet is the consequence of forgetting the umbrella.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-434$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The company offered employees a four-day work week. ______ productivity did not decline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Notably,","Instead,","Otherwise,","Alternatively,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence adds a noteworthy result.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-435$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Archaeologists had long assumed that the settlement was abandoned suddenly. ______ new radiocarbon dates suggest that residents left gradually over several decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,","In addition,","Accordingly,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The new dates contradict the long-held assumption.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-436$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$First, whisk the eggs in a bowl. ______ add the milk and stir gently.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Otherwise,","Next,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentences describe sequential steps.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-437$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Satellite data show that the glacier has retreated about 2 kilometers since 1990. ______ the retreat has accelerated in the past decade.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,","Moreover,","Otherwise,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence adds further information in the same direction.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-438$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The author's early essays are widely anthologized. ______ her later work, which is more experimental, has received far less attention.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Similarly,","Specifically,","By comparison,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The two bodies of work are contrasted in how much attention they receive.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-439$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Some reptiles can survive months without food. ______ the Galápagos tortoise can go a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In fact,","Instead,","Alternatively,","Otherwise,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The tortoise's year is a more extreme example of the same capability.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-440$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Local farmers were worried about the drought. ______ they began planting crop varieties that need less water.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,","Consequently,","Likewise,","Nonetheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Planting drought-tolerant crops is a response to the worry.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-441$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$A bridge may appear to be under little stress when it carries no traffic. ______ the weight of the structure itself places enormous loads on its cables.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","For instance,","Likewise,","In reality,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence corrects the appearance described in the first.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-442$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The red panda is a small mammal native to the Himalayas.
• Its diet consists mainly of bamboo.
• It spends most of its time in trees.$q$, $q$The student wants to introduce the red panda to readers unfamiliar with it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Red pandas spend time in trees, which are tall plants.","The red panda, a small mammal native to the Himalayas, spends most of its time in trees and eats mainly bamboo.","The red panda eats bamboo.","Many mammals live in the Himalayas and eat plants."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice introduces the animal with its location, habitat, and diet.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-443$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Great Wall of China stretches over 13,000 miles.
