• Parts of it were built to repel invaders.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the age of the Great Wall. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Parts of the Great Wall were built to repel invaders.","The Great Wall of China stretches over 13,000 miles.","Construction of the Great Wall of China began more than 2,000 years ago.","The Great Wall is in China."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice highlights how old the wall is.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-444$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Nile is about 4,130 miles long.
• The Amazon's length is estimated at about 4,000 miles.
• Measuring a river's length depends on where one places its source.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why rankings of the world's longest river are disputed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Nile is about 4,130 miles long.","The Amazon is nearly as long as the Nile.","Because a river's length depends on where its source is placed, the Nile and the Amazon, at roughly 4,130 and 4,000 miles, are both claimed as the longest.","Rivers can be measured."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The correct choice links the measurement ambiguity to the dispute.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-445$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Jane Goodall began studying chimpanzees in Tanzania in 1960.
• She observed chimpanzees making and using tools.
• Her discovery challenged the belief that only humans use tools.$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the significance of Goodall's discovery. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Goodall began her work in Tanzania in 1960.","Goodall studied chimpanzees for many years.","Chimpanzees live in Tanzania.","By observing chimpanzees make and use tools, Goodall overturned the belief that only humans do so."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice states the discovery and its importance.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-446$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Mariana Trench is the deepest part of the world's oceans.
• Its deepest point is nearly 36,000 feet below the surface.
• Mount Everest is about 29,000 feet tall.$q$, $q$The student wants to make a comparison that conveys the trench's depth. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Mariana Trench's deepest point, nearly 36,000 feet below the surface, lies farther down than Mount Everest, at about 29,000 feet, rises up.","Mount Everest is about 29,000 feet tall.","The ocean is deep.","The Mariana Trench is the deepest part of the oceans."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice makes the comparison using both numbers.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-447$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Researchers studied 200 households that installed smart thermostats.
• The households reduced heating costs by an average of 8%.
• Savings were largest in homes with irregular occupancy schedules.
• The study lasted one winter.$q$, $q$The student wants to present a finding of the study and a limit on it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The study lasted one winter and involved heating.","Smart thermostats reduced costs in homes with irregular occupancy.","Researchers studied 200 households.","Households with smart thermostats cut heating costs by an average of 8%, although the study covered only a single winter."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the correct choice presents the main result and a limitation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-448$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Monarch butterflies travel up to 3,000 miles during migration.
