  ($q$ei-455$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• In 2019, the town replaced its streetlights with LED lamps.
• The change cut the town's lighting energy use by 60%.
• The new lamps also cost more up front than the old ones.
• The upfront cost was repaid through energy savings within four years.$q$, $q$The student wants to persuade readers that the LED switch was a sound investment. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The new LED lamps cost more up front.","LED lamps use less energy than old lamps.","The town replaced its streetlights in 2019.","Although the LED lamps cost more up front, the 60% reduction in the town's lighting energy use repaid that cost within four years."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice acknowledges the cost and shows it was recovered through savings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-456$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Emperor penguins breed during the Antarctic winter.
• Males incubate a single egg on their feet for about two months.
• During this time the males do not eat.$q$, $q$The student wants to illustrate the males' endurance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Emperor penguins live in Antarctica.","During the Antarctic winter, male emperor penguins incubate an egg on their feet for about two months without eating.","Penguins have eggs.","Emperor penguins breed once a year."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice stresses the long, fasting incubation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-457$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• A 2020 survey found that 62% of commuters in the city drive alone.
• The city's transit agency wants to raise ridership.
• Surveyed drivers most often cited unreliable bus schedules as their reason.$q$, $q$The student wants to recommend a course of action to the transit agency. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because most drivers cited unreliable schedules, the transit agency could raise ridership by making its bus service more reliable.","62% of commuters drive alone.","Buses can be unreliable.","The city's transit agency wants to raise ridership."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice recommends an action grounded in the survey's finding.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-458$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Honey never spoils if it is sealed.
• Archaeologists found edible honey in ancient Egyptian tombs.
• Honey is made by bees.$q$, $q$The student wants to give an example of honey's long shelf life. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Honey never spoils.","Honey is sweet.","Archaeologists have found edible honey in ancient Egyptian tombs.","Honey is made by bees."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The correct choice gives a specific example.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-459$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The printing press was invented around 1440 by Johannes Gutenberg.
• Before it, books were copied by hand.
• By 1500, millions of printed books had been produced across Europe.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the press's effect on book production. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Books were copied by hand.","Gutenberg invented the printing press.","The press was invented around 1440.","After Gutenberg's invention of the printing press around 1440, millions of books were printed across Europe by 1500, replacing laborious copying by hand."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice combines the before-and-after effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-460$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Regular exercise lowers the risk of heart disease.
• A study of 5,000 adults found that those who walked 30 minutes a day had a 20% lower risk.
• The study did not include adults over 65.$q$, $q$The student wants to report the study's result while noting its limited scope. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["In a study of 5,000 adults, walking 30 minutes a day was linked to a 20% lower risk of heart disease, although the study did not include adults over 65.","Adults who walk are healthier.","Exercise lowers risk of heart disease.","The study of 5,000 adults did not include older people."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice states the result and the limitation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-461$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Frida Kahlo painted 55 self-portraits.
• She said she painted herself because she was often alone.
• Many of the portraits show her pain from a bus accident.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain her reason for painting self-portraits. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Kahlo was an artist.","Kahlo, who painted 55 self-portraits, explained that she painted herself because she was often alone.","Kahlo was in a bus accident.","Kahlo painted self-portraits of her pain."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice states her stated reason.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-462$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.
• It covers about 3.6 million square miles.
• It spans parts of eleven countries.$q$, $q$The student wants to state the Sahara's size. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Sahara is hot.","The Sahara spans eleven countries.","The Sahara covers about 3.6 million square miles.","The Sahara is a desert."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the correct choice gives the size.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-463$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Dr. Aliyu studies how bilingual children switch between languages.
• Her team found that bilingual children outperform monolingual peers on tasks requiring attention control.
• The researchers suggest that frequent language switching trains this ability.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a proposed reason for the observed advantage. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Dr. Aliyu's team suggests that bilingual children outperform monolingual peers on attention-control tasks because frequent language switching trains this ability.","Bilingual children outperform monolingual peers.","Dr. Aliyu studies bilingual children.","Switching languages is common."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice pairs the finding with the proposed explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-464$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Apollo 11 mission landed on the Moon on July 20, 1969.
• Neil Armstrong was the first person to walk on the lunar surface.
• Buzz Aldrin joined him about 19 minutes later.$q$, $q$The student wants to name the first person on the Moon and when it happened. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Many astronauts have walked on the Moon.","On July 20, 1969, Neil Armstrong became the first person to walk on the Moon.","Buzz Aldrin joined Armstrong 19 minutes later.","Apollo 11 landed on the Moon."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice includes both who and when.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-465$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Saffron comes from the stigmas of crocus flowers.
• Each flower yields only three stigmas, which must be picked by hand.
• About 150,000 flowers are needed to produce one kilogram of saffron.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why saffron is so expensive. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Saffron is picked by hand.","Saffron comes from crocus flowers.","Saffron is a spice.","Because each crocus yields only three hand-picked stigmas, about 150,000 flowers are needed to make one kilogram of saffron, which accounts for its high price."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice ties the labor and quantity to the cost.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-466$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Bamboo is a type of grass.
• Some species grow up to 35 inches in a single day.
• It is used for construction, furniture, and food.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize bamboo's growth rate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Bamboo is used for construction.","Bamboo is a type of grass.","Bamboo is a plant.","Some species of bamboo, a type of grass, can grow up to 35 inches in a single day."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice highlights the rapid growth.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-467$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Statue of Liberty was a gift from France.
• It was dedicated in 1886.
• It stands on Liberty Island in New York Harbor.$q$, $q$The student wants to say where the Statue of Liberty is. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["It is a famous statue.","The Statue of Liberty stands on Liberty Island in New York Harbor.","It was a gift from France.","It was dedicated in 1886."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the correct choice gives the location.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-468$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Many cities have a 'heat island effect,' in which urban areas are warmer than nearby rural ones.
• Dark pavement and buildings absorb more heat than vegetation does.
• A study found that adding tree cover lowered neighborhood temperatures by up to 5 degrees Fahrenheit.$q$, $q$The student wants to propose a way to reduce the heat island effect, supported by evidence. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Trees reduce temperatures.","Because dark surfaces absorb more heat than vegetation does, cities might reduce the heat island effect by planting trees, which one study found lowered neighborhood temperatures by up to 5 degrees Fahrenheit.","Cities have a heat island effect.","Dark pavement is hot."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice gives a solution, its rationale, and evidence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-469$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The cheetah is the fastest land animal.
• It can reach 60 miles per hour in about three seconds.
• It can sustain this speed for only short distances.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe a limit on the cheetah's speed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The cheetah runs 60 miles per hour.","Although the cheetah can reach 60 miles per hour in about three seconds, it can sustain that speed only over short distances.","The cheetah is the fastest land animal.","Cheetahs are quick."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice includes the limitation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-470$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed that mold had killed bacteria in one of his dishes.
• He identified the mold as Penicillium and named its active substance penicillin.
• Penicillin was not mass-produced until the 1940s.$q$, $q$The student wants to convey that the discovery's impact came years after it was made. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Penicillin was mass-produced.","Fleming named the mold Penicillium.","Fleming discovered penicillin in 1928.","Although Fleming noticed in 1928 that a mold had killed bacteria, penicillin was not mass-produced until the 1940s."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correct choice contrasts the 1928 discovery with the later mass production.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-471$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Early telescopes could resolve only the brightest stars. ______ modern instruments detect objects billions of light-years away.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Therefore,","Specifically,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence expresses contrast relative to the first sentence, so "However" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-472$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Hubble Space Telescope launched in 1990.
• A flaw in its main mirror blurred its first images.
• Astronauts installed corrective optics in 1993.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the problem with the telescope's images and how it was fixed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A flaw in Hubble's main mirror blurred its early images until astronauts installed corrective optics in 1993.","Launched in 1990, the Hubble Space Telescope produced blurry images because of a flaw in its main mirror.","The Hubble Space Telescope launched in 1990.","The Hubble Space Telescope, launched in 1990, was repaired when astronauts installed corrective optics in 1993."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-473$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jazz emerged in New Orleans in the early twentieth century.
• It blends African rhythms with European harmony.
• Improvisation is central to the music.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what jazz combines and a defining feature of the music. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Jazz emerged in New Orleans in the early twentieth century.","Jazz, which emerged in New Orleans in the early twentieth century, centers on improvisation.","Jazz emerged in New Orleans in the early twentieth century as a blend of African rhythms and European harmony.","Combining African rhythms with European harmony, jazz places improvisation at the center of the music."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-474$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Critics praised the film's cinematography. ______ audiences found the plot confusing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Furthermore,","Similarly,","In contrast,","Subsequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence expresses contrast relative to the first sentence, so "In contrast" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-475$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Tardigrades are microscopic animals.
• They can survive extreme heat, cold, and radiation.
• Some have survived exposure to the vacuum of outer space.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize their toughness and a striking example of their survival. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Tardigrades are microscopic animals.","Although tardigrades are microscopic, they can survive extreme heat, cold, and radiation.","Able to survive extreme heat, cold, and radiation, some tardigrades have even survived the vacuum of outer space.","Tardigrades are microscopic animals, yet some have survived exposure to the vacuum of outer space."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-476$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Daniel spent his twenties working as a surveyor. ______ he enrolled in architecture school and changed careers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Thus,","Similarly,","Moreover,","Eventually,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence expresses a sequence of events relative to the first sentence, so "Eventually" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-477$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Language can shift dramatically within a single generation. ______ the word "cool" has acquired entirely new meanings since the 1950s.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","On the other hand,","Eventually,","For example,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence expresses a specific example relative to the first sentence, so "For example" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-478$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city planted thousands of trees along its main avenues. ______ summer temperatures downtown dropped measurably.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Moreover,","In the same way,","On the other hand,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence expresses a cause-and-effect result relative to the first sentence, so "Therefore" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-479$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Sea turtles return to the beach where they hatched to lay their eggs. ______ salmon return to their birth streams to spawn.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","Subsequently,","On the other hand,","For instance,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence expresses a similarity relative to the first sentence, so "Likewise" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-480$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honey never spoils if it is stored in a sealed container.
• Archaeologists have found edible honey in ancient Egyptian tombs.
• Honey's low moisture content and acidity prevent bacterial growth.$q$, $q$The student wants to make the point that honey can last extraordinarily long and explain why. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because its low moisture content and acidity prevent bacterial growth, honey stored in a sealed container never spoils.","Honey stored in a sealed container never spoils, and archaeologists have even found edible honey in ancient Egyptian tombs.","Archaeologists have found edible honey in ancient Egyptian tombs.","Edible honey has been found in ancient Egyptian tombs, a longevity explained by its low moisture content and acidity."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-481$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Wolves were once nearly eradicated from the region. ______ a reintroduction program has restored a stable population.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","On the other hand,","As a result,","Afterward,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence expresses contrast relative to the first sentence, so "On the other hand" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-482$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sourdough bread relies on a starter made of wild yeast and bacteria.
• The starter must be fed flour and water regularly.
• The bacteria give the bread its tangy flavor.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what a sourdough starter is made of and what it does for flavor. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A sourdough starter, made of wild yeast and bacteria, must be fed flour and water regularly.","Sourdough starters must be fed flour and water regularly.","A sourdough starter is made of wild yeast and bacteria, and the bacteria give the bread its tangy flavor.","The starter must be fed flour and water regularly so that its bacteria can give the bread a tangy flavor."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-483$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Researchers first sequenced the plant's genome in 2004. ______ in 2017 they identified the gene responsible for its drought tolerance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Later,","Specifically,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second sentence expresses a sequence of events relative to the first sentence, so "Later" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-484$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Urban planners are experimenting with ways to reduce traffic. ______ some cities have introduced congestion fees for drivers entering downtown.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Consequently,","Nevertheless,","Specifically,","In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second sentence expresses a specific example relative to the first sentence, so "Specifically" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-485$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles each fall.
• They travel from the northern United States and Canada to central Mexico.
• No single butterfly makes the round trip; it takes several generations.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the distance of the migration and the fact that it spans generations. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Monarchs travel from the northern United States and Canada to central Mexico.","Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles each fall, but no single butterfly completes the round trip.","Although monarchs travel from the northern United States and Canada to central Mexico, no single butterfly makes the round trip.","Each fall, monarch butterflies migrate as far as 3,000 miles from the northern United States and Canada to central Mexico."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-486$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Library of Alexandria was founded in the third century BCE.
• It aimed to collect every book in the known world.
• Historians still debate exactly how and when it declined.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the library's ambition and what remains uncertain about it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although the Library of Alexandria aimed to collect every book in the known world, historians still debate how and when it declined.","Founded in the third century BCE, the Library of Alexandria aimed to collect every book in the known world.","The Library of Alexandria was founded in the third century BCE.","Founded in the third century BCE, the Library of Alexandria declined in ways that historians still debate."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-487$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jazz emerged in New Orleans in the early twentieth century.
• It blends African rhythms with European harmony.
• Improvisation is central to the music.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain where and when jazz began and a defining feature of the music. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Combining African rhythms with European harmony, jazz places improvisation at the center of the music.","Jazz blends African rhythms with European harmony.","Jazz emerged in New Orleans in the early twentieth century as a blend of African rhythms and European harmony.","Jazz, which emerged in New Orleans in the early twentieth century, centers on improvisation."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-488$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The violinist trained for years to master the concerto. ______ the pianist devoted equal effort to preparing the accompaniment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Subsequently,","Specifically,","In the same way,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence expresses a similarity relative to the first sentence, so "In the same way" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-489$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries.
• Its total length, including all branches, is about 13,000 miles.
• Much of the surviving wall dates to the Ming dynasty (1368–1644).$q$, $q$The student wants to highlight the wall's enormous length and the age of most of what survives. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Although construction spanned many centuries, much of the surviving Great Wall of China dates to the Ming dynasty.","The Great Wall of China, about 13,000 miles long including its branches, is mostly preserved from the Ming dynasty.","The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries.","Built over many centuries, the Great Wall of China stretches about 13,000 miles when all of its branches are counted."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-490$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The river's water level dropped sharply during the drought. ______ cargo barges could no longer travel upstream.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Similarly,","For instance,","However,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence expresses a cause-and-effect result relative to the first sentence, so "Therefore" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-491$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Mississippi River is about 2,340 miles long.
• It flows through or along the borders of ten states.
• Its watershed drains all or part of 31 states.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize both how many states the river touches and how many states it drains. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Stretching about 2,340 miles, the Mississippi River flows through or along the borders of ten states.","At about 2,340 miles long, the Mississippi River drains all or part of 31 states.","The Mississippi River is about 2,340 miles long.","The Mississippi River flows through or along ten states, and its watershed drains all or part of 31 states."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-492$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Bamboo is a type of grass.
• Some species grow up to three feet in a single day.
• Bamboo is strong enough to be used as scaffolding in parts of Asia.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize bamboo's growth rate and its strength. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though it is a type of grass, bamboo is strong enough to serve as scaffolding in parts of Asia.","Bamboo is a type of grass.","Although bamboo is a type of grass, some species grow up to three feet in a single day.","Some bamboo species grow up to three feet a day, yet the plant is strong enough to be used as scaffolding."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-493$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The new software automatically checks every citation. ______ editors spend far less time on proofreading.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Eventually,","For example,","In addition,","Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second sentence expresses a cause-and-effect result relative to the first sentence, so "Consequently" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-494$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The documentary features rare archival footage. ______ it includes interviews with surviving members of the expedition.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In addition,","However,","Therefore,","Subsequently,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence expresses an additional, supporting point relative to the first sentence, so "In addition" is the logical transition. The other choices signal relationships the sentences do not have.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-495$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Maria Tallchief joined the New York City Ballet in 1947.
• She was the company's first prima ballerina.
• She was a member of the Osage Nation.$q$, $q$The student wants to identify when Tallchief joined the company and her status within it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Maria Tallchief, a member of the Osage Nation, joined the New York City Ballet in 1947.","Maria Tallchief was a member of the Osage Nation.","Maria Tallchief joined the New York City Ballet in 1947 and became its first prima ballerina.","Maria Tallchief, a member of the Osage Nation, was the New York City Ballet's first prima ballerina."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The goal calls for two specific pieces of information from the notes. This choice is the only one that includes both of them; each of the other choices omits one of them or relies on a note that is not relevant to the goal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-496$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Eiffel Tower was completed in 1889.
