• Many kelp forests have shrunk because of warming seas.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the benefits of kelp forests. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Many kelp forests have shrunk because of warming seas.","Kelp forests shelter fish and invertebrates and absorb carbon dioxide from the water.","Kelp forests grow along cold coastlines.","Kelp is a type of seaweed."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$This choice states both benefits directly.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-594$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The printing press was invented around 1440.
• It allowed books to be produced far faster than copying by hand.
• The price of books fell sharply.
• Literacy rates rose over the following centuries.$q$, $q$The student wants to show a chain of effects of the printing press. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Literacy is the ability to read.","The printing press was invented around 1440.","By allowing books to be made far faster than hand copying, the printing press, invented around 1440, lowered book prices and helped raise literacy over the following centuries.","Books were copied by hand before then."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice links invention to price drop to literacy.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-595$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses have three hearts.
• Two pump blood through the gills.
• The third pumps blood to the rest of the body.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the function of the octopus's hearts. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Octopuses are unusual animals.","Blood travels through the gills.","Octopuses have three hearts.","Of an octopus's three hearts, two pump blood through the gills and the third pumps it to the rest of the body."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice divides the hearts' functions clearly.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-596$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Wall of China was built over many centuries.
• Different dynasties built and extended its sections.
• Some sections are made of stone; others are rammed earth.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize that the wall is not uniform. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Many dynasties ruled China.","Earth can be used for building.","Built and extended by different dynasties over many centuries, the Great Wall varies from section to section, some of stone and others of rammed earth.","The Great Wall of China is very long."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice emphasizes variation by linking different builders and materials.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-597$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Solar panels convert sunlight into electricity.
• Their efficiency has improved steadily since the 1970s.
• Costs have dropped by about ninety percent in the last decade.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how affordable solar panels have become. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The cost of solar panels, which convert sunlight into electricity, has dropped by about ninety percent in the last decade.","Sunlight is a renewable resource.","Solar panels convert sunlight into electricity.","Efficiency has improved since the 1970s."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice highlights the cost reduction as the goal requires.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-598$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Dr. Rosalind Franklin was a chemist.
• Her X-ray images of DNA, taken in the early 1950s, revealed its helical structure.
• Her work was used by Watson and Crick without her full knowledge.
• She died in 1958, before the Nobel Prize for the discovery was awarded.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the lack of recognition Franklin received during her lifetime. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Rosalind Franklin was a chemist.","Although Franklin's X-ray images of DNA revealed its helical structure, her work was used without her full knowledge, and she died before the discovery earned a Nobel Prize.","Franklin took X-ray images of DNA in the early 1950s.","Watson and Crick won the Nobel Prize."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice combines her contribution with the missing credit.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-599$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Axolotls are a type of salamander native to Mexico.
• They can regenerate lost limbs, organs, and parts of the brain.
• Scientists study them to understand tissue repair in humans.
• Wild populations are critically endangered.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why scientists study axolotls. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Wild axolotl populations are critically endangered.","Axolotls are native to Mexico.","Because axolotls can regenerate limbs, organs, and even parts of the brain, scientists study them to understand tissue repair in humans.","Axolotls are a type of salamander."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice links the ability to the research motivation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-600$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Silk Road was a network of trade routes linking East Asia and Europe.
• Merchants traded silk, spices, and paper along it.
• Ideas and religions also spread along its paths.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue that the Silk Road's importance went beyond commerce. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Silk was valuable.","The Silk Road, a network of routes linking East Asia and Europe, carried not only silk, spices, and paper but also ideas and religions.","The Silk Road was a network of trade routes.","Merchants traded silk along it."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice contrasts goods with ideas and religions to show breadth.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-601$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Researchers analyzed tree rings from ancient oaks.
• The rings show narrow growth during years of drought.
• Narrow rings cluster around 1100, matching records of famine.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the tree rings support historical records. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Famines occurred in the past.","Oaks can live for centuries.","Researchers analyzed tree rings.","Clusters of narrow tree rings around 1100, which indicate drought, match historical records of famine in the same period."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice ties the evidence to the historical records.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-602$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The red panda lives in the Himalayas.
• It eats mainly bamboo.
• It spends most of its time in trees.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe where the red panda spends its time. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The red panda eats bamboo.","Bamboo is a plant.","The red panda lives in the Himalayas.","The red panda, which eats mainly bamboo, spends most of its time in the trees of the Himalayas."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice answers the goal about location and habits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-603$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Mariana Trench is the deepest part of the world's oceans.
• Its deepest point, Challenger Deep, is nearly 11,000 meters below the surface.
• Only a few crewed expeditions have reached it.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how rarely the trench has been visited. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Challenger Deep is nearly 11,000 meters deep.","The Mariana Trench is the deepest part of the oceans.","The ocean covers most of the planet.","Though Challenger Deep lies nearly 11,000 meters below the surface in the Mariana Trench, only a few crewed expeditions have ever reached it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice highlights how few expeditions have gone there.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-604$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Maple syrup is made from the sap of maple trees.
• Sap is collected in late winter.
• About forty gallons of sap yield one gallon of syrup.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how much sap is needed to make syrup. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Maple syrup is sweet.","Collected in late winter, maple sap must be boiled down at a ratio of about forty gallons to every gallon of syrup.","Maple trees grow in North America.","Sap is collected in late winter."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice emphasizes the ratio, as the goal requires.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-605$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed that mold had killed bacteria in a dish.
• He identified the mold as Penicillium.
• The drug was not mass-produced until the 1940s.
• Penicillin has since saved millions of lives.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the gap between the discovery and its impact. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Fleming noticed in 1928 that Penicillium mold killed bacteria, but penicillin was not mass-produced until the 1940s, after which it saved millions of lives.","Mold can grow in dishes.","Penicillin saved millions of lives.","Fleming identified Penicillium."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice sets the 1928 discovery against the 1940s production and later impact.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-606$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The monarch butterfly migrates up to 3,000 miles.
• It relies on milkweed as the only food for its caterpillars.
• Milkweed has declined across the Midwest due to herbicide use.
• Monarch numbers have fallen sharply.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain a cause of the monarch's decline. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Milkweed grows in the Midwest.","Butterflies are insects.","Because herbicide use has reduced the milkweed on which monarch caterpillars depend, monarch numbers have fallen sharply.","The monarch migrates up to 3,000 miles."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice states the causal chain from herbicide to milkweed to monarchs.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-607$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Bees collect nectar from flowers.
• They turn it into honey.
• Honey is stored in the hive.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how honey is made. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Bees are insects.","Flowers produce nectar.","Bees collect nectar from flowers, turn it into honey, and store it in the hive.","Honey is sweet."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice describes the process in order.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-608$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Jazz began in New Orleans in the early twentieth century.
• It blended African rhythms with European harmonies.
• Improvisation became its defining feature.$q$, $q$The student wants to define jazz's origin and character. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["New Orleans is in Louisiana.","Jazz, which began in early-twentieth-century New Orleans by blending African rhythms with European harmonies, is defined by improvisation.","Jazz is popular worldwide.","Improvisation is spontaneous music."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice covers origin, blend, and defining feature.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-609$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor.
• They support about a quarter of all marine species.
• Rising ocean temperatures cause coral bleaching.
• Bleached corals often die.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the potential consequences of coral loss. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Coral reefs cover little of the ocean floor.","Because coral reefs, which cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, support about a quarter of marine species, the death of bleached corals threatens a disproportionate share of ocean life.","Rising temperatures cause bleaching.","Corals are animals."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice connects the reefs' small area to their outsized importance and the threat.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-610$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Library of Alexandria was a major center of learning in the ancient world.
• It collected scrolls from across the Mediterranean.
• Scholars came to study there.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the library attracted scholars. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Because the Library of Alexandria collected scrolls from across the Mediterranean, scholars came to study at this major center of learning.","Scrolls were made of papyrus.","The library was in Egypt.","Alexandria was a city."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice links the collection to the scholars.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-611$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Penguins are flightless birds.
• Their wings have evolved into flippers.
• This adaptation allows them to swim at speeds up to 15 miles per hour.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how flightlessness benefits penguins. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Flippers are not wings.","Penguins are birds.","Penguins live in cold places.","Although penguins cannot fly, their wings have evolved into flippers that let them swim at up to 15 miles per hour."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice shows the trade-off turning into an advantage.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-612$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The park offers free yoga classes on Saturdays. ______ the classes fill up quickly, so arriving early is recommended.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["For example,","However,","Likewise,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The classes are free but crowded, so 'However' introduces the caveat.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-613$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Studying abroad exposes students to new languages and customs. ______ many return with stronger independence and problem-solving skills.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["In contrast,","Otherwise,","In addition,","Previously,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Independence and problem-solving are further benefits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-614$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The lake looked calm from the shore. ______ beneath the surface, strong currents swirled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Likewise,","In reality,","Similarly,","Therefore,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence contrasts appearance with reality.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-615$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$Initial reports suggested the vaccine was safe for all ages. ______ later trials found that certain groups needed lower doses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Similarly,","However,","Therefore,","Specifically,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The later findings qualify the early claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-616$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The author describes the village in loving detail. ______ she never mentions the poverty that shaped daily life there.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Therefore,","Strikingly,","For example,","Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The omission is surprising given the detail, so 'Strikingly' fits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-617$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The device was designed for surgeons. ______ engineers soon realized it could also assist in deep-sea repairs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Specifically,","Eventually,","Instead,","Nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The new use was realized over time.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-618$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Giraffes are the tallest land animals.
• Adult males can reach 18 feet.
• Their long necks help them reach leaves high in trees.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how giraffes' height helps them eat. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Giraffes are tall.","Giraffes, which can reach 18 feet, use their long necks to reach leaves high in trees.","Trees have leaves.","Adult male giraffes are heavy."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice links height to feeding.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-619$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Tomatoes are botanically fruits.
• They are usually used in savory dishes.
• Many people think of them as vegetables.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why tomatoes cause confusion. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Tomatoes grow on vines.","Savory dishes are common.","Although tomatoes are botanically fruits, they are usually used in savory dishes, so many people think of them as vegetables.","Tomatoes are red."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice explains the source of confusion.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-620$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Elephants live in herds led by an older female.
• The leader remembers locations of water.
• Herds follow her during droughts.$q$, $q$The student wants to describe the herd leader's role. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Elephants live in herds.","An older female leads each elephant herd, remembering where to find water and guiding the herd during droughts.","Droughts are dry periods.","Female elephants are large."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice describes the leader's role directly.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-621$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Dust Bowl was a period of severe dust storms in the 1930s.
• It affected the Great Plains.
• Poor farming practices and drought caused it.
• Thousands of families left their farms.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the causes and consequences of the Dust Bowl. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The Dust Bowl was in the 1930s.","The Great Plains is a region.","Dust storms are windy.","Caused by poor farming practices and drought, the dust storms of the 1930s Dust Bowl drove thousands of Great Plains families from their farms."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice states both cause and consequence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-622$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mount Everest is 29,032 feet tall.
• Climbers attempt it each spring.
• Altitude sickness is a serious risk.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the danger of climbing Everest. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Climbers who attempt Everest's 29,032 feet each spring face the serious risk of altitude sickness.","Spring is a season.","Climbers like challenges.","Everest is tall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice stresses the risk the climbers face.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-623$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Rosetta Stone was found in 1799.
• It bears the same text in three scripts.
• Scholars used it to decipher Egyptian hieroglyphs.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why the Rosetta Stone is significant. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The stone has writing on it.","Egypt has many monuments.","The Rosetta Stone was found in 1799.","Because the Rosetta Stone, found in 1799, bears the same text in three scripts, scholars could use it to decipher Egyptian hieroglyphs."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice ties the three scripts to decipherment.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-624$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Compost is made from decaying organic matter.
• It enriches soil with nutrients.
• It also reduces the amount of waste sent to landfills.$q$, $q$The student wants to present two benefits of composting. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Landfills are large.","Composting, which turns decaying organic matter into material that enriches soil, also reduces the waste sent to landfills.","Compost is made at home.","Soil contains nutrients."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice presents both benefits.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-625$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Wright brothers flew their first powered airplane in 1903.
• The flight lasted twelve seconds.
• It covered 120 feet.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how modest the first flight was. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Airplanes have wings.","The Wright brothers' first powered flight in 1903 lasted just twelve seconds and covered only 120 feet.","The Wright brothers built airplanes.","Flight changed the world."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice highlights the small scale with 'just' and 'only.'$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-626$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Lightning heats the air around it to about 50,000 degrees Fahrenheit.
• This is hotter than the surface of the sun.
• The rapid heating produces thunder.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain what causes thunder. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Lightning heats the surrounding air to about 50,000 degrees Fahrenheit, and the rapid heating produces thunder.","Lightning is bright.","Thunder is loud.","The sun is hot."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice explains the cause of thunder directly.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-627$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Greenland sharks can live for more than 250 years.
• They grow only about one centimeter per year.
• Scientists estimate age by measuring eye lens proteins.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how scientists know the sharks' age. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Greenland sharks live in cold water.","Greenland is an island.","Because Greenland sharks grow only about a centimeter a year, scientists estimate their age, which can exceed 250 years, by measuring proteins in their eye lenses.","Sharks have lenses in their eyes."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice explains the method for age estimation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-628$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sleep helps the brain consolidate memories.
• Students who sleep well after studying perform better on tests.
• Many students stay up late to study.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue that studying late may be counterproductive. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Tests measure learning.","Because sleep helps the brain consolidate memories, students who stay up late to study may perform worse than those who sleep well afterward.","Students study in the evening.","Sleep is important."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice draws the implication from the evidence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-629$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• In 1912, the Titanic sank on its maiden voyage.
• Investigators later found that it carried too few lifeboats.
• Regulations then required lifeboats for every passenger.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how the disaster changed regulations. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Lifeboats are small boats.","The Titanic sank on its maiden voyage.","Investigators look into accidents.","After investigators found that the Titanic, which sank in 1912, carried too few lifeboats, regulations were changed to require lifeboats for every passenger."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice links the investigation's finding to the rule change.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-630$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venus is the hottest planet in the solar system.
• Its atmosphere is mostly carbon dioxide.
• The thick atmosphere traps heat.
• Surface temperatures reach about 900 degrees Fahrenheit.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain why Venus is hotter than Mercury despite being farther from the sun. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Venus, though farther from the sun than Mercury, is hotter because its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere traps heat, pushing surface temperatures to about 900 degrees Fahrenheit.","Venus is the hottest planet.","Mercury is small.","Venus has an atmosphere."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice gives the cause of the difference.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-631$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Researchers surveyed 2,000 commuters.
• Those who cycled to work reported lower stress levels.
• The researchers note that the survey does not prove cycling causes lower stress.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the finding with an appropriate caveat. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Researchers surveyed commuters.","In a survey of 2,000 commuters, cyclists reported lower stress levels, though the researchers caution that the survey does not prove cycling is the cause.","Stress is common.","Cycling is good exercise."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice states the finding and the caveat.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-632$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Amazon rainforest produces a significant share of the world's oxygen.
• Recent research shows that the forest consumes nearly as much oxygen as it produces.
• Its main value is in storing carbon.$q$, $q$The student wants to correct a common misconception. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Forests store carbon.","Although the Amazon is often credited with supplying much of the world's oxygen, research shows it consumes nearly as much as it produces; its main value lies in storing carbon.","The Amazon is large.","Oxygen is a gas."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice states the misconception and corrects it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-633$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Mozart composed his first symphony at age eight.
• He wrote more than 600 works before he died at 35.
• His music is still widely performed.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize how productive Mozart was. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Mozart's music is still widely performed.","Symphonies are long.","Beginning with a symphony at age eight, Mozart wrote more than 600 works before dying at 35.","Mozart died at 35."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The choice highlights output in a short life.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-634$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Seeds can travel by wind, water, or animals.
• Dandelion seeds have light, feathery parachutes.
• These parachutes carry them long distances on the wind.$q$, $q$The student wants to give an example of wind dispersal. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Seeds can travel by water.","Dandelion seeds, which have light, feathery parachutes, are carried long distances on the wind.","Wind is moving air.","Dandelions are weeds."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice gives a specific example of wind dispersal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-635$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The city built a new bike lane downtown.
• Bike commuters increased by 25 percent in six months.
• Car traffic on the same street did not change.$q$, $q$The student wants to present the lane's effect on cycling. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The city built a lane.","Bikes are popular.","Car traffic did not change.","Six months after the city built a downtown bike lane, the number of bike commuters rose 25 percent."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice focuses on the cycling increase.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-636$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Penguins huddle together in winter.
• The huddle keeps them warm.
• They take turns standing at the cold edge.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain how huddling works. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Penguins stay warm in winter by huddling together and taking turns at the cold edge.","Penguins are birds.","Winter is cold.","Penguins live in Antarctica."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The choice describes the practice and its purpose.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-637$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Industrial Revolution began in Britain in the late 1700s.
• Factories drew workers from the countryside to cities.
• Urban populations grew rapidly.
• Housing and sanitation lagged behind.$q$, $q$The student wants to explain the problems caused by urban growth. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["The Industrial Revolution began in Britain.","Cities have people.","Factories are buildings.","As factories drew workers from the countryside, Britain's cities grew faster than their housing and sanitation could handle."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The choice ties growth to inadequate infrastructure.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-638$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.
• It is about the size of the United States.
• Parts of it receive almost no rain.$q$, $q$The student wants to emphasize the Sahara's size and dryness. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Deserts are dry.","The Sahara, the largest hot desert in the world, is about the size of the United States, and parts of it receive almost no rain.","The United States is large.","The Sahara is in Africa."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice includes both size and dryness.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-639$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• A 2019 study found that urban trees lowered nearby temperatures by up to 5 degrees.
• The effect was strongest on streets with continuous tree cover.
• Cities with fewer trees recorded more heat-related illness.$q$, $q$The student wants to argue for planting more street trees. Which choice most effectively does this?$q$, $q$["Cities are warm.","Because urban trees can lower nearby temperatures by up to 5 degrees, especially along streets with continuous cover, cities with more trees may suffer less heat-related illness.","Trees have leaves.","A 2019 study measured temperatures."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The choice supports the argument with the findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ei-640$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Spiders spin webs to catch insects.
